const PROJECTS = [{"id":1,"name":"Reaction-time game (Sandy's first build)","difficulty":"absolute beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from your laptop to the Uno (5V via the Uno's USB-B port). No batteries, no wall plug.","code_language":"cpp","code":"const int LED    = 9;\nconst int BUTTON = 2;\n\nvoid setup() {\n  pinMode(LED, OUTPUT);\n  pinMode(BUTTON, INPUT_PULLUP);   // button shorts to GND when pressed\n  Serial.begin(9600);\n  randomSeed(analogRead(A0));      // different random sequence each power-up\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  Serial.println(\"Get ready ...\");\n  delay(random(2000, 5000));       // random 2-5 second pause\n\n  digitalWrite(LED, HIGH);         // GO\n  unsigned long start = millis();\n\n  while (digitalRead(BUTTON) == HIGH) {\n    // wait for the button press\n  }\n\n  unsigned long reaction = millis() - start;\n  digitalWrite(LED, LOW);\n\n  Serial.print(\"Your reaction: \");\n  Serial.print(reaction);\n  Serial.println(\" ms\");\n  Serial.println();\n  delay(2000);\n}","description":"**What it is**: A little red light blinks on after a surprise pause. You slap the button as fast as you can, and your reaction time pops up on the laptop screen in milliseconds.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Race your dad, your brother, anyone who walks past, and see who has the fastest thumb in the house. Most grown-ups land around 250 ms, so if you beat that you get to brag about it forever.\n\n**Goal:** Arduino lights an LED after a random 2-5 second pause. Player slaps the button as fast as possible after the LED comes on. Reaction time prints to the Serial Monitor in milliseconds. Aiming for sub-250 ms on a good day.\n\n**First electronics project, age 10, no soldering needed.**\n\n## Parts list (allocated below)\n\n- Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone)\n- Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm\n- 1x red 5mm LED\n- 1x 220R resistor (colour bands: red-red-brown-gold)\n- 1x tactile push-button, 4-pin (3x6x3.1mm)\n- 4x M-M jumper wires\n- USB cable for the Uno - check the clone (USB-B \"square printer\", mini-USB, or USB-C; grab one in advance)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nSee the diagram below for the circuit. When building it on the breadboard:\n\n- The breadboard's two long side rails are usually power and ground. Use one rail as GND.\n- The short rows in the middle are joined under the plastic; same row = electrically the same.\n- Plug the LED so its long leg (+) and short leg (-) end up in different rows.\n- The 220Ω resistor goes between the LED's short-leg row and the GND rail.\n- The push-button has 4 legs but only 2 are \"the switch\". If pressing doesn't register, rotate the button 90° and re-seat.\n- Run one M-M jumper from the Arduino GND pin to the GND rail so the whole rail is grounded.\n\n## Talking points while he wires it up\n\nDrip-feed one at a time, not all at once.\n\n- **LEDs have a long leg (+) and a short leg (-).** Backwards = no light, but no damage.\n- **The resistor stops the LED getting too much current** and burning out. Garden-hose analogy: too much water blows the nozzle.\n- **A breadboard's short rows are joined under the plastic.** The two long rails on the sides are usually power and ground.\n- **The code's `loop()` runs forever**, top to bottom, then starts again. That's why the game keeps coming back round.\n- **Average human reaction time is about 250 ms.** That's the benchmark to beat.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\nGive him the goal, let him hunt for the change in the code.\n\n- Make the random pause longer or shorter.\n- Cheat-detect: if the button is pressed *before* the LED lights, print \"Too early!\" instead of a time.\n- Best-of-five mode: track and average over 5 rounds.\n- Add a second LED that turns on when he beats his previous best time.\n\n## Prep checklist for Andy\n\n- [ ] Install the Arduino IDE from [arduino.cc](https://arduino.cc) - free, Windows.\n- [ ] Plug the Uno in once yourself first. Windows will prompt for a CH340 USB driver - let it install.\n- [ ] Open the IDE: **Tools -> Port** should show a COM port. Set **Tools -> Board -> Arduino Uno**.\n- [ ] Confirm the USB ca","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":4},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x3.1mm (4-pin, black/red)","qty":1}]},{"id":23,"name":"Mood Meter (touch the dot, change your face)","difficulty":"absolute beginner","power_supply":"USB-C cable from laptop to the XIAO ESP32-C3 (5V in, regulated to 3.3V on-board; 3.3V powers the OLED and the TTP223B via the breadboard rails)","code_language":"cpp","code":"<![CDATA[// Mood Meter - touch the dot, change your face\n// Hardware: XIAO ESP32-C3 + SSD1306 0.96\" I2C OLED + TTP223B touch module\n//\n// Libraries to install via Arduino IDE Library Manager:\n//   - Adafruit GFX Library\n//   - Adafruit SSD1306\n//\n// Board: \"XIAO_ESP32C3\" (under ESP32 Arduino)\n// Serial baud: 115200\n\n#include <Wire.h>\n#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>\n#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>\n\n// ---- Pin assignments (match the wiring diagram) ----\n// XIAO ESP32-C3 default I2C pins:\n//   SDA = GPIO 6  (silkscreen \"D4\")\n//   SCL = GPIO 7  (silkscreen \"D5\")\n// TTP223B signal:\n//   TOUCH = GPIO 2 (silkscreen \"D0\")\nconst int PIN_TOUCH = 2;\n\n// ---- Display setup ----\n#define SCREEN_WIDTH 128\n#define SCREEN_HEIGHT 64\n#define OLED_RESET   -1     // share the XIAO reset\n#define OLED_ADDR    0x3C   // most cheap 0.96\" SSD1306 boards\nAdafruit_SSD1306 display(SCREEN_WIDTH, SCREEN_HEIGHT, &Wire, OLED_RESET);\n\n// ---- Mood state ----\nenum Mood { HAPPY = 0, SLEEPY = 1, GRUMPY = 2, SURPRISED = 3 };\nconst char* MOOD_NAMES[4] = { \"happy\", \"sleepy\", \"grumpy\", \"surprised\" };\nMood currentMood = HAPPY;\n\n// ---- Touch debounce ----\nint lastTouchState = LOW;\nunsigned long lastTouchChangeMs = 0;\nconst unsigned long DEBOUNCE_MS = 50;\n\n// ---- Timestamp (seconds since boot - good enough without WiFi) ----\nunsigned long lastChangeSec = 0;\n\n// ---------- Drawing helpers ----------\n\n// Draw the face inside a circle in the upper portion of the screen.\n// Center at (64, 24), radius 20. That leaves the bottom for the log line.\nvoid drawFace(Mood m) {\n  const int cx = 64;\n  const int cy = 24;\n  const int r  = 20;\n\n  // outline of the face\n  display.drawCircle(cx, cy, r, SSD1306_WHITE);\n\n  // eyes - default position\n  int eyeLx = cx - 7;\n  int eyeRx = cx + 7;\n  int eyeY  = cy - 5;\n\n  switch (m) {\n    case HAPPY:\n      // round eyes\n      display.fillCircle(eyeLx, eyeY, 2, SSD1306_WHITE);\n      display.fillCircle(eyeRx, eyeY, 2, SSD1306_WHITE);\n      // smile - arc-ish: a short horizontal line with curled ends\n      display.drawLine(cx - 6, cy + 4, cx + 6, cy + 4, SSD1306_WHITE);\n      display.drawPixel(cx - 7, cy + 3, SSD1306_WHITE);\n      display.drawPixel(cx + 7, cy + 3, SSD1306_WHITE);\n      break;\n\n    case SLEEPY:\n      // closed eyes - horizontal lines\n      display.drawLine(eyeLx - 3, eyeY, eyeLx + 3, eyeY, SSD1306_WHITE);\n      display.drawLine(eyeRx - 3, eyeY, eyeRx + 3, eyeY, SSD1306_WHITE);\n      // tiny \"o\" mouth\n      display.drawCircle(cx, cy + 5, 2, SSD1306_WHITE);\n      // a l","description":"**What it is**: A little screen shows a face - happy, sad, surprised, sleepy. Touch the metal dot with your finger and the face flips to the next mood. No buttons, no switches, just tap.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It feels like magic the first time - you barely brush the pad and the face changes, like it can read your mind. Stick it on your bedroom door and make it match your mood so your brother knows whether to knock or run.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x XIAO ESP32-C3 (the brain - runs the sketch, drives the screen)\n- 1x 0.96\" SSD1306 OLED, I2C version (4 pins: VCC, GND, SCL, SDA)\n- 1x TTP223B capacitive touch module (3 pins: VCC, GND, I/O)\n- 1x breadboard (shared 3.3V and GND rails)\n- 8x F-M jumper wires (XIAO header to breadboard rails and modules)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nEverything in this project runs on 3.3V. The XIAO ESP32-C3 is a 3.3V board, the SSD1306 OLED works happily on 3.3V (it actually prefers it), and the TTP223B is rated for 2-5.5V. Do NOT use the 5V pin on the XIAO for any of this.\n\nPower rails on the breadboard:\n- Red rail: XIAO 3V3 pin -> red (+) rail\n- Black rail: XIAO GND pin -> blue (-) rail\n\nOLED (I2C):\n- VCC -> red rail (3.3V)\n- GND -> blue rail\n- SDA -> XIAO D4 (GPIO 6)\n- SCL -> XIAO D5 (GPIO 7)\n\nTTP223B touch module:\n- VCC -> red rail (3.3V)\n- GND -> blue rail\n- I/O (sometimes labeled SIG or OUT) -> XIAO D0 (GPIO 2)\n\nThe TTP223B's I/O pin sits LOW when nothing is touching the pad and goes HIGH when a finger lands on the metal area on top of the module. No pull-up or pull-down resistor needed - the chip handles that internally.\n\nTwo tiny gotchas worth knowing:\n1. The OLED address is usually 0x3C but a few clones are 0x3D - if the screen stays blank, that's the first thing to swap in the code.\n2. The first time the TTP223B powers on it spends ~0.5s calibrating \"what counts as not-touched\". Don't keep your finger on the pad while you press reset.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- I2C uses just two wires (SDA = data, SCL = clock) and can talk to up to ~120 devices in theory. The OLED listens on address 0x3C; if we added a second I2C gadget (temperature sensor, real-time clock, another screen) it would share the same two wires with a different address.\n- \"Capacitive touch\" means the chip is measuring how much electrical charge the pad can hold. A finger nearby adds a tiny bit of capacitance, the chip notices the change, and flips the output pin. No moving parts, no wear.\n- The face is drawn from primitives: `drawCircle`, `drawLine`, `fillCircle`, plus a couple of stray pixels for the smile curl. It's the same toolbox an old 8-bit game would have used.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Add more moods. Adding a fifth case is one enum entry, one name string, one extra `case` in `drawFace`, and bumping the modulo from 4 to 5.\n- Make the eyes blink every few seconds when idle (timer + extra draw call).\n- Connect to WiFi (SSID `GeeFam`, password `PASSWORD_HERE`) and pull the real time from an NTP server so the log timestamp is a clock time instead of seconds-s","allocations":[{"item_name":"Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":8},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"TTP223B Capacitive Touch Module","qty":1}]},{"id":24,"name":"Theremin Lite","difficulty":"absolute beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from laptop to the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (5V via the micro-USB port, regulated on-board to 3.3V for the chip and broken out as VIN/5V for the ultrasonic sensor)","code_language":"cpp","code":"// Theremin Lite\n// Wave your hand above the HC-SR04 ultrasonic sensor and the red LED\n// blinks faster the closer you get. Like a Geiger counter chasing your palm.\n//\n// Board: ESP32 Dev Module (or NodeMCU-32S)\n// Serial: 115200 baud\n\n// ---- Pin map (must match the wiring diagram) ----\nconst int PIN_TRIG = 5;    // ESP32 output -> sensor Trig\nconst int PIN_ECHO = 18;   // sensor Echo -> voltage divider -> ESP32 input\nconst int PIN_LED  = 19;   // ESP32 output -> 220R -> LED anode\n\n// ---- Tuning knobs ----\nconst float DIST_MIN_CM   = 3.0f;    // hand right above the sensor\nconst float DIST_MAX_CM   = 50.0f;   // arm's length away\nconst int   BLINK_FAST_MS = 30;      // shortest on-time when very close\nconst int   BLINK_SLOW_MS = 600;     // longest on-time when far / no hand\n\n// Echo timeout. Shorter = snappier response when nothing is in front of the\n// sensor (the loop won't sit waiting for a phantom echo). 12000us ~= 2 metres\n// of round-trip, which is plenty for hand-waving range.\nconst unsigned long ECHO_TIMEOUT_US = 12000UL;\n\n// Measure distance in centimetres. Returns -1.0f if no echo came back.\nfloat readDistanceCm() {\n  // Send a clean 10-microsecond HIGH pulse on Trig.\n  digitalWrite(PIN_TRIG, LOW);\n  delayMicroseconds(2);\n  digitalWrite(PIN_TRIG, HIGH);\n  delayMicroseconds(10);\n  digitalWrite(PIN_TRIG, LOW);\n\n  // pulseIn waits for Echo to go HIGH, then times how long it stays HIGH.\n  unsigned long durationUs = pulseIn(PIN_ECHO, HIGH, ECHO_TIMEOUT_US);\n  if (durationUs == 0) {\n    return -1.0f; // timeout - nothing in range\n  }\n\n  // Speed of sound is ~343 m/s = 0.0343 cm/us. Divide by 2 for the round trip.\n  return (durationUs * 0.0343f) / 2.0f;\n}\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n  delay(200);\n  Serial.println();\n  Serial.println(\"Theremin Lite ready. Wave your hand!\");\n\n  pinMode(PIN_TRIG, OUTPUT);\n  pinMode(PIN_ECHO, INPUT);\n  pinMode(PIN_LED,  OUTPUT);\n\n  digitalWrite(PIN_TRIG, LOW);\n  digitalWrite(PIN_LED,  LOW);\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  float cm = readDistanceCm();\n\n  // Decide how long the LED should be on, then off.\n  int blinkMs;\n  if (cm < 0) {\n    // No echo - treat it like \"nothing nearby\", slow heartbeat.\n    blinkMs = BLINK_SLOW_MS;\n    Serial.println(\"distance: (no echo)\");\n  } else {\n    // Clamp to the band we care about.\n    if (cm < DIST_MIN_CM) cm = DIST_MIN_CM;\n    if (cm > DIST_MAX_CM) cm = DIST_MAX_CM;\n\n    // Simple linear interpolation (lerp):\n    // at DIST_MIN_CM -> BLINK_FAST_MS, at DIST_MAX_CM -> BLINK_SLOW_MS,\n  ","description":"**What it is**: Wave your hand over the breadboard. The red LED blinks slowly when your hand is far away, then pulses faster and faster as you move it closer, like a heartbeat speeding up. No touching anything - just air.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You are literally controlling a light with your hand from across the desk, like a wizard. Challenge your dad to guess the distance just by watching the blink speed - then move your hand and prove him wrong.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the brain - reads the sensor, blinks the LED)\n- HC-SR04 ultrasonic distance sensor (the \"eye\" that bounces sound off your hand)\n- 5mm red LED (the visible heartbeat)\n- 220R resistor (current limiter for the LED - keeps it from burning out)\n- 1K resistor + 2.2K resistor (voltage divider on the Echo line - see Wiring notes)\n- Breadboard, 400 tie-points\n- A handful of male-male jumper wires\n- A USB cable from your laptop to the ESP32 (power and code-upload in one)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n**The HC-SR04 runs on 5V, but the ESP32 is a 3.3V chip.** Powering the sensor from the ESP32's VIN pin (which mirrors the USB 5V rail on most NodeMCU-32S boards - some silkscreens label this pin `5V` or `VBUS` instead of `VIN`, and on a few revisions VIN sits behind a Schottky diode so it reads ~4.7V; any of those is fine for the HC-SR04) is the easy option. But the sensor's Echo pin will swing up to 5V, and that's too much for the ESP32 GPIO. The fix is a tiny voltage divider made of two resistors:\n\n- Sensor Echo -> 1K resistor -> ESP32 GPIO 18 (this junction is what the ESP32 reads)\n- From that same junction -> 2.2K resistor -> GND\n\nThat divider pulls the 5V Echo signal down to roughly 3.4V at the ESP32 pin. Trig is an output FROM the ESP32 to the sensor, so 3.3V is usually enough to drive it - no divider needed there. **Caveat:** a small minority of HC-SR04 clones (especially newer ones with stricter input thresholds) won't trigger reliably from a 3.3V Trig signal. The symptom is that distance reads -1 (\"no echo\") even when an object is clearly in front of the sensor. The fix is to drive Trig through a level shifter, or use a 5V-tolerant logic source - but try the simple wiring first.\n\n**LED side:** the long leg of the LED (anode, +) goes through the 220R resistor to GPIO 19. The short leg (cathode, -) goes to a GND rail. The resistor can be on either side of the LED - it's there to limit the current, the LED doesn't care which side.\n\n**Pins used:**\n\n- GPIO 5  -> HC-SR04 Trig\n- GPIO 18 -> HC-SR04 Echo (through the voltage divider)\n- GPIO 19 -> LED anode (through the 220R resistor)\n- 5V (VIN) -> HC-SR04 VCC\n- GND -> HC-SR04 GND, LED cathode, and the bottom of the voltage divider\n\n## Talking points\n\n- **How ultrasonic distance works:** the sensor chirps a 40kHz \"click\" your ears can't hear, then listens for the echo. The ESP32 times how long the click takes to come back. Sound travels about 343 metres per second in air, so distance in cm = duration in microseconds * 0.0343 / 2 (the \"divide by 2\"","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 1K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 2.2K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"HC-SR04 Ultrasonic Distance Sensor","qty":1}]},{"id":25,"name":"Don't-Touch-The-Wire steady-hand maze","difficulty":"absolute beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from laptop to the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S micro-USB port (5V in; ESP32 runs on 3.3V on-board, TM1637 display fed from the 5V VIN pin)","code_language":"cpp","code":"// Don't-Touch-The-Wire steady-hand maze\n// Board: ESP32 NodeMCU-32S\n// 7-segment display: TM1637 4-digit module (install \"TM1637\" library by Avishay Orpaz)\n//\n// How it works:\n//   - The bent maze wire is connected to PIN_WIRE with an internal pull-up.\n//   - The wand loop is connected to GND.\n//   - Touching the wand to the maze pulls PIN_WIRE LOW -> that's a strike.\n//   - We only count a strike on a fresh HIGH->LOW transition (edge detection),\n//     so holding the wand against the wire does NOT keep racking up strikes.\n//   - Green LED is on while the player is clear; red LED is on while touching.\n//   - The 4-digit display shows the strike count. Press the button to reset to 0.\n\n#include <Arduino.h>\n#include <TM1637Display.h>\n\n// ---- Pin map (matches the wiring diagram) ----\nconst int PIN_WIRE   = 23;  // maze wire contact (INPUT_PULLUP)\nconst int PIN_LED_R  = 18;  // red LED (touching indicator)\nconst int PIN_LED_G  = 19;  // green LED (all-clear indicator)\nconst int PIN_BTN    = 5;   // reset button (INPUT_PULLUP). GPIO 5 is a strapping pin;\n                            // idle-HIGH (released) matches the boot-time requirement, so this is safe.\nconst int PIN_CLK    = 22;  // TM1637 CLK\nconst int PIN_DIO    = 21;  // TM1637 DIO\n\n// ---- Tuning ----\nconst int MAX_STRIKES = 9999;  // 4-digit display ceiling\n\n// ---- State ----\nTM1637Display display(PIN_CLK, PIN_DIO);\nint strikes = 0;\nint lastWireState = HIGH;  // remembers what the wire pin was last loop, so we can spot HIGH->LOW edges\n\nvoid showScore() {\n  display.showNumberDec(strikes, false);  // false = don't pad with leading zeros\n}\n\nvoid resetGame() {\n  strikes = 0;\n  showScore();\n  digitalWrite(PIN_LED_R, LOW);\n  digitalWrite(PIN_LED_G, HIGH);\n  Serial.println(\"Reset - new round\");\n}\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n  delay(200);\n  Serial.println(\"Steady-hand maze: starting up\");\n\n  pinMode(PIN_WIRE,  INPUT_PULLUP);\n  pinMode(PIN_BTN,   INPUT_PULLUP);\n  pinMode(PIN_LED_R, OUTPUT);\n  pinMode(PIN_LED_G, OUTPUT);\n\n  display.setBrightness(5);  // 0-7\n  resetGame();\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  // ---- 1. Read the maze contact ----\n  // LOW means the wand is touching the wire.\n  int wireState = digitalRead(PIN_WIRE);\n\n  // Edge detection: count a strike ONLY on the moment of contact (HIGH -> LOW).\n  // Holding the wand against the wire stays LOW, so no extra strikes pile up.\n  if (lastWireState == HIGH && wireState == LOW) {\n    delay(20);  // tiny pause then re-read, so contact bounce doesn't double-c","description":"**What it is**: Guide a little metal loop along a bendy wire track without letting them touch. Every time you bump the wire, the red light flashes, a number on the display ticks up, and your strike count climbs. Hold steady and the green light stays on instead.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It's the classic carnival \"buzz wire\" game, but you built it yourself, and the screen keeps score so you can finally settle who in the family has the steadiest hand. Bend the track into a tougher shape every round to mess with whoever goes next.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- **ESP32 NodeMCU-32S** - the brain. Watches the wand and updates the score.\n- **4-digit 7-segment Display Module (TM1637, 4-pin: VCC/GND/CLK/DIO)** - shows the strike count. Powered from 5V (see wiring notes).\n- **LED 5mm Red + 220R resistor** - lights while the wand is touching the wire.\n- **LED 5mm Green + 220R resistor** - lights while the wand is in the clear.\n- **Tactile Push-button Switch (3x6x4.3mm, white pin)** - reset-to-zero button.\n- **Stiff bare wire (~30 cm)** bent into a wavy track. **Wand loop (~3 cm)** of the same wire on a short insulated handle. (Both physical-craft parts, not on the board.)\n- **Heat-shrink tubing / electrical tape / pen barrel** - the insulated handle for the wand so the player's hand doesn't complete the circuit.\n- **Breadboard + jumper wires** to hold everything together.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe whole game is one big switch: the bent maze wire goes to GPIO 23 with the ESP32's internal **pull-up** enabled, so the pin idles HIGH (3.3 V). The wand loop goes straight to **GND**. The instant the loop touches the maze wire, GPIO 23 is dragged to LOW - that's our \"you touched it!\" signal.\n\n- Maze wire -> long jumper to GPIO 23 (INPUT_PULLUP).\n- Wand loop -> long jumper to a GND rail on the breadboard.\n- Red LED: GPIO 18 -> 220R -> LED anode -> LED cathode -> GND.\n- Green LED: GPIO 19 -> 220R -> LED anode -> LED cathode -> GND.\n- Reset button: one leg to GPIO 5 (INPUT_PULLUP), the leg diagonally opposite to GND. The other two pins are the internal short; ignore them.\n- 7-segment module (TM1637): **VCC -> VIN (5 V from the USB rail)**, GND -> GND, CLK -> GPIO 22, DIO -> GPIO 21.\n\n**Why VIN, not 3V3, for the TM1637:** these modules are spec'd for 5 V. At 3.3 V many clones run dim or flicker. The NodeMCU-32S exposes the USB 5 V rail on its VIN pin, which is the right home for the display. The CLK/DIO lines on the module idle at 5 V through pull-ups - the ESP32 is rated as 5 V-tolerant on these GPIOs in practice, and using a TM1637 this way is the standard beginner wiring.\n\n**Heads-up on GPIO 5:** it's an ESP32 \"strapping pin\" - the chip looks at it during boot. With INPUT_PULLUP and the button wired to GND, it idles HIGH (released), which matches what the chip wants at boot. Safe. Just don't later wire something to GPIO 5 that holds it LOW at power-on, or the board will refuse to start and you'll wonder why.\n\nThe ESP32 itself is powered by the USB cable from the laptop; the display takes ","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"4-digit 7-segment Display Module","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":2},{"item_name":"Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x4.3mm (white pin)","qty":1}]},{"id":26,"name":"Temperature Mood Dial","difficulty":"absolute beginner","power_supply":"USB-C cable from laptop (or any 5V USB charger) to the XIAO ESP32-C3; the XIAO's 5V pin feeds the SG90 servo, and the XIAO's 3V3 pin feeds the DHT11.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// Temperature Mood Dial\n// Board: Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3\n// Reads the DHT11 every ~2 seconds and sweeps an SG90 servo\n// across a hand-drawn gauge based on the temperature.\n//\n// Libraries needed (install via Arduino IDE Library Manager):\n//   - \"DHT sensor library\" by Adafruit\n//   - \"Adafruit Unified Sensor\" (dependency of the above)\n//   - \"ESP32Servo\" by Kevin Harrington / John K. Bennett\n//\n// Wiring (matches the Mermaid diagram):\n//   DHT11 VCC  -> XIAO 3V3   (NOT 5V - keeps the DATA line at 3.3V so\n//                             it is safe for the XIAO's 3.3V GPIO)\n//   DHT11 GND  -> XIAO GND\n//   DHT11 DATA -> XIAO D2 (GPIO 4)\n//   SG90 red   -> XIAO 5V    (servo genuinely needs 5V)\n//   SG90 brown -> XIAO GND\n//   SG90 orange-> XIAO D3 (GPIO 5)\n//   470uF cap across the 5V and GND rails near the servo.\n\n#include <DHT.h>\n#include <ESP32Servo.h>\n\n// ----- Pins -----\nconst int DHT_PIN   = 4;   // D2 on the XIAO silkscreen\nconst int SERVO_PIN = 5;   // D3 on the XIAO silkscreen\n\n// ----- Sensor config -----\n#define DHT_TYPE DHT11\nDHT dht(DHT_PIN, DHT_TYPE);\n\n// ----- Servo -----\nServo dialServo;\n\n// ----- Temperature mapping -----\n// Tune these to match your room. The needle sweeps the full\n// 0..180 degrees between MIN_TEMP_C and MAX_TEMP_C.\n// With 15..30 the five mood zones are evenly 3 C wide:\n//   15-18 BRRR | 18-21 CHILLY | 21-24 NICE | 24-27 TOASTY | 27-30 LAVA\nconst float MIN_TEMP_C = 15.0;  // \"BRRR\"   end of the gauge\nconst float MAX_TEMP_C = 30.0;  // \"LAVA\"   end of the gauge\n\n// ----- Smoothing -----\n// We move the servo toward the target one step at a time so the\n// needle glides instead of snapping. Tweak STEP_DEG for speed.\nconst int STEP_DEG       = 2;   // degrees per step\nconst int STEP_DELAY_MS  = 25;  // pause between steps\nint currentAngle = 90;          // where the needle is right now\nint targetAngle  = 90;          // where we want it to be\n\nunsigned long lastReadMs = 0;\nconst unsigned long READ_INTERVAL_MS = 2000;  // DHT11 hates being read faster\n\nint tempCtoAngle(float tempC) {\n  // Clamp the temperature into the visible range.\n  if (tempC < MIN_TEMP_C) tempC = MIN_TEMP_C;\n  if (tempC > MAX_TEMP_C) tempC = MAX_TEMP_C;\n  // Map Celsius -> 0..180 degrees. Multiplying by 100 keeps the\n  // map() math integer-friendly.\n  long t100   = (long)(tempC * 100.0);\n  long min100 = (long)(MIN_TEMP_C * 100.0);\n  long max100 = (long)(MAX_TEMP_C * 100.0);\n  return (int)map(t100, min100, max100, 0, 180);\n}\n\nconst char* moodFor(float tempC) {\n","description":"**What it is**: A little cardboard dial with a pointer that swings to show how warm or cold the room is. When the air is freezing it points at BRRR, when it's hot it swings all the way to LAVA, and it keeps re-checking every couple of seconds on its own.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Breathe on the sensor and watch the needle creep toward TOASTY in real time, or stick it by a window and catch the moment the sun comes out. You built a thing that feels the world and reacts - no buttons, no app, just your dial quietly paying attention.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3 (the brain - reads the sensor, drives the servo).\n- 1x DHT11 Temperature & Humidity Sensor module (3-pin breakout: VCC / DATA / GND).\n- 1x SG90 Servo (the moving needle).\n- 1x Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (gives the XIAO and DHT11 a home and shared power rails).\n- 1x 470uF electrolytic capacitor (sits across 5V and GND near the servo to absorb its current spikes).\n- 1x Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc pack (servo lead and DHT11 module both need female-ended jumpers to reach the breadboard).\n- 1x Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc pack (a few short wires for the 5V, 3V3 and GND rails on the breadboard).\n- Craft supplies (not in inventory, grab from a drawer): a square of cardboard, a marker, a thin cardboard strip for the needle, sticky tape.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe XIAO has two power-out pins: `5V` (passthrough from the USB-C jack) and `3V3` (the on-board regulator). It matters which one we use for each part:\n\n- The SG90 servo really wants 5V. Its motor and coil are spec'd for 4.8-6V; on 3.3V it would be weak and twitchy.\n- The DHT11 module is happy anywhere from 3.0V to 5.5V, so we run it from `3V3`. That keeps its DATA line at 3.3V, which matches the XIAO's GPIO. If we powered the DHT11 from 5V, its DATA pin would idle at 5V too and we'd be pushing 5V into a 3.3V-only GPIO - that's the level-shifting problem the talking points mention. Running the sensor at 3V3 sidesteps it entirely with zero extra parts.\n\n1. Plug the XIAO into the breadboard so each row of pins lands on its own column.\n2. Run M-M jumpers to build three rails:\n   - XIAO `5V` -> red (+) rail (for the servo only).\n   - XIAO `3V3` -> orange tie-strip a few rows over (for the DHT11 only). Most breadboards only have two power rails, so just pick an unused row and call it the 3V3 rail - the DHT11 is the only thing that touches it.\n   - XIAO `GND` -> blue (-) rail (shared by everything).\n3. DHT11 module pins:\n   - `VCC` (or `+`) -> the **3V3** rail (NOT the red 5V rail).\n   - `GND` (or `-`) -> blue rail (GND).\n   - `DATA` (or `OUT`) -> XIAO `D2` (GPIO 4).\n4. SG90 servo wires:\n   - Red -> red rail (5V).\n   - Brown (or black) -> blue rail (GND).\n   - Orange (or yellow) -> XIAO `D3` (GPIO 5).\n5. The 470uF capacitor. Look for the stripe with a \"-\" on the side - that's the negative leg. Plug the negative leg into the blue (GND) rail and the other leg into the red (5V) rail, as close to where the servo's red and brown wires plug in as possible. This little reservoir","allocations":[{"item_name":"Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 470uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"SG90 Servo (9g micro)","qty":1},{"item_name":"DHT11 Temperature & Humidity Sensor","qty":1}]},{"id":27,"name":"Shake-to-roll electronic D6 dice","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from laptop to the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S micro-USB port (5V in, regulated to 3.3V on board for all modules)","code_language":"cpp","code":"// Shake-to-roll D6 dice (ESP32 NodeMCU-32S)\n// Hardware: ESP32 + SW-420 vibration switch + TM1637 4-digit display + 1 green LED\n//\n// Library required: \"TM1637\" by Avishay Orpaz\n//   Arduino IDE -> Tools -> Manage Libraries -> search \"TM1637\" -> install\n//\n// What it does:\n//   - Sits idle with the display blanked\n//   - When you shake the breadboard, the vibration switch fires\n//   - The green LED turns on and the display flickers through random faces\n//   - The flicker slows down, then stops on a random number 1..6\n//   - The green LED turns off; the result stays on screen until next shake\n\n#include <Arduino.h>\n#include <TM1637Display.h>\n\n// ----- Pin assignments (these MUST match the wiring diagram) -----\n// GPIO27 is a safe general-purpose pin (NOT a boot strapping pin), so the\n// SW-420's signal can't accidentally affect how the ESP32 boots.\nconst int PIN_SHAKE   = 27;  // SW-420 DO  -> GPIO27\nconst int PIN_LED     = 26;  // Green LED  -> GPIO26 (through 220R)\nconst int PIN_TM_CLK  = 18;  // TM1637 CLK -> GPIO18\nconst int PIN_TM_DIO  = 19;  // TM1637 DIO -> GPIO19\n\n// ----- Display setup -----\nTM1637Display display(PIN_TM_CLK, PIN_TM_DIO);\n\n// ----- Roll / cooldown state -----\n// After a roll we ignore the sensor briefly so the leftover vibration\n// from your hand hitting the table doesn't immediately re-roll.\nunsigned long rollCooldownUntil = 0;\nconst unsigned long ROLL_COOLDOWN_MS = 1500;\n\nvoid showNumber(int n) {\n  // showNumberDec prints a number on the display. The \"false\" means\n  // \"don't pad with leading zeros\". The \"1\" prints one digit only.\n  // The final \"3\" puts that digit in the rightmost position (index 3).\n  display.showNumberDec(n, false, 1, 3);\n}\n\nvoid clearDisplay() {\n  uint8_t blank[4] = { 0, 0, 0, 0 };\n  display.setSegments(blank);\n}\n\nint rollDice() {\n  // esp_random() is the ESP32's hardware random number generator.\n  // We take the result modulo 6 (so 0..5) and add 1 to get 1..6.\n  return (int)(esp_random() % 6) + 1;\n}\n\nvoid doRollAnimation() {\n  digitalWrite(PIN_LED, HIGH);\n\n  // Shorter delays at the start, longer ones at the end:\n  // that's what makes it feel like a real die losing energy.\n  int delays[11] = { 60, 60, 60, 60, 80, 100, 130, 170, 220, 300, 400 };\n  int steps = 11;\n\n  for (int i = 0; i < steps; i++) {\n    int face = rollDice();\n    showNumber(face);\n    delay(delays[i]);\n  }\n\n  // Final result\n  int result = rollDice();\n  showNumber(result);\n\n  // Three quick LED blinks to \"announce\" the result\n  for (int i = 0","description":"**What it is**: Shake the breadboard like a pair of dice in your hand. A little green light flashes, numbers blur across the screen, and then it stops on your roll - 1 through 6, just like a real die.\n\n**Why it's fun**: No more lost dice under the couch on board game night. Plus the numbers slow down as they land, so every shake feels like a real tumble - your brain totally falls for it.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the brain - reads the shake sensor and drives the display)\n- SW-420 vibration switch module (digital \"did something shake?\" sensor)\n- 4-digit 7-segment display module (TM1637 driver - only needs two data pins)\n- 1x 5mm green LED + 1x 220 ohm resistor (lights up while the dice is \"rolling\")\n- Breadboard + DuPont jumper wires\n- Micro-USB cable from your laptop to power the ESP32\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe ESP32 sits across the breadboard's centre gutter. Both side rails get jumpered to the ESP32: one rail is 3.3V, the other is GND.\n\n**Power rails**\n- ESP32 `3V3` pin -> red rail on the breadboard\n- ESP32 `GND` pin -> blue (ground) rail on the breadboard\n\n**SW-420 vibration module** (3 pins: VCC, GND, DO)\n- VCC -> 3.3V red rail\n- GND -> blue ground rail\n- DO  -> ESP32 GPIO27\n\nGPIO27 is a regular general-purpose pin on the ESP32. We deliberately avoided GPIO12, GPIO14 and a couple of others called \"strapping pins\" - those control how the chip boots, and a sensor pulling them around at power-on can confuse the boot process.\n\nThe SW-420 is essentially a digital switch. It sits HIGH normally and flickers LOW when the little spring inside gets jostled. The board has a sensitivity trimpot you can twist with a small screwdriver if it's too touchy or not touchy enough.\n\n**TM1637 4-digit display** (4 pins: CLK, DIO, VCC, GND)\n- VCC -> 3.3V red rail\n- GND -> blue ground rail\n- CLK -> ESP32 GPIO18\n- DIO -> ESP32 GPIO19\n\nThese modules are happy on 3.3V even though most listings say 5V. Two data pins is all it takes - the TM1637 chip on the back of the module handles the actual segment timing.\n\n**Green LED** (the \"rolling\" indicator)\n- Long leg (anode) -> 220 ohm resistor -> ESP32 GPIO26\n- Short leg (cathode) -> blue ground rail\n\nThe resistor goes BEFORE the LED, on the GPIO side. The order doesn't matter electrically, but it's a good habit.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- **Why the SW-420 is \"noisy\":** one shake makes the spring inside rattle on and off many times. The code handles this with a classic two-step debounce: read once, wait 50 ms for the bouncing to settle, read again. If both reads agree, it was a real shake. The exact same trick works for noisy push buttons.\n- **Why the dice slows down:** the animation array `delays[]` has shorter delays at the start and longer ones at the end. That alone makes it feel like a real tumbling die. No physics needed.\n- **Where the randomness comes from:** `esp_random()` uses the ESP32's hardware random number generator. On an Arduino Uno you'd have to \"seed\" the random function with noise from a floating pin - on ","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":10},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1},{"item_name":"4-digit 7-segment Display Module","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"SW-420 Vibration Switch Module","qty":1}]},{"id":28,"name":"Motion-Triggered Jump Scare Box","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from laptop to the ESP32 NodeMCU (5V via the USB port; the 5V rail feeds the PIR and the servo, the 3.3V GPIO drives signals). A 470 uF capacitor sits across the servo's 5V/GND to soak up inrush spikes.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// Motion-Triggered Jump Scare Box\n// ESP32 NodeMCU-32S + HC-SR501 PIR + SG90 servo + red LED.\n//\n// One-time setup: install the \"ESP32Servo\" library via\n//   Arduino IDE -> Tools -> Manage Libraries... -> search \"ESP32Servo\"\n//\n// Behaviour (top to bottom, no state machine - just a linear loop):\n//   1. Boot: ~60-second PIR warm-up. LED blinks fast. Motion ignored.\n//   2. Armed: LED solid ON. Watching the PIR.\n//   3. Motion: snap the lid open, wait 2s, ease it shut.\n//   4. Cool-down: ~10 seconds before we look at the PIR again.\n\n#include <ESP32Servo.h>\n\nconst int PIN_PIR   = 13;   // HC-SR501 OUT  -> GPIO 13 (digital input)\nconst int PIN_SERVO = 25;   // SG90 signal   -> GPIO 25 (quiet at boot)\nconst int PIN_LED   = 27;   // Red LED (via 220R) -> GPIO 27\n\nconst int SERVO_CLOSED_DEG = 0;     // lid down / spring loaded\nconst int SERVO_OPEN_DEG   = 120;   // lid flipped open\n\nconst unsigned long WARMUP_MS   = 60UL * 1000UL;  // PIR settle time\nconst unsigned long OPEN_HOLD_MS = 2000;          // how long the lid stays open\nconst unsigned long COOLDOWN_MS = 10UL * 1000UL;  // quiet time after a trigger\n\nServo lidServo;\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n  delay(200);\n  Serial.println();\n  Serial.println(\"Jump Scare Box booting...\");\n\n  pinMode(PIN_PIR, INPUT);\n  pinMode(PIN_LED, OUTPUT);\n  digitalWrite(PIN_LED, LOW);\n\n  lidServo.setPeriodHertz(50);              // SG90 wants 50 Hz\n  lidServo.attach(PIN_SERVO, 500, 2400);    // typical SG90 pulse window\n  lidServo.write(SERVO_CLOSED_DEG);\n\n  // Warm up the PIR. Blink the LED fast so you can see we are waiting.\n  Serial.println(\"Warming up PIR (60s)...\");\n  unsigned long warmStart = millis();\n  while (millis() - warmStart < WARMUP_MS) {\n    digitalWrite(PIN_LED, HIGH);\n    delay(120);\n    digitalWrite(PIN_LED, LOW);\n    delay(120);\n  }\n\n  digitalWrite(PIN_LED, HIGH);              // solid LED = armed\n  Serial.println(\"PIR ready. ARMED.\");\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  // Are we seeing motion?\n  if (digitalRead(PIN_PIR) == HIGH) {\n    Serial.println(\"MOTION! Flipping lid.\");\n    digitalWrite(PIN_LED, LOW);\n\n    // Snap open fast - hobby servos move as fast as their gears allow.\n    lidServo.write(SERVO_OPEN_DEG);\n    delay(OPEN_HOLD_MS);\n\n    // Ease shut a little more gently in case the spider is on a string.\n    for (int a = SERVO_OPEN_DEG; a >= SERVO_CLOSED_DEG; a -= 5) {\n      lidServo.write(a);\n      delay(20);\n    }\n    lidServo.write(SERVO_CLOSED_DEG);\n\n    // Cool-down: slow blink so you can see we are not","description":"**What it is**: Set the little cardboard box on a shelf and walk away. When someone wanders past, the lid flips open and a paper spider pops out at them.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Hide it outside your sister's bedroom door and wait for the scream. The box sits perfectly still until the exact second someone walks by - it feels less like electronics and more like the box is alive.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S - the brain. Reads the PIR, blinks the LED, twitches the servo.\n- 1x HC-SR501 PIR Motion Sensor - the \"eye\". Detects warm bodies moving past.\n- 1x SG90 Servo (9g micro) - the muscle. Tape it under the lid; horn flips the lid open.\n- 1x 5mm Red LED - \"armed\" indicator so you know the trap is live.\n- 1x 220R resistor - current-limiter for the LED so it does not burn out.\n- 1x 470 uF electrolytic capacitor - soaks up the servo's start-up current spike so the ESP32 does not brown out and reset when the lid snaps. Long leg (+) to the 5V rail, short leg (-) to GND. Polarity matters - backwards can pop the cap.\n- 1x Breadboard - holds the LED, resistor, capacitor, and power rails.\n- Jumper wires (F-M for the PIR/servo headers, M-M for breadboard hops).\n- USB cable from laptop to power the ESP32.\n- (Not in the inventory) a small cardboard box and a paper spider on a spring or rubber band.\n\n## One-time software setup\n\nBefore this sketch will compile, install the **ESP32Servo** library:\n\n1. Arduino IDE -> Tools -> Manage Libraries...\n2. Search for `ESP32Servo` (by Kevin Harrington / John K. Bennett).\n3. Click Install. Done - this is a one-time step.\n\nIf you skip this you will get `ESP32Servo.h: No such file or directory` when you try to upload.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n- The HC-SR501 is a 5V module. Wire its VCC to the ESP32 `VIN` pin (the raw rail coming back out of the USB port), and GND to any ESP32 GND. Its OUT pin only swings up to ~3.3V, so it is safe to feed straight into ESP32 GPIO 13 - no level shifter, no resistor divider.\n- The SG90 also runs from 5V. Red wire to `VIN`, brown wire to GND, orange (signal) to **GPIO 25**. The ESP32 GPIO is 3.3V, but the SG90 signal pin reads a 3.3V pulse just fine.\n- Place the 470 uF capacitor on the breadboard right next to where the servo's red and brown wires plug in. Long leg goes into the 5V rail row, short leg goes into the GND rail row. This is the single most important non-obvious part of the build - skipping it is what makes \"lid snaps, ESP32 reboots, sketch starts over\" happen.\n- The LED has a long leg (anode, +) and a short leg (cathode, -). Long leg goes through the 220R resistor to GPIO 27. Short leg goes to a GND rail.\n- The PIR has two orange potentiometers on the back. Start with **sensitivity** turned roughly to the middle and **time delay** turned all the way counter-clockwise (shortest hold, ~3 seconds). There is also a jumper on the side with positions **H** and **L**:\n  - **L** (single trigger / non-retriggerable) - the PIR fires once per motion event, then forces a quiet period. This is what w","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":6},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":4},{"item_name":"Capacitor 470uF 16V (electrolytic)","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"SG90 Servo (9g micro)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"HC-SR501 PIR Motion Sensor","qty":1}]},{"id":29,"name":"Hidden-Magnet Treasure Hunter","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from laptop to the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (5V via the micro-USB port; ESP32 regulates down to 3.3V on board)","code_language":"cpp","code":"// Hidden-Magnet Treasure Hunter\n// ESP32 NodeMCU-32S + KY-024 (linear Hall) sensor + TM1637 4-digit display + LED\n//\n// Sweep the sensor near a hidden neodymium magnet. The LED blinks faster the\n// closer you get, and the 4-digit display shows the live field \"strength\".\n//\n// Library required (install via Arduino IDE Library Manager):\n//   \"TM1637Display\" by Avishay Orpaz\n\n#include <Arduino.h>\n#include <TM1637Display.h>\n\n// ---- Pin map (must match wiring diagram) ----\nconst int HALL_PIN  = 34;   // ADC1_CH6, input-only on the ESP32 - perfect for analog sensors\nconst int LED_PIN   = 2;    // onboard blue LED + external red LED via 220R.\n                            // NOTE: GPIO 2 is a strapping pin. It must be LOW (or floating)\n                            // at boot. setup() writes LOW *before* pinMode OUTPUT to keep\n                            // the strapping condition safe.\nconst int TM_CLK    = 22;\nconst int TM_DIO    = 21;\n\n// ---- Tuning knobs ----\n// How many samples to average when establishing the \"no magnet\" baseline at startup.\nconst int   BASELINE_SAMPLES   = 200;\n// Anything below this distance-from-baseline counts as \"no magnet detected\".\nconst int   NOISE_FLOOR        = 30;\n// Field strength that maps to the FASTEST blink (magnet basically touching sensor).\n// You may need to lower this if your magnet is weak, or raise it if it's strong.\nconst int   STRENGTH_AT_MAX    = 1500;\n// Blink interval bounds, in milliseconds. Far away = slow blink, close = fast blink.\nconst unsigned long BLINK_SLOW_MS = 800;\nconst unsigned long BLINK_FAST_MS = 40;\n\nTM1637Display display(TM_CLK, TM_DIO);\n\nint           baseline           = 2048;   // refined at startup\nunsigned long lastBlinkToggle    = 0;\nbool          ledOn              = false;\nunsigned long lastDisplayUpdate  = 0;\n\nvoid setup() {\n    Serial.begin(115200);\n    delay(200);\n    Serial.println();\n    Serial.println(\"Hidden-Magnet Treasure Hunter starting up.\");\n\n    // IMPORTANT: write LOW before pinMode OUTPUT so GPIO 2 (a strapping pin)\n    // stays low through the moment it switches to output. Do not reorder.\n    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);\n    pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);\n\n    // ESP32 ADC: 12-bit (0-4095), 11dB attenuation gives ~0-3.3V range.\n    analogReadResolution(12);\n    analogSetPinAttenuation(HALL_PIN, ADC_11db);\n\n    display.setBrightness(5);   // 0 = dim, 7 = bright\n    display.clear();\n\n    // ---- Establish baseline ----\n    // Hold the sensor away from any magnet for the first second ","description":"**What it is**: Hide a magnet anywhere - under a couch cushion, inside a book, behind a wall. Wave the sensor near it and a red LED lights up while a little screen counts higher and higher the closer you get.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It is a real treasure detector that sees through stuff your eyes cannot. Make your dad hide the magnet, time how fast you find it, then swap and try to stump him.\n\n## Parts list\n\nFrom the kit:\n\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the brain - reads the Hall sensor, blinks the LED, drives the display)\n- 1x KY-024 Linear Magnetic Hall Sensor (4-pin: A0 analog out, D0 digital out, +, -). This is the *linear* Hall sensor - its analog output voltage swings up or down depending on the magnet's pole and how close it is. We only use the A0 (analog) pin.\n- 1x 4-digit 7-segment Display Module (TM1637-based, 4 pins: VCC, GND, DIO, CLK)\n- 1x LED 5mm Red (any colour works, red is most visible against couch cushions)\n- 1x Resistor 220R 1/8W 1% (the LED's bodyguard - keeps it from burning out)\n- 1x Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)\n- 1x Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc bundle (for breadboard-to-breadboard hops)\n- 1x Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc bundle (for ESP32 header pin to breadboard, and to the KY-024 / TM1637 modules)\n\nYou supply:\n\n- 1x small neodymium magnet - the \"treasure\". A fridge magnet works in a pinch; a small neodymium is much more dramatic.\n- 1x USB cable (laptop to ESP32 NodeMCU-32S, micro-USB).\n\n## Libraries to install\n\nOpen Arduino IDE > Tools > Manage Libraries... and install:\n\n- **TM1637Display** by Avishay Orpaz - drives the 4-digit display.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe ESP32 NodeMCU-32S has plenty of pins. We're using:\n\n- GPIO 34 -> KY-024 A0 (analog signal). GPIO 34 is an input-only ADC pin, perfect for reading an analog voltage. We ignore the D0 (digital) pin entirely.\n- 3.3V -> KY-024 + (the KY-024 is happy on 3.3V)\n- GND -> KY-024 -\n- GPIO 2 -> 220 ohm resistor -> LED anode (long leg), then LED cathode to GND.\n- GPIO 22 -> TM1637 CLK\n- GPIO 21 -> TM1637 DIO\n- 3.3V -> TM1637 VCC (these modules run fine on 3.3V)\n- GND -> TM1637 GND\n\n**About GPIO 2:** GPIO 2 is also the onboard blue LED, so you'll see the onboard one mirror the external red one - free bonus. GPIO 2 is also a \"strapping pin\" the ESP32 reads at power-on to decide its boot mode. The pin must be LOW (or floating) at boot for normal program execution. The sketch deliberately calls `digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW)` *before* `pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT)` in `setup()` so that the moment the pin becomes an output it's already low, which keeps the strapping condition correct. Don't reorder those two lines.\n\nWith no magnet near the KY-024, the analog reading sits around the midpoint (~1900-2100 on a 0-4095 scale). As a magnet approaches, the value swings up OR down depending on which pole of the magnet faces the sensor's flat side. The sketch tracks the absolute distance from the resting baseline, so either pole works.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- Magnetic fields are invisible but the LED makes them visibl","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"4-digit 7-segment Display Module","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"KY-024 Linear Magnetic Hall Sensor","qty":1}]},{"id":30,"name":"Plant SOS","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB-C cable from laptop to the XIAO ESP32-C3 (5V); the XIAO's onboard regulator powers the OLED from its 3.3V rail, and the soil sensor's VCC is gated by a GPIO (D2) so it only sees 3.3V during the ~10 ms read window.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// Plant SOS - XIAO ESP32-C3 + soil moisture sensor + 0.96\" OLED + red LED.\n//\n// Reads the analog soil moisture pin and draws one of three faces on the\n// OLED depending on how wet the soil is. When the soil is \"thirsty\" the\n// red LED blinks once per second.\n//\n// Reliability tweaks vs. a naive version:\n//   - The soil probe's VCC is driven by a GPIO (PIN_SOIL_PWR) and only\n//     energised for ~10 ms around each read. Always-on 3.3 V across a\n//     resistive probe electrolyses the prongs and they corrode in weeks.\n//   - The OLED address is auto-detected between 0x3C and 0x3D, with the\n//     result printed over Serial so you can pin it down.\n//   - The polarity of the moisture reading (wet=low vs wet=high) depends\n//     on the specific sensor board. Flip SENSOR_WET_IS_LOW if needed.\n//\n// Libraries (Arduino IDE Library Manager):\n//   - Adafruit GFX Library\n//   - Adafruit SSD1306\n//\n// Board: \"XIAO_ESP32C3\" under ESP32 Arduino.\n\n#include <Wire.h>\n#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>\n#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>\n\n// ----- Pins -----\n// XIAO ESP32-C3 silkscreen D-labels map to these GPIOs:\n//   D0=GPIO2 (ADC), D1=GPIO3, D2=GPIO4, D4=GPIO6 (SDA), D5=GPIO7 (SCL)\nstatic const int PIN_SOIL      = D0;   // analog moisture input,  GPIO2\nstatic const int PIN_LED       = D1;   // red LED + 220R,         GPIO3\nstatic const int PIN_SOIL_PWR  = D2;   // soil sensor VCC gate,   GPIO4\n\n// ----- OLED -----\n#define OLED_W   128\n#define OLED_H   64\nstatic const uint8_t OLED_ADDR_PRIMARY = 0x3C;\nstatic const uint8_t OLED_ADDR_FALLBACK = 0x3D;\nAdafruit_SSD1306 display(OLED_W, OLED_H, &Wire, -1);\n\n// ----- Sensor polarity -----\n// Most 4-pin LM393 resistive boards: wet -> LOW number, dry -> HIGH number.\n// Some capacitive replacements and a few odd resistive clones invert this.\n// Watch the Serial Monitor: dunk the probe in water, then hold it in air.\n// If wet gives a HIGHER number than dry, set this to false.\nstatic const bool SENSOR_WET_IS_LOW = true;\n\n// ----- Moisture thresholds (raw 12-bit ADC, 0..4095) -----\n// These are the \"wet-is-low\" defaults. They are auto-flipped below if\n// SENSOR_WET_IS_LOW is false, so you do not need to touch the conditionals.\n// Recalibrate after watching Serial in air vs. in a glass of water.\nstatic const int WET_BELOW = 1800;   // wetter than this -> HAPPY\nstatic const int DRY_ABOVE = 2800;   // drier than this  -> CRYING\n\n// LED blink state for the \"thirsty\" case\nunsigned long lastBlinkMs = 0;\nbool ledOn = false;\nstatic const unsigned long BLI","description":"**What it is**: Stick the two metal prongs into your plant's dirt. A tiny screen shows a happy face when the soil is wet, a sleepy face when it's getting dry, and a crying face when the plant is thirsty - and a red LED blinks at you until you water it.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Your plant basically gets a face and tells you how it feels. Spit on the prongs, dunk them in a glass of water, hold them in the air - the little face changes right in front of you like the plant is reacting to what you do.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3 (the brain - reads the sensor, drives the OLED and LED)\n- 1x Soil Moisture Sensor (the two-prong probe + small comparator board; we only use the analog \"AO\" pin)\n- 1x 0.96\" OLED Display (I2C) (the little blue/white screen; talks over 2 wires)\n- 1x LED 5mm Red (the \"thirsty\" warning blinker) - the legs plug directly into the breadboard, no jumper needed\n- 1x Resistor 220R (current limit for the red LED - never wire an LED straight to a GPIO) - leads plug directly into the breadboard, no jumper needed\n- 1x Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (no soldering - everything plugs in)\n- Jumper wires M-M (for the OLED and sensor connections - not for the LED itself)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nPower rails first:\n\n- XIAO **3V3** pin -> breadboard red (+) rail\n- XIAO **GND** pin -> breadboard blue (-) rail\n\nOLED (I2C, 4 wires - it shares the same 2 data lines no matter how many I2C things you add later):\n\n- OLED **VCC** -> red rail (3.3V)\n- OLED **GND** -> blue rail\n- OLED **SDA** -> XIAO **D4** (GPIO 6, the default SDA on XIAO ESP32-C3)\n- OLED **SCL** -> XIAO **D5** (GPIO 7, the default SCL on XIAO ESP32-C3)\n\nMost 0.96\" SSD1306 modules answer at I2C address **0x3C**, but a meaningful minority ship at **0x3D** (sometimes selectable by a resistor jumper on the back of the board). The sketch tries 0x3C first, then 0x3D, and prints which one worked over Serial - so you don't have to guess.\n\nSoil sensor (analog - we read a voltage that changes with moisture):\n\n- Sensor **VCC** -> XIAO **D2** (GPIO 4) - **not** the 3.3V rail. The sketch turns this pin on for ~10 ms around each reading and off in between. Continuously powering a resistive probe electrolyses the metal prongs and they corrode in weeks; gating the supply slows that down dramatically.\n- Sensor **GND** -> blue rail\n- Sensor **AO** -> XIAO **D0** (GPIO 2, an ADC-capable pin)\n- The sensor's **DO** pin is left disconnected - that one is just an on/off threshold and we want the smooth analog value instead.\n- Most common 4-pin LM393 boards happily accept 3.3V on VCC, which is what D2 supplies. A rare variant has an on-board 3-pin regulator and insists on 5V - if your board's silkscreen says \"5V only\", wire VCC to the XIAO's **5V** pin instead and accept the corrosion trade-off (or use a transistor to gate the 5V line).\n\nRed LED (active-high - \"HIGH = on\"):\n\n- XIAO **D1** (GPIO 3) -> one leg of the 220R resistor\n- Other leg of the 220R -> long leg (anode, +) of the red LED\n- Short leg (cathode, -) of the L","allocations":[{"item_name":"Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Soil Moisture Sensor","qty":1}]},{"id":31,"name":"Earthquake Cam","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from laptop to the ESP32 (5V in). The ESP32's VIN pin re-exposes that 5V to power the MAX7219 matrix; the ESP32's onboard 3V3 regulator powers the SW-420 sensor and drives the GPIO logic.","code_language":"cpp","code":"<![CDATA[// Earthquake Cam\n// ESP32 NodeMCU + SW-420 vibration switch + MAX7219 8x8 LED matrix + warning LED.\n// On any shake, count the bumps over a 10-second window and show a Richter-style\n// bar graph + a verdict label (\"MOUSE FART\" ... \"GODZILLA\").\n//\n// Library needed (install once in Arduino IDE Library Manager):\n//   \"MD_MAX72XX\"  by majicDesigns\n//\n// Arduino IDE board:  ESP32 Arduino > ESP32 Dev Module\n//\n// POWER NOTE: the matrix VCC goes to the ESP32's 5V / VIN pin (USB-powered),\n// NOT to 3V3. The MAX7219 datasheet wants 4.0 to 5.5 V. The ESP32 GPIO logic\n// is 3.3 V but the MAX7219 reads anything above ~2 V as HIGH, so direct\n// connections from GPIO to DIN/CS/CLK work fine.\n\n#include <SPI.h>\n#include <MD_MAX72xx.h>\n\n// ---- Pin map (matches the wiring diagram) ----\nconst int PIN_SW420    = 27;   // SW-420 digital OUT\nconst int PIN_LED_WARN = 26;   // Yellow warning LED through 220R\nconst int PIN_MATRIX_CS = 5;   // MAX7219 CS / LOAD\n// MOSI = GPIO 23, SCK = GPIO 18 (hardware SPI defaults on ESP32)\n\n// ---- Matrix config ----\n// If the display looks garbled, scrambled, mirrored, or upside-down,\n// try the OTHER values below in order. This single line is the #1\n// thing that varies between cheap 8x8 modules.\n//   FC16_HW       <- most common on \"FC-16\" red modules (try first)\n//   GENERIC_HW    <- many no-name eBay/Aliexpress modules\n//   PAROLA_HW     <- original Parola-branded boards\n//   ICSTATION_HW  <- IcStation-branded boards\n#define HARDWARE_TYPE MD_MAX72XX::FC16_HW\n#define MAX_DEVICES   1\nMD_MAX72XX matrix = MD_MAX72XX(HARDWARE_TYPE, PIN_MATRIX_CS, MAX_DEVICES);\n\n// ---- Event counting ----\nconst unsigned long WINDOW_MS   = 10000UL;   // 10 second measuring window\nconst unsigned long DEBOUNCE_MS = 25UL;      // ignore re-triggers inside this gap\n\nvolatile unsigned long lastPulseMs = 0;\nvolatile unsigned int  pulseCount  = 0;\n\nvoid IRAM_ATTR onShake() {\n  unsigned long now = millis();\n  if (now - lastPulseMs >= DEBOUNCE_MS) {\n    lastPulseMs = now;\n    pulseCount++;\n  }\n}\n\n// Map a count to a 1..8 \"Richter\" level for the bar graph.\nint countToLevel(unsigned int count) {\n  if (count <= 1)   return 1;\n  if (count <= 3)   return 2;\n  if (count <= 6)   return 3;\n  if (count <= 10)  return 4;\n  if (count <= 18)  return 5;\n  if (count <= 30)  return 6;\n  if (count <= 50)  return 7;\n  return 8;\n}\n\nconst char* levelToVerdict(int level) {\n  switch (level) {\n    case 1: return \"MOUSE FART\";\n    case 2: return \"TIPTOE\";\n    case 3: return \"BUMP\";\n","description":"**What it is**: Thump the table next to it. A yellow lamp lights up while it counts your shakes, then a tiny 8x8 grid of red lights draws a bar graph showing how big your \"earthquake\" was, with a label that scrolls past like \"TINY TREMOR\" or \"GODZILLA\".\n\n**Why it's fun**: It is basically a Richter scale for whacking the desk. Take turns with your dad or your brother and see who can pound out the biggest quake without knocking the breadboard off the table.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU (the brain)\n- 1x SW-420 vibration switch module (the \"earthquake\" sensor)\n- 1x 8x8 LED matrix with MAX7219 driver board (the display)\n- 1x yellow LED (the warning lamp that lights while a quake is being measured)\n- 1x 220 ohm resistor (current-limit for the yellow LED)\n- 1x breadboard, ~12 DuPont jumpers\n- 1x USB cable (laptop to ESP32; this is also where the 5V for the matrix comes from)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nTwo power rails this time, not one. Bring the ESP32's `3V3` pin to one red breadboard strip, and the ESP32's `VIN` pin (which is the USB 5V passing through the board) to a *separate* red strip. Tie `GND` to the blue strip - both rails share ground.\n\n- **8x8 LED matrix (MAX7219):** `VCC` to the **5V / VIN** rail, NOT 3V3. The MAX7219 datasheet wants 4.0 to 5.5 V; running it on 3.3 V is folk wisdom and can brown out the ESP32's onboard regulator when the panel lights up. `GND` to GND. `DIN` to `GPIO 23` (MOSI), `CS` to `GPIO 5`, `CLK` to `GPIO 18` (SCK). The MAX7219 reads anything above ~2 V as HIGH, so the ESP32's 3.3 V logic signals drive it fine directly.\n- **SW-420 module:** `VCC` to **3V3**, `GND` to GND, `DO` (digital out) to ESP32 `GPIO 27`. The little blue trim-pot on the module sets the trigger threshold - if it triggers constantly even when still, turn that pot clockwise until the on-board \"trigger\" LED only blinks when you actually tap the board.\n- **Yellow LED:** long leg (anode) through the 220 ohm resistor to `GPIO 26`. Short leg (cathode) to GND. The resistor stops the LED from burning out.\n- Keep the SW-420 mounted firmly on the breadboard - if it dangles on jumper wires, the wires themselves will jiggle and trigger false quakes.\n\n## Safety notes\n\nNothing in this build runs above 5 V, so the mains-safety warnings that apply to the 2-channel relay project explicitly do NOT apply here. The only real hazards are: (a) backwards LED polarity will simply not light, no damage; (b) shorting 5V to GND on the breadboard will trip the laptop's USB port protection but not hurt the ESP32. Unplug USB before re-wiring anything.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- The SW-420 isn't really a \"sensor\" in the analog sense - it's a tiny spring around a pin that briefly closes a circuit when shaken. So we don't measure shake *strength*, we count shake *pulses* per unit time. Lots of pulses in 10 seconds = bigger quake.\n- We use a hardware *interrupt* on the sensor pin. Interrupts let the chip drop whatever it's doing the instant a pulse arrives and bump a counter. That way we neve","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":12},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Yellow","qty":1},{"item_name":"8x8 LED Dot-matrix Display (red)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"SW-420 Vibration Switch Module","qty":1}]},{"id":32,"name":"Simon Says - 4-color memory game","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from laptop to the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (5V in via the board's USB port, 3.3V on the GPIO header)","code_language":"cpp","code":"/*\n * Simon Says - 4-color memory game\n * Target board: ESP32 NodeMCU-32S\n *\n * How it plays:\n *   - The board flashes a growing sequence of colors (red, yellow, green, blue).\n *   - You press the matching buttons in the same order.\n *   - Get it right and the sequence grows by one and speeds up a little.\n *   - Get it wrong and all four LEDs blink three times to tell you the game is over.\n *   - Then it restarts with a new random sequence.\n */\n\n#include <Arduino.h>\n\n// ---- Pin assignments (match the wiring diagram) ----\n// LEDs: any output-capable GPIO is fine. We avoid 0, 2, 12, 15 (boot pins)\n// and 2 specifically because it is the on-board user LED on this board.\nconst int LED_PINS[4] = {25, 26, 27, 14};       // Red, Yellow, Green, Blue\n\n// Buttons: ALL four pins MUST have internal pull-ups so INPUT_PULLUP works.\n// GPIO 34, 35, 36, 39 are input-only and have NO internal pull-up - do not\n// use them here without an external 10k pull-up to 3V3.\n// 32 and 33 are general-purpose; 13 and 19 are also safe and pull-up capable.\nconst int BTN_PINS[4] = {32, 33, 13, 19};       // Red, Yellow, Green, Blue\n\nconst char* COLOR_NAMES[4] = {\"RED\", \"YELLOW\", \"GREEN\", \"BLUE\"};\n\n// ---- Game tuning ----\nconst int  MAX_SEQUENCE   = 64;     // longest sequence we will ever remember\nconst int  START_FLASH_MS = 600;    // how long each LED stays on at round 1\nconst int  MIN_FLASH_MS   = 150;    // we never go faster than this\nconst int  GAP_MS         = 200;    // pause between LEDs in the playback\nconst unsigned long PLAYER_TIMEOUT_MS = 5000; // 5 s to press the next button\nconst int  DEBOUNCE_MS    = 25;     // tactile button bounce window\n\n// ---- Game state ----\nint sequence[MAX_SEQUENCE];\nint sequenceLength = 0;\nint flashDuration  = START_FLASH_MS;\n\n// ---------- helpers ----------\n\nvoid allLedsOff() {\n  for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) digitalWrite(LED_PINS[i], LOW);\n}\n\nvoid flashColor(int colorIndex, int durationMs) {\n  digitalWrite(LED_PINS[colorIndex], HIGH);\n  delay(durationMs);\n  digitalWrite(LED_PINS[colorIndex], LOW);\n  delay(GAP_MS);\n}\n\n// Read one button with a tiny debounce. Returns true if pressed.\n// INPUT_PULLUP means \"not pressed = HIGH, pressed = LOW\".\nbool isPressed(int colorIndex) {\n  if (digitalRead(BTN_PINS[colorIndex]) == LOW) {\n    delay(DEBOUNCE_MS);\n    if (digitalRead(BTN_PINS[colorIndex]) == LOW) {\n      return true;\n    }\n  }\n  return false;\n}\n\n// Block until ANY button is pressed, or timeout expires.\n// Returns the color index pressed, or -1 on","description":"**What it is**: Four colored LEDs flash a pattern at you - red, yellow, green, blue. You press the buttons underneath in the exact same order. Get it right and the pattern grows by one. Get it wrong and the game is over.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It is the classic Simon Says toy, but YOU built it. Challenge your dad to see who can remember the longest sequence, and watch the flashes get faster and faster until your brain melts.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the brain - runs the state machine and the randomizer)\n- 1x Red, 1x Yellow, 1x Green, 1x Blue 5mm LED (the four \"Simon\" pads)\n- 4x 220 ohm resistors (one in series with each LED so the LED does not burn out)\n- 4x tactile push-buttons (one under each LED)\n- 1x breadboard, ~18 DuPont jumpers\n- USB cable from laptop to the ESP32 (also how power gets in)\n\nNo external pull-up resistors are needed because every button GPIO chosen here (32, 33, 13, 19) supports the ESP32's internal pull-up.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nLEDs (one per color, repeat the same pattern four times):\n\n- ESP32 GPIO -> 220 ohm resistor -> LED anode (long leg)\n- LED cathode (short leg, with the flat side) -> breadboard GND rail\n- GPIOs used: Red = 25, Yellow = 26, Green = 27, Blue = 14\n\nButtons (also one per color):\n\n- One leg of the button -> an ESP32 GPIO\n- The opposite leg -> breadboard GND rail\n- No external pull-up resistor needed - the sketch turns on the ESP32's internal pull-up, so the GPIO sits HIGH at rest and reads LOW when pressed\n- GPIOs used: Red = 32, Yellow = 33, Green = 13, Blue = 19\n\nPower rails:\n\n- ESP32 `GND` -> breadboard \"-\" rail. All four LED cathodes and all four button ground legs come here.\n- The `3V3` pin is not needed for this project - every GPIO already provides its own pull-up internally.\n\nPin notes (worth knowing):\n\n- GPIO 34, 35, 36, 39 are INPUT-ONLY on the ESP32 and have NO internal pull-up. They look tempting because there are four of them, but a button wired only between an input-only pin and GND will float when released and give random readings. If you ever want to use one anyway, add an external 10 k ohm pull-up resistor from the pin to 3V3.\n- Avoid GPIO 0, 12, 15 for buttons - they are boot-strapping pins and can stop the board from starting if held LOW or HIGH at power-on.\n- GPIO 2 is the on-board blue user LED on this board. Do not reuse it for a button - the LED and the button would fight each other.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- The \"memory\" of the game is just an array of numbers. Each new round appends a fresh random value (0-3) and replays the whole array from the start.\n- Debouncing: when you press a tactile button the metal contacts bounce for a few milliseconds, so the GPIO sees several rapid LOW/HIGH/LOW transitions. The sketch reads the pin, waits a tiny moment (`DEBOUNCE_MS`), and reads again to confirm.\n- `INPUT_PULLUP` flips your intuition: pressed = LOW, not pressed = HIGH. That is because the internal resistor pulls the pin to 3.3 V by default, and the button shorts it to GND when you pr","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":18},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Blue","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Yellow","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":4},{"item_name":"Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x3.1mm (4-pin, black/red)","qty":4}]},{"id":33,"name":"Stopwatch + Lap Memory on Serial Monitor","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from laptop to the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (5V in via USB, the onboard regulator makes 3.3V for the chip; no external parts so nothing else needs feeding).","code_language":"cpp","code":"/*\n * Stopwatch + Lap Memory on Serial Monitor\n *\n * Hardware: ONE ESP32 NodeMCU-32S, USB cable. Nothing else.\n *\n *   - BOOT button (GPIO 0, active-LOW) is the only user input.\n *   - Built-in blue LED (GPIO 2) blinks once per second while running,\n *     and is off when stopped. Visual \"is it running?\" indicator.\n *   - Serial Monitor at 115200 baud is the display.\n *\n * Controls:\n *   - Short tap of BOOT (< 600 ms):  toggle start / stop.\n *   - Long press of BOOT (>= 600 ms, while running):  record a lap.\n *   - Long press of BOOT (>= 600 ms, while stopped):  reset to zero.\n *\n * The Serial Monitor prints the running MM:SS once a second, and prints\n * each recorded lap in MM:SS.hh (hundredths of a second) along with the\n * split since the previous lap. The fastest lap so far is tagged \"BEST\".\n *\n * Notes for the reader:\n *   - We deliberately do NOT use delay(). Every timing decision is made\n *     by comparing millis() values, so the loop stays responsive.\n *   - The BOOT button is wired with a hardware pull-up on the board, so\n *     INPUT_PULLUP is requested for safety but is not strictly required.\n *   - At power-on the BOOT button may already be held by the user (for\n *     flash mode). We sample its stable state once in setup() and treat\n *     that as the baseline, so we do not fire a phantom \"release\" event\n *     when the user lets go just after boot.\n */\n\n#include <Arduino.h>\n\n// ---------- pin map (matches the wiring diagram) ----------\nconst uint8_t PIN_BTN = 0;   // BOOT button, active-LOW\nconst uint8_t PIN_LED = 2;   // built-in blue LED (try 5 or 22 if your clone differs)\n\n// ---------- timing constants ----------\nconst uint16_t DEBOUNCE_MS    = 25;\nconst uint16_t LONG_PRESS_MS  = 600;\nconst uint16_t PRINT_PERIOD_MS = 1000;  // how often we reprint MM:SS while running\nconst uint16_t LED_BLINK_MS    = 500;   // LED toggles every 500 ms -> 1 Hz blink\n\n// ---------- stopwatch state ----------\nbool      running       = false;\nuint32_t  startMillis   = 0;   // millis() value at the moment we last started\nuint32_t  accumulatedMs = 0;   // total time banked from previous start/stop cycles\n\n// ---------- lap memory ----------\nconst uint8_t MAX_LAPS = 20;\nuint32_t laps[MAX_LAPS];       // each entry = total elapsed ms at the moment Lap was pressed\nuint8_t  lapCount     = 0;\nuint8_t  bestLapIndex = 0xFF;  // 0xFF means \"no best yet\"\nuint32_t bestLapMs    = 0xFFFFFFFF;\n\n// ---------- button debounce + short/long detection ----------\n// Event codes r","description":"**What it is**: Plug the ESP32 into your laptop and open the Serial Monitor. Tap the BOOT button to start a stopwatch, tap again to stop, hold it down to save a lap time, and a tiny blue light blinks once a second while the clock is running.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Race your dad down the hallway, time how long you can hold your breath, or see who can stack ten cups the fastest, and every lap gets saved so you can compare runs. One little button does three different jobs depending on how you press it, which feels a bit like a secret code your fingers know.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the entire project - nothing else needed)\n- 1x USB-C cable from your laptop\n\nThat is the whole shopping list. This version of the project was deliberately re-scoped so it can be built with **only the hardware already in Andy's kit**. An earlier draft used a TM1637 4-digit display, an SSD1306 OLED, two tactile push-buttons, a breadboard, and jumpers - none of which are in the kit yet. When those parts arrive, see \"Things to change once it works\" for the upgrade path.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThere is no wiring. Plug the USB-C cable from your laptop into the ESP32. That is it. The two pieces of \"hardware UI\" we use are both already on the board:\n\n- **BOOT button** (next to the USB-C jack). It is wired to GPIO 0 with a built-in pull-up. Pressed = LOW, released = HIGH. We use it as our one user button.\n- **Built-in blue LED** on GPIO 2 (the small LED next to the WiFi shield, not the red power LED). It blinks once a second while the stopwatch is running and is off when stopped, so you can see the state at a glance without looking at the screen.\n\nThe \"display\" is the Arduino IDE Serial Monitor at 115200 baud. Open it with `Tools > Serial Monitor` (or Ctrl+Shift+M) after upload.\n\n**Do not press the EN button while timing** - EN is a hardware reset, it reboots the chip and you lose the run. Only BOOT is safe to press.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- **Why `millis()` instead of `delay()`.** `delay(1000)` would freeze the chip for a whole second, during which it cannot read the button or update the LED. `millis()` just asks \"how many milliseconds since boot?\" without stopping anything, so the same `loop()` can poll the button, blink the LED, and print the time all at once.\n- **One button, three actions, via a small state machine.** A short tap (< 600 ms) toggles start/stop. A long press (>= 600 ms while held) records a lap if running, or resets everything if stopped. The function `readButton()` measures how long the button was held and reports a `SHORT_PRESS` or `LONG_PRESS` event. The `loop()` then decides what that event means based on whether the stopwatch is currently running. That separation - \"low-level: was it short or long?\" vs. \"high-level: what does that mean right now?\" - is the core idea of a state machine, and it shows up in almost every embedded project later.\n- **Active-LOW buttons.** When BOOT is *pressed*, GPIO 0 reads `LOW` (0V), not `HIGH`. That is because the pin is p","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1}]},{"id":34,"name":"Family Doorbell Web Page","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from a laptop or USB wall charger into the ESP32's USB-C port (5V, the board's onboard regulator drops it to 3.3V for the chip)","code_language":"cpp","code":"#include <WiFi.h>\n#include <WebServer.h>\n\n// ---- WiFi credentials ----\nconst char* WIFI_SSID = \"GeeFam\";\nconst char* WIFI_PASS = \"PASSWORD_HERE\";\n\n// ---- Pins ----\n// Button between GPIO 4 and GND. We enable the internal pull-up, so the\n// pin reads HIGH when the button is open and LOW when it is pressed.\nconst int BUTTON_PIN = 4;\n// Built-in blue LED on the NodeMCU-32S. Lights briefly each time the\n// doorbell fires so we can see the press without watching the browser.\nconst int LED_PIN = 2;\n\n// ---- State ----\nWebServer server(80);\n\n// \"ring count\" goes up by one every time the button is pressed.\n// The web page polls /status and notices when this number has changed,\n// then plays its \"Ding!\" animation. Using a counter (not just a flag)\n// means we never miss a press if two come close together.\nvolatile unsigned long ringCount = 0;\nunsigned long lastRingMillis = 0;\n\n// Debounce: ignore another press within 400 ms of the previous one,\n// otherwise the mechanical button's contact bounce registers as several\n// rings for one push.\nconst unsigned long DEBOUNCE_MS = 400;\nint lastButtonReading = HIGH;\n\n// ---- Web page ----\n// Served at \"/\". Plain HTML + a tiny bit of JavaScript that polls the\n// ESP32 once a second for the current ring count. When the count goes\n// up, the page shows the \"Ding!\" message and the time it happened.\nconst char INDEX_HTML[] PROGMEM = R\"HTML(\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width,initial-scale=1\">\n  <title>Family Doorbell</title>\n  <style>\n    body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; text-align: center;\n           background: #0f172a; color: #e2e8f0; margin: 0; padding: 2rem; }\n    h1 { font-size: 2rem; margin-top: 3rem; }\n    #status { font-size: 3rem; margin-top: 2rem; transition: color .3s; }\n    #stamp  { font-size: 1.25rem; color: #94a3b8; margin-top: 1rem; }\n    .ring   { color: #f59e0b; animation: pulse 1s ease-out 3; }\n    @keyframes pulse {\n      0%   { transform: scale(1);   }\n      50%  { transform: scale(1.15);}\n      100% { transform: scale(1);   }\n    }\n  </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <h1>Family Doorbell</h1>\n  <div id=\"status\">All quiet.</div>\n  <div id=\"stamp\"></div>\n  <script>\n    let lastCount = 0;\n    let firstLoad = true;\n    async function poll() {\n      try {\n        const r = await fetch('/status');\n        const j = await r.json();\n        if (firstLoad) {\n          lastCount = j.count;\n          firstLoad = false;\n        } else ","description":"**What it is**: Press the button on the breadboard. Every phone in the house that has the web page open goes \"Ding!\" and the counter jumps up by one.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It is a real doorbell you built yourself, and Dad's phone buzzes the second you push the button from the other room. Sneak up, mash it five times, and watch every screen in the house freak out at once.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the brain - hosts the web page and watches the button)\n- 1x push button (the doorbell itself)\n- 1x breadboard (holds the button without soldering)\n- 4x DuPont jumper wires (one to GPIO 4, one to GND, two spare)\n- 1x USB cable (powers the ESP32 from a laptop or USB wall charger)\n\nNo resistor is needed for the button: we turn on the ESP32's internal pull-up resistor in code (`INPUT_PULLUP`). The LED that lights up on each press is the blue one already built into the NodeMCU board, so it has its own resistor on the PCB.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe button has 4 legs but they are wired in pairs internally. Sit the button across the breadboard's centre gap. Use diagonally opposite legs - that way you can't accidentally pick two legs that are already shorted together.\n\n- One leg of the button -> ESP32 `GPIO 4`\n- Diagonally opposite leg of the button -> ESP32 `GND`\n\nThat's all the wiring. When the button is open, GPIO 4 reads HIGH (pulled up internally to 3.3V). When pressed, GPIO 4 is shorted to GND and reads LOW. The code watches for that transition.\n\nPower comes from the USB cable on the ESP32 - no separate power supply, no battery, nothing to plug into mains.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- The button is just a switch. No \"smartness\" in it. The ESP32 is what turns \"switch closed\" into \"tell every phone in the house\".\n- The web page doesn't get pushed to; it asks the ESP32 every second \"has the count gone up?\". That's called polling. It's the simplest approach that works on every phone browser without extra libraries.\n- We count rings instead of just storing \"is ringing\". A counter means two phones loading the page at slightly different times still both notice the same press.\n- Debouncing: a physical button's metal contacts bounce when they touch, so one press can look like 3-5 presses electrically. The 400 ms timer in the code ignores those echoes.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Make the page beep or vibrate the phone (a tiny bit of Web Audio API or `navigator.vibrate`).\n- Add a mDNS name (`ESP32.local`) so you don't have to memorise the IP.\n- Push instead of poll: switch to Server-Sent Events (`/events` endpoint) so the page updates instantly with no 1-second lag.\n- Log the last 10 rings with timestamps so you can see who rang while you were out.\n- Move from the breadboard button to a real doorbell button by the front door (still 2 wires).\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nThis is the smallest possible \"real\" IoT loop: a thing in the physical world (a button) makes a thing on a screen change (a web page), with nothing in between except WiFi. Andy gets to feel ","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":4},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x3.1mm (4-pin, black/red)","qty":1}]},{"id":35,"name":"Garage Light Web Switch (with Relay)","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB-A to USB-C cable into the NodeMCU-32S (5V, also feeds the relay coil via the JDVcc-bridged jumper), plus a separate 12V DC barrel-jack supply for the LED strip on the switched side of the relay.","code_language":"cpp","code":"<![CDATA[// Garage Light Web Switch\n// ESP32 NodeMCU-32S + 2-ch Songle relay board (low-level trigger, active-LOW)\n// Hosts a tiny web page with a giant ON/OFF button that switches relay channel 1.\n\n#include <WiFi.h>\n#include <WebServer.h>\n\n// ---- WiFi ----\nconst char* WIFI_SSID     = \"GeeFam\";\nconst char* WIFI_PASSWORD = \"PASSWORD_HERE\";\n\n// ---- Pins ----\n// IN1 on the relay board -> GPIO 26 on the ESP32.\n// The Songle board is ACTIVE-LOW: LOW energizes the coil (light ON), HIGH releases it.\nconst int RELAY_IN1 = 26;\n\n// ---- State ----\nbool lightOn = false;\n\nWebServer server(80);\n\n// Apply the current state to the relay pin (handles the active-LOW inversion).\nvoid applyRelay() {\n  digitalWrite(RELAY_IN1, lightOn ? LOW : HIGH);\n}\n\n// Build the HTML page. One big button that flips state on click.\nString renderPage() {\n  String stateLabel = lightOn ? \"ON\"  : \"OFF\";\n  String action     = lightOn ? \"off\" : \"on\";\n  String btnColor   = lightOn ? \"#ef4444\" : \"#10b981\"; // red when on (press to turn off), green when off\n\n  String html;\n  html.reserve(1200);\n  html += \"<!doctype html><html><head><meta charset='utf-8'>\";\n  html += \"<meta name='viewport' content='width=device-width,initial-scale=1'>\";\n  html += \"<title>Garage Light</title>\";\n  html += \"<style>\";\n  html += \"body{font-family:system-ui,sans-serif;background:#0f172a;color:#f8fafc;\";\n  html += \"display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;justify-content:center;\";\n  html += \"min-height:100vh;margin:0;}\";\n  html += \"h1{font-size:2rem;margin-bottom:0.25rem;}\";\n  html += \"p{opacity:0.7;margin-top:0;}\";\n  html += \"a.btn{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;\";\n  html += \"width:70vw;max-width:320px;height:70vw;max-height:320px;\";\n  html += \"border-radius:50%;background:\" + btnColor + \";color:#fff;\";\n  html += \"font-size:3rem;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;\";\n  html += \"box-shadow:0 10px 30px rgba(0,0,0,0.4);margin-top:1.5rem;}\";\n  html += \"a.btn:active{transform:scale(0.97);}\";\n  html += \"</style></head><body>\";\n  html += \"<h1>Garage Light</h1>\";\n  html += \"<p>Currently: <strong>\" + stateLabel + \"</strong></p>\";\n  html += \"<a class='btn' href='/\" + action + \"'>\" + stateLabel + \"</a>\";\n  html += \"</body></html>\";\n  return html;\n}\n\nvoid handleRoot() {\n  server.send(200, \"text/html\", renderPage());\n}\n\nvoid handleOn() {\n  lightOn = true;\n  applyRelay();\n  server.sendHeader(\"Location\", \"/\");\n  server.send(303);\n}\n\nvoid handleOff() {\n  lightOn = false;\n  applyRelay();\n  server.send","description":"**What it is**: Open a web page on your phone and tap a big button. CLICK - a light across the room turns on. Tap it again, CLICK, and the light turns off.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Your phone is now a magic wand for a real light, and you can hear the little click every time you tap. Show your friends - they will think you wired up the whole house.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the brains and web server)\n- 2-ch Songle relay board (only channel 1 is used here)\n- 12V LED strip, short piece, or any 12V low-voltage lamp\n- 12V DC power supply for the LED strip (separate from the ESP32 USB power)\n- Breadboard (holds the ESP32 only)\n- DuPont jumper wires (M-F from ESP32 header to relay header, M-M for the 12V load side)\n- USB-A to USB-C cable (the NodeMCU-32S has a USB-C connector)\n\nHeads-up: only the ESP32 and the relay board are confirmed on hand from the kit list in CLAUDE.md. The 12V strip, 12V supply, breadboard, jumpers, and USB-C cable are not listed there. Confirm these are actually in the box before starting, or we will be stuck halfway through wiring.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nTwo power worlds meet in this project. Keep them straight.\n\nESP32 side (5V from USB):\n\n- ESP32 `5V` pin -> relay header `VCC`\n- ESP32 `GND` -> relay header `GND`\n- ESP32 `GPIO 26` -> relay header `IN1`\n\nThe breadboard is just a perch for the ESP32 and a convenient place to start the three jumpers that run to the relay header. The relay board already has its own logic pin header, so nothing else is wired on the breadboard rails.\n\nThe relay's JDVcc jumper stays in its default position (bridged to Vcc), so the relay coil is powered from the ESP32's 5V USB rail. This is the simplest wiring and fine for a small DC load.\n\n12V side (the load being switched):\n\n- 12V supply `+` -> relay channel 1 `COM` screw terminal\n- Relay channel 1 `NO` screw terminal -> LED strip `+`\n- LED strip `-` -> 12V supply `-`\n\nThe 12V supply ground does NOT need to touch the ESP32 ground. That is the whole point of the relay: the high-side wire is broken and remade by a mechanical contact, with no electrical connection back to the ESP32.\n\nActive-LOW gotcha: the Songle board energizes the coil when IN1 is pulled LOW. The code drives the pin HIGH before calling `pinMode(..., OUTPUT)` so the relay does not click ON during boot.\n\n3.3V trigger caveat (per CLAUDE.md, `Relay_2CH_Songle/` section): most Songle clones will trigger fine off a 3.3V ESP32 GPIO because the optocoupler LED only needs a small forward current. A few stubborn ones really do want a 5V swing - if relay 1 clicks unreliably, only sometimes, or only after the board warms up, that is the first thing to suspect. Workaround is a tiny NPN transistor or a level-shifter feeding IN1; ask before reaching for those, it is rarely needed.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- The relay is a remote-controlled light switch. The ESP32 cannot handle 12V directly, so it tells a tiny electromagnet (the relay coil) to physically push two metal contacts together.\n- Hear the click? ","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"2-channel Songle relay module","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1}]},{"id":36,"name":"WiFi Signal Strength Meter on OLED","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB-C cable from a power bank (user-supplied) to the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S USB-C port (5V). The board powers the OLED off its 3.3V rail.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// WiFi Signal Strength Meter on OLED\n// Board:  ESP32 NodeMCU-32S\n// Screen: SSD1306 128x64 OLED, I2C, address 0x3C\n// Wiring: SDA -> GPIO 21, SCL -> GPIO 22, VCC -> 3V3, GND -> GND\n//\n// Install these libraries via Arduino IDE Library Manager once:\n//   - \"Adafruit SSD1306\"  by Adafruit\n//   - \"Adafruit GFX Library\" by Adafruit\n//\n// Before uploading: replace PASSWORD_HERE below with the real GeeFam\n// WiFi password. Credentials live inside this sketch, not in CLAUDE.md.\n//\n// What it does: connects to WiFi, then every 250 ms reads the current\n// RSSI (signal strength) and draws a bar graph + numeric value + a\n// plain-English quality label. Walk around the house holding the\n// ESP32 + power bank to hunt for weak spots.\n\n#include <WiFi.h>\n#include <Wire.h>\n#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>\n#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>\n\n// ---- WiFi credentials ----\nconst char* WIFI_SSID     = \"GeeFam\";\nconst char* WIFI_PASSWORD = \"PASSWORD_HERE\";\n\n// ---- I2C pins (ESP32 defaults) ----\nconst int PIN_SDA = 21;\nconst int PIN_SCL = 22;\n\n// ---- OLED config ----\nconst int SCREEN_WIDTH  = 128;\nconst int SCREEN_HEIGHT = 64;\nconst int OLED_RESET    = -1;     // share the ESP32 reset\nconst int OLED_ADDR     = 0x3C;   // standard for these modules\n\nAdafruit_SSD1306 display(SCREEN_WIDTH, SCREEN_HEIGHT, &Wire, OLED_RESET);\n\n// RSSI range we map onto the bar. -30 dBm = \"on top of the router\",\n// -90 dBm = \"barely audible\".\nconst int RSSI_STRONG = -30;\nconst int RSSI_WEAK   = -90;\n\nvoid connectWiFi() {\n  display.clearDisplay();\n  display.setCursor(0, 0);\n  display.setTextSize(1);\n  display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);\n  display.println(\"Connecting to:\");\n  display.println(WIFI_SSID);\n  display.display();\n\n  WiFi.mode(WIFI_STA);\n  WiFi.begin(WIFI_SSID, WIFI_PASSWORD);\n\n  unsigned long start = millis();\n  while (WiFi.status() != WL_CONNECTED && millis() - start < 20000) {\n    delay(250);\n    display.print(\".\");\n    display.display();\n  }\n\n  display.clearDisplay();\n  display.setCursor(0, 0);\n  if (WiFi.status() == WL_CONNECTED) {\n    display.println(\"Connected!\");\n    display.print(\"IP: \");\n    display.println(WiFi.localIP());\n  } else {\n    display.println(\"WiFi failed.\");\n    display.println(\"Will retry...\");\n  }\n  display.display();\n  delay(1500);\n}\n\nconst char* qualityLabel(int rssi) {\n  if (rssi >= -55) return \"Excellent\";\n  if (rssi >= -67) return \"Good\";\n  if (rssi >= -75) return \"Okay\";\n  if (rssi >= -85) return \"Weak\";\n  return \"Dead zone\";\n}\n\nvoid drawMeter(int rssi) {\n  display.clea","description":"**What it is**: Plug it in and a tiny screen lights up with a bar that grows and shrinks as you walk around the house. The fatter the bar, the stronger the WiFi where you're standing.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You become the WiFi detective. Hunt down the dead zones, find the magic spot in the back bedroom where the signal finally comes back, and prove to your dad once and for all which room has the worst internet.\n\n## Parts list\n\nFrom Andy's inventory:\n\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the \"brain\" - reads WiFi signal strength and drives the screen)\n- 1x 0.96\" OLED Display (I2C) - SSD1306, 128x64 monochrome (the little screen that shows the bar graph)\n- 1x Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points) (for tidy wiring without solder)\n- 4x Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc (we use 4 of the 40-pack: VCC, GND, SDA, SCL between ESP32 and OLED)\n\nNot in the inventory - grab from around the house:\n\n- USB power bank (any 5V \"phone charger\" pack with a USB output)\n- USB-C cable to go from the power bank to the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S's USB-C port\n\nThese two are treated as user-supplied because they aren't catalogued in the electronics stock - any spare phone-charging gear will do.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe OLED is an \"I2C\" device, which means it talks to the ESP32 over just two signal wires plus power and ground - four wires total. I2C is great because you can hang several different I2C devices off the same two pins later without needing more GPIO.\n\n- OLED `VCC` -> ESP32 `3V3` pin (the OLED is happy with 3.3V; do NOT use the 5V pin or the screen can be damaged)\n- OLED `GND` -> ESP32 `GND` pin\n- OLED `SDA` -> ESP32 `GPIO 21` (the default I2C data pin on the ESP32)\n- OLED `SCL` -> ESP32 `GPIO 22` (the default I2C clock pin on the ESP32)\n\nPlug both modules into the breadboard so nothing is dangling, then run the four jumpers between them. When you finish testing on the laptop, unplug the USB cable and move it to the power bank - everything keeps running because the ESP32 is happy with any 5V USB source.\n\nBefore uploading: open `WiFiRSSIMeter.ino` and replace `PASSWORD_HERE` with the real GeeFam WiFi password. Per the project convention, credentials live inside the sketch (not in any CLAUDE.md or commit).\n\n## Talking points\n\n- **RSSI** (Received Signal Strength Indicator) is just a number in dBm that the WiFi chip reports for the access point it's connected to. -30 dBm is \"I'm sitting on the router\", -90 dBm is \"I can barely hear it\". -67 dBm or better is fine for streaming video, worse than -75 dBm is where things get flaky.\n- The bar graph is just a filled rectangle whose width is mapped from the RSSI range onto the screen width. It's the same idea as a volume bar in any music app.\n- \"I2C\" uses only two signal wires (SDA = data, SCL = clock) and an address to pick which device on the bus to talk to. The OLED's address is almost always `0x3C`.\n- Because the ESP32 reconnects to WiFi automatically, you can keep walking around even if the signal drops out briefly - the meter will recover on its own.\n\n## Th","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":4},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1}]},{"id":37,"name":"Distance-Triggered Treat Dispenser","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from a 5V/2A phone-style charger (or laptop port) into the ESP32 NodeMCU; VIN/5V feeds the HC-SR04 and SG90 with a 470 uF cap across the servo rail, and the HC-SR04 ECHO line is divided 1k/2k down to 3.3V before reaching the ESP32 GPIO.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// Distance-Triggered Treat Dispenser\n// ESP32 NodeMCU + HC-SR04 + SG90 servo + TM1637 4-digit display\n//\n// Hold your hand ~10 cm above the ultrasonic sensor; the servo rotates\n// the slotted disk once and the running count on the display goes up.\n//\n// Libraries to install via Arduino IDE > Library Manager:\n//   - \"ESP32Servo\" by Kevin Harrington / John K. Bennett\n//   - \"TM1637Display\" by Avishay Orpaz\n\n#include <Arduino.h>\n#include <ESP32Servo.h>\n#include <TM1637Display.h>\n\n// ---- Pin assignments (must match the Mermaid wiring diagram) ----\nconst int PIN_TRIG    = 5;   // HC-SR04 TRIG (ESP32 3.3V out is fine for the input)\nconst int PIN_ECHO    = 18;  // HC-SR04 ECHO via 1k/2k divider down to ~3.3V\nconst int PIN_SERVO   = 13;  // SG90 signal wire (orange/yellow)\nconst int PIN_TM_CLK  = 22;  // TM1637 CLK\nconst int PIN_TM_DIO  = 21;  // TM1637 DIO\n\n// ---- Behaviour tuning ----\nconst float TRIGGER_CM        = 10.0;   // hand must be within this distance\nconst float TRIGGER_HYST_CM   = 20.0;   // must move back beyond this to re-arm\nconst int   SERVO_REST_ANGLE  = 0;      // disk slot blocked\nconst int   SERVO_DISPENSE    = 90;     // disk rotated to drop one M&M\nconst unsigned long DISPENSE_HOLD_MS = 350;  // hold open, then hold closed\nconst unsigned long LOOP_MS          = 60;   // ~16 Hz sensor polling when idle\n\nServo treatServo;\nTM1637Display display(PIN_TM_CLK, PIN_TM_DIO);\n\nint  treatCount = 0;\nbool armed      = true;   // false while hand still hovering, prevents repeats\n\n// Non-blocking dispense state machine. The servo move is launched from loop()\n// so that ultrasonic polling does not pause for ~700 ms during a dispense.\nenum DispenseState { IDLE, OPENING, CLOSING };\nDispenseState dispenseState = IDLE;\nunsigned long dispenseStateStartedMs = 0;\n\n// Measure distance in centimetres. Returns -1.0 on timeout.\nfloat readDistanceCm() {\n  digitalWrite(PIN_TRIG, LOW);\n  delayMicroseconds(2);\n  digitalWrite(PIN_TRIG, HIGH);\n  delayMicroseconds(10);\n  digitalWrite(PIN_TRIG, LOW);\n\n  // 25 ms timeout corresponds to a round-trip of ~430 cm, i.e. a maximum\n  // measurable one-way distance of ~4 m. Plenty for hand-over-dispenser use.\n  unsigned long pulseUs = pulseIn(PIN_ECHO, HIGH, 25000UL);\n  if (pulseUs == 0) return -1.0;\n\n  // Speed of sound ~343 m/s -> 0.0343 cm/us, divide by 2 for round trip.\n  return (pulseUs * 0.0343f) / 2.0f;\n}\n\nvoid startDispense() {\n  treatServo.write(SERVO_DISPENSE);\n  dispenseState = OPENING;\n  dispenseStateStartedMs = millis(","description":"**What it is**: Hold your hand over the box. A motor spins a little cardboard wheel and exactly one M&M drops into your palm. A small red display ticks up so you can see how many treats it has handed out today.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You built a candy machine that obeys your hand like magic, with no button to press. Set a daily limit, race your brother to trigger it the fastest, or aim it at the dog and watch his brain explode.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- ESP32 NodeMCU (the brain - reads sensor, drives servo, updates display)\n- HC-SR04 ultrasonic distance module (looks straight up for your hand)\n- SG90 micro-servo (rotates the cardboard disk that drops one M&M)\n- TM1637 4-digit display (shows the running count of treats dispensed)\n- 1k and 2k resistors (voltage divider on the HC-SR04 ECHO line - see Wiring notes)\n- 470 uF electrolytic capacitor (across the servo's 5V/GND, prevents brownout resets)\n- Breadboard + male-to-male DuPont jumpers\n- USB cable from a 5V/2A phone-style charger (preferred) or a laptop port for power\n\nYou will also build a tiny cardboard chassis: a tube (like an empty toilet-roll tube) holds the M&Ms, a flat cardboard disk on the servo horn has one M&M-sized slot cut out of it. As the servo turns from 0 deg to 90 deg the slot lines up with a hole in the chute and exactly one M&M drops out. This is the fun part - it will probably take three tries to get the disk geometry right.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n- The HC-SR04 needs 5V on Vcc and its ECHO pin outputs 5V logic. The ESP32's GPIOs are 3.3V (not 5V tolerant on the datasheet), so we drop ECHO with a simple 1k / 2k divider: ECHO -> 1k -> GPIO 18, and GPIO 18 -> 2k -> GND. That gives 5 V x 2k / (1k + 2k) = ~3.3 V at the GPIO. TRIG goes the other way (ESP32 out to sensor in) and a 3.3 V high is enough to trigger the HC-SR04, so no divider needed there.\n- The SG90 servo also wants 5V. Both 5V devices share the ESP32's VIN/5V pin (which on the NodeMCU board comes straight from the USB connector). The servo can briefly draw 500-700 mA when it stalls, and that dip on the shared rail can reset the ESP32. We park a 470 uF electrolytic capacitor right across the servo's 5V and GND on the breadboard rail to absorb those dips. Mind the polarity: the striped leg is the negative side and goes to GND. Use a 5V/2A USB charger rather than a laptop port if you can.\n- The TM1637 display is happy on 3.3V or 5V; we use 3.3V because the data lines are then natively at ESP32 logic level.\n- All grounds tie together on the breadboard's blue rail. This is essential - if the sensor's ground floats, distance readings will be garbage.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- \"What is an ultrasonic sensor?\" It chirps a sound too high-pitched for us to hear, then times how long the echo takes to come back. Sound travels ~343 m/s, so the code multiplies microseconds by 0.0343 cm/us and divides by 2 (because the chirp goes there and back).\n- \"Why does it only dispense one M&M, not a stream?\" Look at the `armed` variable. We disarm as soon as a ha","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":12},{"item_name":"Capacitor 470uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1},{"item_name":"4-digit 7-segment Display Module","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"SG90 Servo (9g micro)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 1K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 2.2K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"HC-SR04 Ultrasonic Distance Sensor","qty":1}]},{"id":38,"name":"Mailbox-Opened Pinger","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"18650 cell, charged by a TP4056 charge-only module; cell B+ goes straight to the XIAO BAT pad (3.7V, no boost stage), B- to XIAO GND","code_language":"cpp","code":"#include <WiFi.h>\n#include <HTTPClient.h>\n#include <esp_sleep.h>\n#include <driver/gpio.h>\n\n// WiFi credentials\nconst char* WIFI_SSID = \"GeeFam\";\nconst char* WIFI_PASSWORD = \"PASSWORD_HERE\";\n\n// Pins\nconst gpio_num_t TILT_PIN = GPIO_NUM_2;  // D0 on the XIAO - tilt switch input (wake source)\nconst int LED_PIN  = 3;                  // D1 on the XIAO - blue LED indicator\n\n// How long to keep the LED on as a visible \"mail!\" pulse (ms)\nconst unsigned long LED_ON_MS = 5000;\n\n// How long to wait for WiFi before giving up (ms)\nconst unsigned long WIFI_TIMEOUT_MS = 15000;\n\n// After we wake, ignore further wiggles for this long, to ride out a bouncing tilt switch.\nconst unsigned long DEBOUNCE_HOLDOFF_MS = 3000;\n\n// Counter that survives deep sleep\nRTC_DATA_ATTR int bootCount = 0;\n\nvoid flashLed(int times, int onMs, int offMs) {\n  for (int i = 0; i < times; i++) {\n    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, HIGH);\n    delay(onMs);\n    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);\n    delay(offMs);\n  }\n}\n\nbool connectWifi() {\n  WiFi.mode(WIFI_STA);\n  WiFi.begin(WIFI_SSID, WIFI_PASSWORD);\n\n  unsigned long start = millis();\n  while (WiFi.status() != WL_CONNECTED) {\n    if (millis() - start > WIFI_TIMEOUT_MS) {\n      return false;\n    }\n    delay(250);\n  }\n  return true;\n}\n\nvoid sendPing() {\n  // Simple GET to a public test endpoint. Swap for your own webhook /\n  // Home Assistant URL once the Pi is online (see Things to change).\n  HTTPClient http;\n  String url = \"http://worldtimeapi.org/api/timezone/Etc/UTC\";\n  http.begin(url);\n  http.setTimeout(5000);\n  int code = http.GET();\n  http.end();\n\n  if (code > 0) {\n    // Two quick flashes = \"ping sent OK\"\n    flashLed(2, 100, 100);\n  } else {\n    // One long flash = \"ping failed but I woke up\"\n    flashLed(1, 800, 0);\n  }\n}\n\nvoid goToSleep() {\n  // Make sure LED is off before sleeping\n  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);\n\n  // ESP32-C3 deep-sleep wake-on-GPIO.\n  //   - The classic ESP32's ext1 API does NOT work on the C3 (no RTC_IO peripheral).\n  //   - gpio_wakeup_enable() sets the trigger level for the pin.\n  //   - esp_deep_sleep_enable_gpio_wakeup() arms wake-from-deep-sleep on the bitmask.\n  // We use HIGH-level wake because our external 100k pull-down keeps the pin LOW\n  // while the flap is closed; the tilt switch pulls it up to 3V3 when it flips.\n  gpio_wakeup_enable(TILT_PIN, GPIO_INTR_HIGH_LEVEL);\n  esp_deep_sleep_enable_gpio_wakeup(1ULL << TILT_PIN, ESP_GPIO_WAKEUP_GPIO_HIGH);\n\n  // Bye!\n  esp_deep_sleep_start();\n}\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n","description":"**What it is**: Hide a tiny box inside your mailbox. The second the mail carrier lifts the flap, an LED inside your house blinks to tell you the mail just arrived. No more guessing, no more checking an empty mailbox five times a day.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You built a real-life notification, like a phone alert but made out of parts on your desk. The best part: the gadget sleeps for weeks on one battery and wakes up the instant something happens, like a little robot guard dog for your mail.\n\nA battery-powered XIAO ESP32-C3 sits inside the mailbox, asleep almost all the time. When the mail carrier lifts the flap, a tilt switch closes, pulls a GPIO pin HIGH, and the C3 wakes from deep sleep. It connects to WiFi, fires off an HTTP ping, blinks the indoor LED, then goes back to sleep. With the boost stage removed and the 18650 wired straight to the XIAO's battery pad, a single cell should run the project for a long time - though see the honest battery-life note below.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x XIAO ESP32-C3 (the sleeper)\n- 1x bare 2-pin Angle Tilt Switch (a tube with a metal ball that bridges two contacts when tilted - no electronics inside)\n- 1x 18650 cell + TP4056 charge-only module (power)\n- 1x 100k resistor - external pull-down on the wake pin AND the R of an RC debounce filter\n- 1x 100nF ceramic cap - the C of the same RC debounce filter\n- 1x Blue LED + 1x 100R resistor (visible \"I woke up\" indicator)\n- 1x Breadboard, ~8x M-M DuPont jumpers\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n**Power path (no boost stage - this matters for battery life):**\n\n- USB-C into the TP4056's input only when you want to charge the cell.\n- 18650 cell into the TP4056's B+ / B- pads.\n- TP4056 OUT+ goes to the XIAO's BAT pad (on the underside of the board - Seeed exposes it specifically for this use case). OUT- goes to XIAO GND.\n- The XIAO's own LDO runs directly from the 3.7V cell - no boost-to-5V-then-back-down-to-3.3V round trip, which was costing milliamps of quiescent current 24/7 in the earlier draft.\n\n**Tilt switch wiring (bare 2-pin switch + external pull-down + RC debounce):**\n\n- One leg of the tilt switch to 3V3 on the XIAO.\n- Other leg to GPIO2 (D0) AND to one leg of the 100nF cap AND to one leg of the 100k resistor.\n- Other leg of the 100nF cap to GND.\n- Other leg of the 100k resistor to GND.\n\nThat little RC network does two jobs at once:\n- The 100k pulls GPIO2 firmly LOW whenever the switch is open (including during deep sleep, when the chip's own internal pull-downs are off).\n- The 100k + 100nF together form a low-pass filter with a ~10ms time constant, which absorbs the contact bounce of the ball rolling around in the tube.\n\n**LED:**\n\n- LED long leg (anode) to XIAO D1 (GPIO3) through the 100R resistor.\n- LED short leg (cathode) to XIAO GND.\n- (220R was too large here. Blue LEDs have a forward voltage around 3.0-3.2V, so on a 3.3V GPIO the resistor sees only ~0.1-0.3V of headroom. 100R gives a few mA of forward current - bright enough to see across the room. If yours still looks dim, a dir","allocations":[{"item_name":"Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":8},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Blue","qty":1},{"item_name":"18650 Charger / Protection Board (TP4056, USB-C)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 100K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 100R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Angle / Tilt Switch (ball)","qty":1}]},{"id":39,"name":"OLED Spirit Level","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"USB-C cable from laptop to the XIAO ESP32-C3 (5V in, 3.3V out on the 3V3 pin powers the IMU and OLED)","code_language":"cpp","code":"/*\n * OLED Spirit Level\n *\n * Board: XIAO ESP32-C3 (3.3 V logic)\n * I2C bus shared by:\n *   - MPU-6500 IMU       at address 0x68\n *   - SSD1306 OLED 128x64 at address 0x3C\n *\n * Wiring (matches the diagram):\n *   XIAO 3V3 -> IMU VCC, OLED VCC\n *   XIAO GND -> IMU GND, OLED GND, LED cathode\n *   XIAO D4 (GPIO6) SDA -> IMU SDA + OLED SDA\n *   XIAO D5 (GPIO7) SCL -> IMU SCL + OLED SCL\n *   XIAO D2 (GPIO4) -> 220R -> Green LED anode\n *\n * Required libraries (Arduino Library Manager):\n *   - Adafruit GFX Library\n *   - Adafruit SSD1306\n *\n * The MPU-6500 is talked to directly over Wire (no extra library needed) so\n * the project stays small and the I2C details stay visible.\n */\n\n#include <Wire.h>\n#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>\n#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>\n#include <math.h>\n\n// --- Pins ---------------------------------------------------------------\nstatic const int PIN_SDA       = 6;   // D4 on the XIAO silkscreen\nstatic const int PIN_SCL       = 7;   // D5 on the XIAO silkscreen\nstatic const int PIN_LEVEL_LED = 4;   // D2 on the XIAO silkscreen\n\n// --- I2C addresses ------------------------------------------------------\nstatic const uint8_t MPU_ADDR  = 0x68;\nstatic const uint8_t OLED_ADDR = 0x3C;\n\n// --- MPU-6500 registers -------------------------------------------------\nstatic const uint8_t MPU_REG_PWR_MGMT_1   = 0x6B;\nstatic const uint8_t MPU_REG_ACCEL_CONFIG = 0x1C;\nstatic const uint8_t MPU_REG_ACCEL_XOUT_H = 0x3B;\n\n// At the default +/-2 g range the sensitivity is 16384 LSB per g.\nstatic const float ACCEL_LSB_PER_G = 16384.0f;\n\n// --- OLED ---------------------------------------------------------------\nstatic const int SCREEN_W = 128;\nstatic const int SCREEN_H = 64;\nAdafruit_SSD1306 display(SCREEN_W, SCREEN_H, &Wire, -1);\n\n// --- Bubble / level tuning ----------------------------------------------\n// Center of the \"vial\" on the OLED.\nstatic const int CENTER_X = SCREEN_W / 2;\nstatic const int CENTER_Y = SCREEN_H / 2;\nstatic const int VIAL_RADIUS   = 28;   // outer ring drawn around the bubble\nstatic const int BUBBLE_RADIUS = 6;\nstatic const int TARGET_RADIUS = 9;    // small ring marking dead-on level\n\n// Degrees of tilt that move the bubble all the way to the edge of the vial.\nstatic const float FULL_SCALE_DEG = 15.0f;\n\n// Hysteresis for the \"level\" LED: turn ON below LEVEL_ON_DEG, OFF only\n// above LEVEL_OFF_DEG. The gap stops the LED chattering on noise.\nstatic const float LEVEL_ON_DEG  = 1.0f;\nstatic const float LEVEL_OFF_DEG = 1.5f;\nbool isLevel = false;\n","description":"**What it is**: Tilt the breadboard and a little bubble slides around on the OLED screen, just like the bubble in a real spirit level at the hardware store. When the board is perfectly flat, the bubble lands in the center and a green LED lights up to tell you \"you're level.\"\n\n**Why it's fun**: It is a real tool you can carry around the house and use to check if shelves, picture frames, or the kitchen table are crooked. Your dad probably owns the boring plastic version, but yours has a glowing screen and a light that congratulates you when you nail it.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- XIAO ESP32-C3 (3.3 V logic, USB-C powered)\n- MPU-6500 IMU breakout (I2C, address 0x68)\n- 0.96\" SSD1306 OLED, 128x64, I2C (address 0x3C)\n- Green LED + 220R current-limiting resistor\n- Breadboard and a handful of DuPont jumpers\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThis project is the first time we put two devices on the same I2C bus. I2C is designed for exactly that: every device listens on the same two wires (SDA = data, SCL = clock) and is told apart by its address. The OLED answers to 0x3C and the MPU-6500 answers to 0x68, so there is no conflict.\n\n- Both the IMU and the OLED get **3.3 V from the XIAO's 3V3 pin**, NOT 5 V. The XIAO's GPIOs are 3.3 V tolerant only; feeding a 5 V I2C device into them can slowly damage the chip.\n- Tie all grounds together on the breadboard's blue rail.\n- XIAO `SDA` (D4 / GPIO6) goes to BOTH the IMU SDA pin and the OLED SDA pin.\n- XIAO `SCL` (D5 / GPIO7) goes to BOTH the IMU SCL pin and the OLED SCL pin.\n- The MPU-6500 and OLED breakouts already have pull-up resistors on SDA/SCL on the board, so no extra pull-ups are needed.\n- Green LED long leg (anode) to GPIO D2 (GPIO4) through the 220R resistor. Short leg (cathode) to GND.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- **Why two addresses work on one bus:** I2C is like a party line. The XIAO shouts \"0x3C, are you there?\" and only the OLED answers. Then \"0x68, what's your acceleration?\" and only the IMU answers. The clock pin keeps everyone in step.\n- **Tilt from gravity:** when the board sits flat, gravity pulls straight down through the Z axis of the IMU and X / Y read ~0. Tip the breadboard and some of that 1 g leaks into X or Y. `atan2(ax, az)` and `atan2(ay, az)` give us the tilt angles in radians, which we convert to degrees.\n- **Why the bubble goes to the high side:** in a real spirit level the bubble floats to the high end because it is less dense than the liquid. We fake that by drawing the bubble offset in the same direction as the tilt (positive tilt -> bubble offset positive on screen).\n- **Why the LED has a deadband:** raw accel data jitters by a fraction of a degree even when still. If we lit the LED at \"exactly 0.0 degrees\" it would flicker. We light it only when both pitch and roll are within +/-1.0 degrees, and turn it off only above +/-1.5 degrees. That gap is called hysteresis.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Add a calibration step: average 200 samples at boot and treat that as the new \"zero\" so the level reads true e","allocations":[{"item_name":"Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":10},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"MPU-6500 6-Axis IMU (GY-6500)","qty":1}]},{"id":40,"name":"Two-ESP32 Walkie-Talkie Lights","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"Two USB-C cables, one to each board (5V each) - the XIAO and the NodeMCU each get their own power from a laptop, phone charger, or USB hub.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// =====================================================================\n// Two-ESP32 Walkie-Talkie Lights\n// =====================================================================\n// Flash this sketch TWICE - once per board - changing the ROLE define.\n//\n//   Board A: XIAO ESP32-C3   -> uncomment ROLE_XIAO,    comment ROLE_NODEMCU\n//   Board B: ESP32 NodeMCU   -> uncomment ROLE_NODEMCU, comment ROLE_XIAO\n//\n// In this build the XIAO is ONLY the remote (sends button presses) and\n// the NodeMCU is ONLY the lamp (runs the web server, drives the LED).\n// The XIAO does not run a web server. The bidirectional version lives\n// in the \"Things to change once it works\" section.\n//\n// We avoid the IP chicken-and-egg using mDNS: the NodeMCU advertises\n// itself as \"nodemcu-lamp.local\" on the LAN, and the XIAO talks to that\n// name instead of a hardcoded IP. No re-flash cycle, no DHCP guesswork.\n// =====================================================================\n\n// ---- PICK ONE ROLE BEFORE UPLOADING -------------------------------\n#define ROLE_XIAO       // <-- uncomment when flashing the XIAO\n// #define ROLE_NODEMCU // <-- uncomment when flashing the NodeMCU\n// -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#include <WiFi.h>\n\nconst char* WIFI_SSID = \"GeeFam\";\n// TODO: replace PASSWORD_HERE with the GeeFam password before flashing.\nconst char* WIFI_PASS = \"PASSWORD_HERE\";\n\n// mDNS hostname the NodeMCU advertises; the XIAO calls it by this name.\nconst char* LAMP_HOSTNAME = \"nodemcu-lamp\";\n\n#if defined(ROLE_XIAO)\n  #include <HTTPClient.h>\n  const int BUTTON_PIN = 4;   // XIAO D2 = GPIO4, wired to button (other leg to GND)\n#elif defined(ROLE_NODEMCU)\n  #include <WebServer.h>\n  #include <ESPmDNS.h>\n  const int LED_PIN = 16;     // NodeMCU GPIO16 -> 220R -> LED anode; cathode -> GND\n  WebServer server(80);\n  bool ledOn = false;\n#else\n  #error \"Uncomment exactly one of ROLE_XIAO or ROLE_NODEMCU.\"\n#endif\n\n#if defined(ROLE_NODEMCU)\nvoid applyLed() {\n  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, ledOn ? HIGH : LOW);\n}\n\nvoid handleRoot() {\n  server.send(200, \"text/plain\",\n    String(\"NodeMCU lamp. LED is \") + (ledOn ? \"ON\" : \"OFF\"));\n}\n\nvoid handleOn() {\n  ledOn = true;\n  applyLed();\n  Serial.println(\"[HTTP] /led/on  -> LED ON\");\n  server.send(200, \"text/plain\", \"OK on\");\n}\n\nvoid handleOff() {\n  ledOn = false;\n  applyLed();\n  Serial.println(\"[HTTP] /led/off -> LED OFF\");\n  server.send(200, \"text/plain\", \"OK off\");\n}\n\nvoid handleToggle() {\n  ledOn = !ledOn;\n  applyLed();\n ","description":"**What it is**: Two tiny boards on opposite sides of the room, no wires between them. Push the button on one and the red LED on the other one blinks on. Push it again, it blinks off.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It feels like magic the first time. You stand in the kitchen, your dad's in the living room, you press the button, and his light flips on. That's the same trick every smart light switch in the world uses, and you just built one.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3 (the \"remote\" - has the button)\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the \"lamp\" - has the external LED)\n- 1x Tactile push-button (wired to the XIAO)\n- 1x Red LED (wired to the NodeMCU)\n- 1x 220R resistor (current-limit for the LED)\n- 1x Breadboard (everything plugs into this, no soldering)\n- Jumper wires (M-M for breadboard rails, F-M for board pins)\n- Two USB-C cables (one per board)\n- Existing GeeFam WiFi network\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n**XIAO side (the button):**\n- One leg of the push-button -> XIAO pin `D2` (GPIO 4)\n- Other leg of the push-button -> XIAO `GND`\n- No external pull-up resistor needed - the sketch turns on the ESP32's internal pull-up (`INPUT_PULLUP`), so a press reads LOW.\n\n**NodeMCU side (the LED):**\n- NodeMCU `GPIO 16` -> 220R resistor -> long leg (anode, +) of the LED\n- Short leg (cathode, -) of the LED -> NodeMCU `GND`\n- The resistor goes in series with the LED. `GPIO -> resistor -> LED -> GND` is the easy mental model.\n- Why GPIO16 and not GPIO5? GPIO5 is a \"strapping pin\" on the classic ESP32 - it's sampled at reset and has an internal pull-up active during boot, which makes any LED on it flicker for a moment every time the board resets. GPIO16 is a plain general-purpose pin with no boot-time role, so it's the cleaner first-project choice.\n\n**Both boards plug into their own USB-C cable.** They never connect to each other physically - they talk over WiFi.\n\n## Bring-up sequence (read before flashing)\n\nThis project uses mDNS so we don't have to chase IP addresses. The NodeMCU advertises itself on the LAN as `nodemcu-lamp.local`, and the XIAO talks to that name. No hardcoded peer IP, no chicken-and-egg re-flash cycle.\n\n1. Open the sketch. Replace `PASSWORD_HERE` with the GeeFam WiFi password (there's a `// TODO` comment marking the spot).\n2. **Flash the NodeMCU first:** uncomment `#define ROLE_NODEMCU`, comment out `#define ROLE_XIAO`, upload. Open Serial Monitor (115200) and watch for `mDNS up: http://nodemcu-lamp.local/`. Optional sanity check: from any browser on the same WiFi, open `http://nodemcu-lamp.local/` and you should see the lamp's status text.\n3. **Flash the XIAO second:** flip the `ROLE_` defines (XIAO on, NodeMCU off), upload. Open Serial Monitor and you should see it print `Will talk to http://nodemcu-lamp.local/led/toggle`.\n4. Press the button on the XIAO. The NodeMCU's LED toggles. The XIAO's serial log shows `-> GET ...` and `peer replied 200 : OK on`.\n\nIf mDNS resolution misbehaves on your network, the description for the \"Things to change\" section below explains","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x4.3mm (white pin)","qty":1}]},{"id":41,"name":"Hallway Weather Window","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"USB-A to Micro-USB from laptop or 5V/1A charger into the ESP32. 3.3V rail feeds the DHT11; 5V rail feeds LCD, relay coil, and (via COM/NO) the LED strip. Cap strip at ~200 mA on this rail; longer strips need a separate 5V/2A supply, GND tied.","code_language":"cpp","code":"/*\n * Hallway Weather Window\n * ----------------------\n * Top row of the LCD shows live indoor temperature + humidity from a DHT11.\n * Bottom row scrolls a short outdoor-weather summary pulled from Open-Meteo\n * (free, no API key) over HTTPS. If rain is forecast in the next 12 hours,\n * a relay closes and turns on a 5V LED \"rain warning light\".\n *\n * Board:  ESP32 NodeMCU-32S\n * Wiring: see wiring_diagram in the project record. Pins must match:\n *   LCD SDA -> GPIO 21    LCD SCL -> GPIO 22\n *   DHT11 DATA -> GPIO 4\n *   Relay IN   -> GPIO 25  (active-LOW: LOW = closed)\n *   Status LED -> GPIO 2   (via 220R to GND)\n *\n * Libraries needed (Arduino Library Manager):\n *   - \"LiquidCrystal I2C\" by Frank de Brabander\n *   - \"DHT sensor library\" by Adafruit  (+ \"Adafruit Unified Sensor\")\n *   - \"ArduinoJson\" by Benoit Blanchon (v6.x)\n *\n * Note on HTTPS: we use WiFiClientSecure with setInsecure(). This skips\n * certificate validation, which is fine for a hobby weather widget.\n * For a production-ish project you would pin the Open-Meteo root CA.\n */\n\n#include <WiFi.h>\n#include <WiFiClientSecure.h>\n#include <HTTPClient.h>\n#include <Wire.h>\n#include <LiquidCrystal_I2C.h>\n#include <DHT.h>\n#include <ArduinoJson.h>\n\n// ---------- user-edit area ----------\nconst char* WIFI_SSID     = \"GeeFam\";\nconst char* WIFI_PASSWORD = \"PASSWORD_HERE\";\n\n// Lat/lon for the forecast. Defaults: roughly Manchester, UK.\n// Change to your hallway's latitude/longitude (Google \"lat lon <town>\").\nconst float LAT = 53.48f;\nconst float LON = -2.24f;\n\n// If the chance of rain in any of the next 12 hours is >= this, warn.\nconst int RAIN_THRESHOLD_PERCENT = 50;\n// ------------------------------------\n\n// Pin assignments (must match the wiring diagram exactly)\nconst int PIN_DHT        = 4;\nconst int PIN_RELAY      = 25;\nconst int PIN_STATUS_LED = 2;\n\n#define DHTTYPE DHT11\nDHT dht(PIN_DHT, DHTTYPE);\n\n// LCD backpacks are usually 0x27 or 0x3F. We probe at boot and then\n// construct the real LCD object once we know which address answered.\nLiquidCrystal_I2C* lcd = nullptr;\nuint8_t lcdAddr = 0x00;\n\n// Timing\nunsigned long lastSensorReadMs   = 0;\nunsigned long lastWeatherFetchMs = 0;\nunsigned long lastScrollStepMs   = 0;\nconst unsigned long SENSOR_INTERVAL_MS  = 2000UL;               // every 2 s\nconst unsigned long WEATHER_INTERVAL_MS = 10UL * 60UL * 1000UL; // every 10 min\nconst unsigned long SCROLL_INTERVAL_MS  = 350UL;\n\n// State we render\nfloat   indoorTempC  = NAN;\nfloat   indoorHumid  = NAN;\nString ","description":"**What it is**: Hang it in the hallway. A little screen shows the temperature inside your house and a one-word forecast pulled live from the internet (like SUNNY or RAINY). When rain is coming, a blue light strip flicks on so you see it on your way out the door.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It is basically a tiny weather robot that yells \"TAKE A COAT!\" at your family before school. Your friends have to check a phone to know if it will rain - you just glance at the wall.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the brain, WiFi-capable)\n- 1x LCD 1602 with I2C Backpack (display)\n- 1x DHT11 Temperature & Humidity Sensor (indoor reading - 3-pin breakout preferred)\n- 1x 1-channel Relay Module (switches the LED strip)\n- 1x LED 5mm Blue (status indicator while talking to the internet)\n- 1x 220R resistor (current limit for the blue LED)\n- 1x Breadboard 830-point\n- DuPont jumper wires (M-M, M-F, F-F mix)\n- 1x USB-A to Micro-USB cable (power + programming)\n\nYou also need (not in this allocation, supplied separately):\n- A short 5V LED strip (or single LED-on-leads). The relay switches its POSITIVE leg between the 5V rail and the strip - see the wiring diagram. Keep it short: about 200 mA max at 5V if you are powering everything from one USB port (see \"Power budget\" below).\n- A free Open-Meteo API endpoint (no key required) - the sketch already points at Andy's home grid square in northern England; change the lat/lon at the top to wherever your hallway is.\n- If your DHT11 is the bare 4-pin sensor (not the small breakout PCB), add a 4.7k-10k pull-up resistor from DATA to 3.3V. The blue 3-pin breakout has this on board already.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n- The LCD's I2C backpack uses only 4 wires: VCC -> 5V, GND -> GND, SDA -> GPIO 21, SCL -> GPIO 22. The ESP32's I2C default is exactly those two pins, no configuration needed.\n- The DHT11 needs 3.3V. Some clones tolerate 5V, but 3.3V is safer with an ESP32 because its GPIO is 3.3V; using 5V Vcc with a 3.3V data pin can clamp current through the protection diodes. Data goes to GPIO 4. If you bought the bare 4-pin DHT11 (no PCB), you also need a 4.7k-10k pull-up from DATA to 3.3V; the 3-pin breakout already has one.\n- The relay module is low-level trigger (active-LOW). IN -> GPIO 25. We set the pin HIGH *before* `pinMode(..., OUTPUT)` so the relay doesn't click on at boot.\n- We are doing **high-side switching of the LED strip's positive leg**: the 5V rail goes into the relay's **COM** terminal, the strip's **+** wire goes to the relay's **NO** (normally open) terminal, and the strip's **-** wire returns directly to GND. When the relay energises, COM connects to NO and the strip lights up. NC is left unused.\n- The blue status LED goes from GPIO 2 -> 220R resistor -> LED anode -> LED cathode -> GND. GPIO 2 is also the NodeMCU's built-in blue LED on most clones, so you will see two LEDs blink in sync. That is fine - it confirms your wiring matches the silkscreen.\n- **GPIO 2 strapping-pin caveat**: GPIO 2 must be LOW or floating at boot ","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Blue","qty":1},{"item_name":"1-channel Relay Module","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"LCD 1602 with I2C Backpack","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"DHT11 Temperature & Humidity Sensor","qty":1}]},{"id":42,"name":"MQTT Home: Pi broker + XIAO sensor publisher","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"Orange Pi One: 5V / 2A wall adapter via the 4.0/1.7mm barrel jack (NOT micro-USB). XIAO ESP32-C3: 5V over USB-C from a laptop or phone charger. DHT11 draws power from the XIAO's 3V3 rail. Router + WiFi assumed already powered.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// MqttSensorPublisher.ino\n//\n// XIAO ESP32-C3 reads a DHT11 every 30 seconds and publishes\n// temperature + humidity to the Mosquitto broker running on the\n// Orange Pi One. This is the \"leaf endpoint\" half of the project;\n// the Orange Pi is the \"hub\" running mosquitto on port 1883.\n//\n// Topics published:\n//   home/livingroom/temperature   (float, degC, retained)\n//   home/livingroom/humidity      (float, %RH,  retained)\n//   home/livingroom/status        (\"online\" / \"offline\" via LWT)\n//\n// Subscribe from any machine on GeeFam to watch live:\n//   mosquitto_sub -h <pi-ip> -t 'home/livingroom/#' -v\n//\n// Libraries required (Arduino Library Manager):\n//   - PubSubClient   by Nick O'Leary\n//   - DHT sensor library by Adafruit  (+ Adafruit Unified Sensor)\n\n#include <WiFi.h>\n#include <PubSubClient.h>\n#include <DHT.h>\n\n// ---------- User config ----------\nconst char* WIFI_SSID     = \"GeeFam\";\nconst char* WIFI_PASSWORD = \"PASSWORD_HERE\";\n\n// Set this to the Orange Pi's IP shown on the router admin page\n// after first boot. Update once and reflash.\nconst char* MQTT_HOST = \"192.168.88.40\";\nconst uint16_t MQTT_PORT = 1883;\n\nconst char* MQTT_CLIENT_ID = \"xiao-livingroom\";\nconst char* TOPIC_TEMP     = \"home/livingroom/temperature\";\nconst char* TOPIC_HUM      = \"home/livingroom/humidity\";\nconst char* TOPIC_STATUS   = \"home/livingroom/status\";\n\nconst uint32_t PUBLISH_INTERVAL_MS = 30000;  // 30 s\n\n// ---------- Wiring ----------\n// DHT11 DATA -> XIAO GPIO 3 (silkscreen \"D1\")\n// DHT11 VCC  -> XIAO 3V3\n// DHT11 GND  -> XIAO GND\n// 10K pull-up between DATA and 3V3 (skip if your DHT11 module already has one)\nconst uint8_t DHT_PIN  = 3;\nconst uint8_t DHT_TYPE = DHT11;\n\nDHT dht(DHT_PIN, DHT_TYPE);\nWiFiClient wifiClient;\nPubSubClient mqtt(wifiClient);\n\nuint32_t lastPublishMs = 0;\n\nvoid connectWifi() {\n  Serial.print(\"WiFi: connecting to \");\n  Serial.println(WIFI_SSID);\n  WiFi.mode(WIFI_STA);\n  WiFi.begin(WIFI_SSID, WIFI_PASSWORD);\n  while (WiFi.status() != WL_CONNECTED) {\n    delay(500);\n    Serial.print(\".\");\n  }\n  Serial.println();\n  Serial.print(\"WiFi: IP=\");\n  Serial.println(WiFi.localIP());\n}\n\nvoid connectMqtt() {\n  mqtt.setServer(MQTT_HOST, MQTT_PORT);\n  while (!mqtt.connected()) {\n    Serial.print(\"MQTT: connecting to \");\n    Serial.print(MQTT_HOST);\n    Serial.print(\":\");\n    Serial.print(MQTT_PORT);\n    Serial.print(\" ... \");\n    // Last-will: if we vanish, broker publishes \"offline\" so the Pi\n    // (or a dashboard) knows this node went away.\n    bool ok = mqtt.co","description":"**What it is**: A tiny sensor sits on your shelf and quietly sniffs the room's temperature and humidity. Every 30 seconds it whispers the numbers across WiFi to a little computer in another room, and you watch them pop up on your laptop screen.\n\n**Why it's fun**: This is your first project where two gadgets in different parts of the house actually talk to each other - one listens, one talks, and you're the puppet master in the middle. Unplug the sensor and a \"I'm gone!\" message shows up on the laptop a few seconds later, like the gadget left you a goodbye note.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- Orange Pi One (H3) - the protagonist. Already booted into Armbian with `mosquitto` and `mosquitto-clients` installed (see the resume checklist in the OrangePi_One folder).\n- Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3 - the leaf sensor node.\n- DHT11 temperature + humidity sensor (3-pin module variant has its own pull-up; bare 4-pin DHT11 needs a 10K resistor between DATA and 3V3).\n- 1x 10K resistor (1/8W) for the DHT11 pull-up.\n- Mini breadboard + a handful of M-M jumpers to hold the XIAO and DHT11.\n- Router / WiFi on `GeeFam` (2.4 GHz) - Pi on Ethernet, XIAO on WiFi, both on the same subnet.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThis project is mostly a network wiring diagram, not a breadboard one. Three things have to be true:\n\n1. **Pi on the LAN.** Plug the Pi's Ethernet into the same router that broadcasts `GeeFam`. Power it from the 5V/2A barrel supply (NOT micro-USB - see the OrangePi_One CLAUDE notes about silent brownouts). Find its IP on the router admin page the same way you found the XIAO at `192.168.88.30`.\n2. **Mosquitto listening on the Pi.** After SSH-ing in with `ssh root@<pi-ip>`, run:\n   ```bash\n   apt update && apt install -y mosquitto mosquitto-clients\n   # Allow LAN clients (for learning only - no auth yet):\n   cat > /etc/mosquitto/conf.d/lan.conf <<'EOF'\n   listener 1883 0.0.0.0\n   allow_anonymous true\n   EOF\n   systemctl restart mosquitto\n   systemctl enable  mosquitto\n   ```\n   Confirm with `ss -tlnp | grep 1883`. You should see mosquitto bound to `0.0.0.0:1883`.\n3. **DHT11 on the XIAO.**\n   - `DHT11 VCC -> XIAO 3V3`\n   - `DHT11 GND -> XIAO GND`\n   - `DHT11 DATA -> XIAO D1 (GPIO 3)`\n   - 10K resistor between `DATA` and `3V3` (skip if your DHT11 is the 3-pin pre-pulled-up variant)\n\nUpdate `MQTT_HOST` in the sketch to the Pi's IP before flashing. Open Serial Monitor at 115200, then on the Pi run:\n\n```bash\nmosquitto_sub -h localhost -t 'home/livingroom/#' -v\n```\n\nYou should see `home/livingroom/status online` immediately, followed by temperature + humidity every 30 seconds.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- **Pub/sub vs request/response.** The XIAO doesn't know or care who's listening. It pushes readings to a topic; anything that cares (a dashboard, Home Assistant, a Node-RED flow on the same Pi) subscribes. Adding a second display device later is zero changes to the publisher.\n- **Last-will testament.** `connect()` registers `home/livingroom/status = \"offline\"` as a \"send this if I die\". If the XIAO loses","allocations":[{"item_name":"Orange Pi One (H3)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"DHT11 Temperature & Humidity Sensor","qty":1}]},{"id":43,"name":"Joystick Doodle Pad on OLED","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"ESP32 is powered from a laptop via USB; the joystick module and OLED both run off the ESP32's 3.3V rail through the breadboard.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// JoystickDoodle - paint on a 128x64 OLED with a 2-axis joystick\n//\n// Move the stick to move a cursor. Press the joystick button to toggle\n// \"pen down\" mode - while pen is down, the cursor leaves a trail.\n// Hold the button for 1 second to clear the screen.\n//\n// Hardware: ESP32 NodeMCU-32S + 0.96\" SSD1306 I2C OLED + analog joystick\n// Libraries: Adafruit_GFX, Adafruit_SSD1306 (install via Library Manager)\n//\n// Drawing strategy (kept simple on purpose):\n//   We keep our OWN canvas as a bool[128][64] - one cell per OLED pixel.\n//   Each frame we:\n//     1. clear the OLED buffer\n//     2. copy our canvas onto the buffer (every \"drawn\" pixel)\n//     3. stamp the blinking cursor on top\n//     4. push the buffer to the screen with display()\n//   This avoids any \"save/restore the pixel under the cursor\" trickery,\n//   so the cursor can never leave phantom dots behind.\n\n#include <Wire.h>\n#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>\n#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>\n\n// ----- Pin map (MUST match the wiring diagram) -----\nconst int PIN_VRX = 34;   // joystick X axis -> ADC1_CH6 (input-only pin)\nconst int PIN_VRY = 35;   // joystick Y axis -> ADC1_CH7 (input-only pin)\nconst int PIN_SW  = 32;   // joystick button (active LOW, needs internal pull-up)\nconst int PIN_SDA = 21;   // OLED I2C data\nconst int PIN_SCL = 22;   // OLED I2C clock\n\n// ----- Display setup -----\nconst int SCREEN_W = 128;\nconst int SCREEN_H = 64;\nconst int OLED_ADDR = 0x3C;   // common SSD1306 address; some clones use 0x3D\nAdafruit_SSD1306 display(SCREEN_W, SCREEN_H, &Wire, -1);\n\n// ----- Our own canvas: true = the pen has drawn here -----\nbool canvas[SCREEN_W][SCREEN_H];\n\n// ----- Cursor + pen state -----\n// Use float for sub-pixel movement so a slow stick push still scrolls smoothly.\nfloat cursorX = 64.0f;        // start in the middle of a 128-wide screen\nfloat cursorY = 32.0f;        // start in the middle of a 64-tall screen\nbool penDown = false;\n\n// ----- Button debounce + long-press -----\nbool      lastButton    = HIGH;   // pull-up means HIGH = released\nuint32_t  buttonDownAt  = 0;\nbool      longHandled   = false;\nconst uint32_t LONG_PRESS_MS = 1000;\n\nvoid clearCanvas() {\n  for (int x = 0; x < SCREEN_W; x++) {\n    for (int y = 0; y < SCREEN_H; y++) {\n      canvas[x][y] = false;\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n  delay(200);\n\n  pinMode(PIN_SW, INPUT_PULLUP);\n\n  // ESP32 ADC: default is 12-bit (0..4095) with ~3.3V full scale\n  analogReadResolution(12);\n  analogSetAttenuation(ADC_11db);\n\n  Wire","description":"**What it is**: Wiggle the joystick and a tiny blinking dot zips around a little screen. Click the stick down and the dot starts leaving a white trail behind it, so you can draw whatever you want one pixel at a time.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It's like having your own pocket Etch-A-Sketch that you actually built with your own hands. Draw your name, sketch a stick-figure dad, write a secret message, then hold the button for a second and watch the whole screen wipe clean so you can start over.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x Joystick Module (2-axis + button) - the star: two potentiometers (X and Y) and a push-button under the cap\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S - reads the joystick and drives the screen\n- 1x 0.96\" OLED Display (I2C) - the 128x64 canvas you paint on\n- 1x Breadboard (~400 tie-points) - share 3.3V and GND between three things cleanly\n- 1x Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc - power rails on the breadboard\n- 1x Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc - female ends plug into the joystick/OLED header pins\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nEverything in this build runs off the ESP32's 3.3V rail. Most generic 0.96\" SSD1306 OLED modules accept 3.3-5V on VCC because they have an onboard regulator, but we are using 3.3V everywhere so the whole project shares one rail - cleaner and one less thing to think about.\n\nA note on the joystick's VCC pin: most of these modules are silkscreened \"+5V\". Ignore that label and wire it to 3.3V anyway. The joystick is just two potentiometers and a push-button, so it doesn't care what voltage you feed it - the ADC reading is a ratio of the supply voltage, and the code reads it the same way at 3.3V as it would at 5V. If you DO connect the joystick's VCC to 5V on an ESP32, the button's SW pin will sit near 5V when the button is pressed, and that 5V will land on GPIO 32 - a 3.3V-only input. That's the kind of mistake that quietly stresses the chip, so just stick to 3.3V.\n\nJoystick to ESP32:\n\n- `+5V` (yes, even though it says +5V on the silkscreen) -> 3.3V rail\n- `GND` -> GND rail\n- `VRX` -> GPIO 34 (X axis, an input-only ADC pin - perfect for this)\n- `VRY` -> GPIO 35 (Y axis, input-only ADC pin)\n- `SW`  -> GPIO 32 (the click-down button under the stick)\n\nOLED to ESP32:\n\n- `VCC` -> 3.3V rail\n- `GND` -> GND rail\n- `SDA` -> GPIO 21 (default ESP32 I2C data line)\n- `SCL` -> GPIO 22 (default ESP32 I2C clock line)\n\nThe joystick button has no external pull-up resistor, so the code turns on the ESP32's internal pull-up (`INPUT_PULLUP`). That means \"released\" reads HIGH and \"pressed\" reads LOW - watch for that in the talking points.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- Move the stick: a blinking dot (the cursor) moves around the screen.\n- Click the button: the pen toggles between \"up\" and \"down\". With the pen down, the cursor leaves a trail of white pixels - that's the drawing.\n- Hold the button for one second: the screen wipes clean and the pen lifts. Good for \"oops, start over\".\n- The cursor blinks so you can see where you are even on a blank canvas.\n- The deadzone in the code (`DEADZONE = 250`) means the cur","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Joystick Module (2-axis + button)","qty":1}]},{"id":44,"name":"Laser Tripwire Alarm","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from a laptop or 5V phone charger into the Arduino Uno; the Uno's 5V rail powers the laser, photoresistor, and alarm LED.","code_language":"cpp","code":"<![CDATA[// Laser Tripwire Alarm\n// Aim the KY-008 red laser at the KY-018 photoresistor.\n// When something blocks the beam, the red alarm LED turns on\n// and the Serial Monitor prints \"BEAM BROKEN!\".\n//\n// Board: Arduino Uno R3 (5V logic)\n// Open Serial Monitor at 9600 baud to see readings.\n\nconst int PIN_LASER     = 7;   // KY-008 signal pin (S)\nconst int PIN_LDR       = A0;  // KY-018 analog out\nconst int PIN_ALARM_LED = 9;   // 5mm red LED through 330R to GND\n\n// Tuning: higher number on A0 = MORE light hitting the photoresistor.\n// We calibrate at startup, then trip if the reading falls well below\n// the bright baseline.\nint brightBaseline = 0;\nconst int TRIP_MARGIN = 150;   // how far below baseline counts as \"blocked\"\n\nvoid setup() {\n  pinMode(PIN_LASER, OUTPUT);\n  pinMode(PIN_ALARM_LED, OUTPUT);\n\n  Serial.begin(9600);\n  delay(500);\n  Serial.println(F(\"Laser Tripwire starting...\"));\n\n  // Turn the laser on and let the photoresistor settle.\n  digitalWrite(PIN_LASER, HIGH);\n  delay(800);\n\n  // Take a few readings of the \"beam clear\" state and average them.\n  long sum = 0;\n  const int samples = 20;\n  for (int i = 0; i < samples; i++) {\n    sum += analogRead(PIN_LDR);\n    delay(25);\n  }\n  brightBaseline = sum / samples;\n\n  Serial.print(F(\"Bright baseline (beam on target) = \"));\n  Serial.println(brightBaseline);\n  Serial.print(F(\"Will trip if reading drops below \"));\n  Serial.println(brightBaseline - TRIP_MARGIN);\n  Serial.println(F(\"Aim the laser at the photoresistor and wave your hand through the beam.\"));\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  int reading = analogRead(PIN_LDR);\n  bool broken = (reading < brightBaseline - TRIP_MARGIN);\n\n  if (broken) {\n    digitalWrite(PIN_ALARM_LED, HIGH);\n    Serial.print(F(\"BEAM BROKEN!  reading=\"));\n    Serial.println(reading);\n  } else {\n    digitalWrite(PIN_ALARM_LED, LOW);\n  }\n\n  delay(50);\n}\n]]>","description":"**What it is**: A real red laser shoots a thin beam across the room to a little sensor on the other side. The second someone walks through and breaks the beam, an alarm LED snaps on - just like in spy movies.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Set it up across your bedroom doorway and you'll know the instant your sister sneaks in. It's the closest thing to a real museum security system you can build on a kitchen table, and the laser dot is honestly just cool to look at.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- Arduino Uno R3 (the brain, 5V GPIO)\n- KY-008 Red Laser Module (the beam - the star of the show)\n- KY-018 Photoresistor Module (the beam catcher)\n- 5mm red LED + 330R resistor (the alarm indicator)\n- Breadboard, jumper wires\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n- **Laser (KY-008)**: three pins, labeled S (signal), middle (often unused or +), and `-` (GND). On most KY-008 boards the laser turns on whenever **S is HIGH and `-` is GND**. Wire S to Arduino pin 7 and `-` to Arduino GND. The middle pin is usually not connected, leave it floating.\n- **Photoresistor (KY-018)**: three pins - `S` (analog out), middle = VCC (5V), `-` = GND. Wire S to A0, middle to 5V, `-` to GND.\n- **Alarm LED**: long leg (anode) to Arduino pin 9 through the 330R resistor on the breadboard. Short leg (cathode) to GND. The resistor is non-negotiable - bare LEDs on a GPIO pin die fast.\n- **Aim**: with everything wired up, hold the laser so the red dot lands exactly on the small black sensor on top of the KY-018 module. The closer the dot is to the sensor, the bigger the difference between \"beam on\" and \"beam broken\" - which makes the tripwire more sensitive.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- \"Why does the laser need GND?\" Every circuit needs a complete loop. Electricity leaves the 5V/signal pin, does its work in the laser, and has to come back home through GND. Without GND, no loop, no light.\n- \"Why doesn't the laser go through my hand?\" Red 650nm laser light is visible, fairly low energy, and very directional. Your skin absorbs and scatters it, so almost none reaches the sensor on the other side - that's exactly why this works as a tripwire.\n- \"Why calibrate at startup?\" Different rooms have different ambient light. The sketch reads the photoresistor 20 times in the first second, averages those, and uses that as the \"beam is hitting the target\" baseline. Then any big drop from that baseline means something interrupted the beam.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Add a buzzer or relay-driven siren in parallel with the alarm LED so it makes noise.\n- Replace `delay(50)` with a latch: once tripped, stay tripped until a button is pressed to re-arm.\n- Aim the laser at a mirror placed across the room and bounce the beam back into the photoresistor - turn a doorway into a long tripwire.\n- Log the time of each break to Serial and graph it later.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nThis is the project that makes a 10-year-old say \"this is the coolest thing I've ever built.\" A real red laser, a real invisible tripwire across the kitchen, an alarm that g","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"KY-008 Red Laser Module (650nm)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"KY-018 Photoresistor Module","qty":1}]},{"id":45,"name":"Reaction-Time Game v2 (Proto PCB Edition)","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"USB cable from a laptop or 5V phone charger into the Arduino Uno's USB-B socket - the Uno regulates and feeds the proto PCB's 5V/GND rails.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// =====================================================================\n//  Reaction-Time Game v2 -- PROTO PCB EDITION\n//  Hardware: Arduino Uno R3 on a hand-soldered double-sided proto PCB.\n//\n//  Pins (must match the wiring diagram):\n//    D2  -> START button  (to GND when pressed, INPUT_PULLUP + ext 10K)\n//    D3  -> PLAYER button (to GND when pressed, INPUT_PULLUP + ext 10K)\n//    D9  -> RED LED   (WAIT / FAIL indicator), via 330R to GND\n//    D10 -> GREEN LED (GO indicator),          via 330R to GND\n//\n//  Game:\n//    1. Press START.  Red LED comes on (= \"wait\").\n//    2. After a random 1.5 - 4.5 s, red goes off, green comes on.\n//    3. Hit PLAYER as fast as you can. Reaction time printed on Serial.\n//    4. Press PLAYER while red is still on = false-start: red flashes.\n// =====================================================================\n\nconst uint8_t PIN_START  = 2;\nconst uint8_t PIN_PLAYER = 3;\nconst uint8_t PIN_RED    = 9;\nconst uint8_t PIN_GREEN  = 10;\n\n// Indexes for the per-button debounce state arrays below.\n// Pass these (not raw pin numbers) into wasPressed().\nconst uint8_t BTN_START_IDX  = 0;\nconst uint8_t BTN_PLAYER_IDX = 1;\nconst uint8_t NUM_BUTTONS    = 2;\n\nenum GameState { IDLE, WAITING, GO, RESULT, FALSE_START };\nGameState state = IDLE;\n\nunsigned long waitStartMs = 0;\nunsigned long waitDurationMs = 0;\nunsigned long goStartMs = 0;\n\n// Per-button debounce state, keyed by a small index (0..NUM_BUTTONS-1)\n// rather than the raw GPIO pin number. This way the helper can never\n// silently misbehave if a pin >= array-size were ever passed in.\nbool          btnLastReading[NUM_BUTTONS] = { false, false };\nbool          btnStablePressed[NUM_BUTTONS] = { false, false };\nunsigned long btnLastChangeMs[NUM_BUTTONS] = { 0, 0 };\n\n// ---- Debounced \"was this button just pressed?\" helper ----\n// idx selects which slot in the per-button arrays to use.\nbool wasPressed(uint8_t idx, uint8_t pin) {\n  const unsigned long DEBOUNCE_MS = 25;\n  bool reading = (digitalRead(pin) == LOW);      // active-LOW\n  if (reading != btnLastReading[idx]) {\n    btnLastChangeMs[idx] = millis();\n    btnLastReading[idx] = reading;\n  }\n  bool isStable = (millis() - btnLastChangeMs[idx]) > DEBOUNCE_MS;\n  bool fired = false;\n  if (isStable && reading && !btnStablePressed[idx]) {\n    fired = true;\n    btnStablePressed[idx] = true;\n  } else if (isStable && !reading) {\n    btnStablePressed[idx] = false;\n  }\n  return fired;\n}\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n  pinMode(PIN_ST","description":"**What it is**: It is your old reaction-time game, but built for keeps. Instead of wires popping out of a breadboard, the buttons and LEDs sit on a real circuit board that you can drop in a drawer, pull out next week, and it still works.\n\n**Why it's fun**: This is the first time you get to use a soldering iron, and at the end you walk away with a finished gadget that looks store-bought, not \"school project\". Hand it to your little brother and watch him try (and fail) to beat your best time.\n\n**Soldering project - up front warning.** This is the one project in the kit that uses a soldering iron. Everything else has been solderless (breadboard + DuPont jumpers). The point of this build is to take a circuit Andy already knows works - the reaction-time game that was his first build - and make it *permanent* on a double-sided proto PCB so it lives on a shelf instead of occupying the breadboard. Treat it as Andy's intermediate solo project. His son can watch and read out reaction times, but the iron stays in adult hands.\n\nThe Arduino itself is **not** soldered. We solder female header sockets onto the proto PCB and the Uno plugs into them, so the brain is reusable for the next thing. Only the buttons, LEDs, resistors, and short wire links get soldered.\n\nWorkflow (this is half the lesson):\n\n1. Rebuild the game on a breadboard one last time using these exact pins. Confirm green/red/buttons all behave.\n2. Plan the layout on the PCB *before* heating the iron - a sharpie dot on each hole that gets a part.\n3. Solder the lowest-profile parts first (resistors), then headers and buttons, LEDs last.\n4. Flip the board, route point-to-point with the stripped insides of an M-M jumper wire. Keep wires short.\n5. Inspect every joint under a lamp - should be shiny and concave, not a dull blob. Re-flow anything that looks like a \"cold\" joint.\n6. Plug the Uno in *last*. Power up. If anything draws weird current, unplug fast.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x Double-Sided Prototype PCB (the star - the new home of the circuit)\n- 1x Arduino Uno R3 (sits in a socket on the PCB, not soldered in)\n- 2x Tactile push-button (START + PLAYER)\n- 1x Green 5mm LED (GO light)\n- 1x Red 5mm LED (WAIT / FAIL light)\n- 2x 330R resistor (LED current limit)\n- 2x 10K resistor (button pull-ups - belt-and-braces alongside `INPUT_PULLUP`)\n- 1x Female header strip, cut into two short rails to socket the Uno's D-pins\n- 1x Male header strip, cut for the PCB I/O block\n- Hookup wire (stripped from an M-M jumper) for point-to-point links on the underside\n- 1x Breadboard for the pre-solder dry run\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n- Both buttons are wired **active-LOW**: one leg goes to its Uno pin, the other to GND. The Uno's internal pull-up (`INPUT_PULLUP`) plus the external 10K resistor to the **5V rail** pulls the pin HIGH when the button is open. Pressing the button shorts it to GND.\n- LEDs face the same way every time: long leg (anode) toward the Uno pin via the 330R, short leg (cathode) to GND.\n- Keep the LED resistor right","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"Pin Header 1x40 Female (2.54mm)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Pin Header 1x40 Male Breakaway (2.54mm)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Double-Sided Prototype PCB (assorted sizes)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":2},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":2},{"item_name":"Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x5mm (white pin)","qty":2}]},{"id":46,"name":"Clap-O-Meter (Live Sound Bar Graph)","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB-C cable from a laptop or 5V phone charger into the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S; the ESP32's onboard 3.3V regulator powers the KY-037 mic, the OLED, and the red LED through a 330R resistor.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// Clap-O-Meter: KY-037 microphone + OLED bar graph + loud-noise LED\n// Board: ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (Arduino IDE: \"ESP32 Dev Module\" or \"NodeMCU-32S\")\n//\n// What it does:\n//   - Reads the KY-037 analog output (GPIO34) on a fixed 1 ms tick (~1000 samples/sec).\n//   - Tracks how far each sample swings away from the quiet baseline.\n//   - Draws a live bar graph on the 0.96\" I2C OLED.\n//   - Flashes the red LED when a loud sound (clap, shout) is detected.\n//\n// Libraries (install via Arduino Library Manager):\n//   - Adafruit SSD1306\n//   - Adafruit GFX Library\n//\n// Wiring (matches the Mermaid diagram in description):\n//   KY-037 VCC -> ESP32 3V3   (10uF cap between VCC and GND, right at the module)\n//   KY-037 GND -> ESP32 GND\n//   KY-037 A0  -> ESP32 GPIO 34   (input-only ADC pin, perfect for a sensor)\n//   OLED VCC   -> ESP32 3V3\n//   OLED GND   -> ESP32 GND\n//   OLED SDA   -> ESP32 GPIO 21\n//   OLED SCL   -> ESP32 GPIO 22\n//   LED anode  -> 330R -> ESP32 GPIO 23\n//   LED cathode-> ESP32 GND\n\n#include <Wire.h>\n#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>\n#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>\n\n// --- Pin assignments (must match the wiring diagram) ---\nconst int PIN_MIC_AOUT = 34;   // KY-037 analog out\nconst int PIN_LED      = 23;   // Red LED through 330R\nconst int PIN_SDA      = 21;   // OLED SDA\nconst int PIN_SCL      = 22;   // OLED SCL\n\n// --- OLED setup ---\nconst int SCREEN_W = 128;\nconst int SCREEN_H = 64;\nAdafruit_SSD1306 display(SCREEN_W, SCREEN_H, &Wire, -1);\n\n// --- Sound tracking state ---\nint baseline       = 1900;   // average \"quiet\" ADC reading, set during calibration\nint peakLoudness   = 0;      // largest |sample - baseline| we have seen recently\n\n// --- Timing ---\nunsigned long lastSampleMs = 0;\nunsigned long lastDecayMs  = 0;\nunsigned long lastDrawMs   = 0;\nunsigned long ledOffAtMs   = 0;\n\n// --- Tunable thresholds ---\nconst int LOUD_THRESHOLD = 400;   // deviation from baseline that counts as \"loud\"\nconst int MAX_LOUDNESS   = 1500;  // anything past this maxes out the bar\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n  pinMode(PIN_LED, OUTPUT);\n  digitalWrite(PIN_LED, LOW);\n\n  // ESP32 ADC: 12 bit (0..4095), full 0..3.3V range\n  analogReadResolution(12);\n  analogSetPinAttenuation(PIN_MIC_AOUT, ADC_11db);\n\n  Wire.begin(PIN_SDA, PIN_SCL);\n  if (!display.begin(SSD1306_SWITCHCAPVCC, 0x3C)) {\n    Serial.println(\"OLED not found at 0x3C, halting.\");\n    while (true) { delay(1000); }\n  }\n\n  display.clearDisplay();\n  display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);\n  display.setTextSize(1);\n  ","description":"**What it is**: Clap your hands. A bar shoots up across the little screen and a red light flashes. Whisper and the bar barely twitches. Yell and it pegs all the way to the edge.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You finally get to SEE how loud you are, like the bouncing bars on a music speaker. Have a shouting contest with your brother and watch the screen prove who actually won.\n\nA live loudness meter built around the KY-037 microphone. The ESP32 reads the mic, scales the result onto a bar across the OLED, and flashes a red LED whenever the sound crosses a \"loud\" threshold - so a clap, a shout or a slammed door becomes a thing you can see.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- KY-037 Microphone Sensor (the protagonist) - analog audio input\n- ESP32 NodeMCU-32S - reads the mic, drives the OLED, controls the LED\n- 0.96\" OLED Display (I2C) - the bar graph and the labels\n- 5mm red LED + 330R resistor - flashes on loud sound\n- 10uF electrolytic capacitor - decoupling across the mic VCC/GND for a cleaner signal\n- Breadboard, a pack of M-M jumpers, a pack of F-M jumpers\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n- The KY-037 runs happily off the ESP32's 3.3V rail. Put VCC and GND on the breadboard power rails first.\n- Drop the 10uF electrolytic cap straight across the mic's VCC and GND pins on the breadboard. Longer leg goes to VCC, shorter leg to GND. This soaks up the little voltage wobbles the mic causes when WiFi or the OLED is busy, and the analog reading visibly settles down.\n- The mic's A0 (analog out) goes to GPIO34. That pin is input-only on the ESP32, which is exactly what you want for a sensor - nothing in your sketch can accidentally drive it.\n- The OLED is I2C: SDA -> GPIO21, SCL -> GPIO22. These are the ESP32's default I2C pins.\n- The red LED gets the usual treatment: GPIO23 -> 330R resistor -> long leg of LED -> short leg to GND. Without the resistor the LED burns out and possibly the GPIO with it.\n- Leave the D0 pin on the KY-037 unconnected. That's the built-in comparator with the blue trimpot, and we don't use it - the sketch does the threshold work in software so you can tweak it without a screwdriver.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- The mic doesn't output \"loudness\" - it outputs the audio waveform, swinging above and below a quiet midpoint many hundreds of times a second. We sample on a steady 1 ms tick (~1000 Hz) and measure how far each sample is from the calibrated quiet baseline. That absolute swing is what we draw.\n- The bar uses a \"peak with decay\" trick: every new high pushes the bar up instantly, but a steady countdown drags it back down 20 ms at a time. That's why a clap shoots the bar up and then it slides back - matches how our ears feel loudness.\n- The calibration at boot is just a 1-second average of the quiet room. Cover the mic or shout at it during boot and the baseline will be wrong - reset and let the room be quiet for a second.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Lower `LOUD_THRESHOLD` until a finger snap sets the LED off, or raise it so only a real clap counts.\n- Add a second LED ","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 10uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"KY-037 Microphone Sensor","qty":1}]},{"id":47,"name":"Laser Posture Coach (desk slouch detector)","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S powered via its USB-C port from a laptop or 5 V phone charger; the VL53L0X, OLED, LEDs and button are fed from the ESP32's 3.3 V rail.","code_language":"cpp","code":"#include <Wire.h>\n#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>\n#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>\n#include <Adafruit_VL53L0X.h>\n\n// ---------- Pin map (must match the wiring diagram) ----------\nconst int PIN_SDA       = 21;   // ESP32 default I2C SDA\nconst int PIN_SCL       = 22;   // ESP32 default I2C SCL\nconst int PIN_LED_GREEN = 25;   // good-posture LED\nconst int PIN_LED_RED   = 26;   // slouch-warning LED\nconst int PIN_BUTTON    = 27;   // recalibrate button (active LOW, internal pull-up)\n\n// ---------- OLED ----------\nconst int SCREEN_W = 128;\nconst int SCREEN_H = 64;\nAdafruit_SSD1306 display(SCREEN_W, SCREEN_H, &Wire, -1);\n\n// ---------- ToF sensor ----------\nAdafruit_VL53L0X lox = Adafruit_VL53L0X();\n\n// ---------- Posture state ----------\n// Target distance from sensor to chest when sitting upright.\n// Default 500 mm; press the button while sitting upright to recalibrate.\nint targetDistance_mm = 500;\n\n// Warning threshold: anything closer than this is \"slouching\".\n// We define \"too close\" as 80 mm closer than target.\nconst int SLOUCH_MARGIN_MM = 80;\n\n// VL53L0X gives noisy single reads, so we average a few.\nconst int SAMPLES = 4;\n\n// Debounce\nunsigned long lastButtonMs = 0;\nconst unsigned long DEBOUNCE_MS = 250;\n\nint readDistanceAveraged_mm() {\n  long sum = 0;\n  int valid = 0;\n  for (int i = 0; i < SAMPLES; i++) {\n    VL53L0X_RangingMeasurementData_t m;\n    lox.rangingTest(&m, false);\n    if (m.RangeStatus != 4) {  // status 4 = out of range\n      sum += m.RangeMilliMeter;\n      valid++;\n    }\n    delay(15);\n  }\n  if (valid == 0) return -1;\n  return (int)(sum / valid);\n}\n\nvoid drawScreen(int distance_mm, bool slouching, bool outOfRange) {\n  display.clearDisplay();\n  display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);\n\n  display.setTextSize(1);\n  display.setCursor(0, 0);\n  display.print(\"Posture Coach\");\n\n  display.setCursor(0, 12);\n  display.print(\"Target: \");\n  display.print(targetDistance_mm);\n  display.print(\" mm\");\n\n  display.setTextSize(2);\n  display.setCursor(0, 26);\n  if (outOfRange) {\n    display.print(\"-- mm\");\n  } else {\n    display.print(distance_mm);\n    display.print(\" mm\");\n  }\n\n  display.setTextSize(2);\n  display.setCursor(0, 48);\n  if (outOfRange) {\n    display.print(\"STAND BACK\");\n  } else if (slouching) {\n    display.print(\"SLOUCH!\");\n  } else {\n    display.print(\"GOOD\");\n  }\n\n  display.display();\n}\n\nvoid recalibrate() {\n  // Take a fresh reading and treat it as the new \"upright\" distance.\n  display.clearDisplay();\n  display.setTextSize(1);\n  display.setCursor(0, 24);","description":"**What it is**: A tiny laser on your desk watches your chest while you work. Sit up straight and a green light says \"GOOD.\" Hunch toward the screen and a red light flashes \"SLOUCH!\" until you straighten back up.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You built a robot hall monitor for your own posture - it catches you slouching before your mom does. Same kind of laser ranging that robot vacuums use to dodge furniture, except yours is yelling at you to sit up.\n\nA laser-based desk posture coach. The VL53L0X sits flat on the desk pointing straight up. When you sit upright, it sees your chest at (say) 500 mm. When you slouch forward toward the screen, that distance shrinks. A green LED + \"GOOD\" message means upright; a red LED + \"SLOUCH!\" warning means you've crept too close. A button lets you recalibrate the \"upright\" distance for your chair height.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (brain, drives I2C and LEDs)\n- VL53L0X ToF distance sensor (GY-VL53L0XV2 module) - the protagonist\n- 0.96\" I2C OLED display - shows live distance and posture verdict\n- 1x green 5 mm LED + 330R resistor (good-posture light)\n- 1x red 5 mm LED + 330R resistor (slouch warning)\n- 1x tactile push-button (3x6x4.3 mm) - recalibrate\n- Breadboard + DuPont jumper wires\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n- The ESP32 is powered from a laptop USB port via the on-board USB-C jack. Everything else is fed from the ESP32's 3.3 V and GND rails.\n- Both the OLED and the VL53L0X are I2C devices. They share SDA (GPIO 21) and SCL (GPIO 22). They have different I2C addresses (OLED 0x3C, VL53L0X 0x29), so they coexist on the same two wires without any extra hardware.\n- The VL53L0X module already has on-board pull-up resistors on SDA/SCL and a 3.3 V regulator + level shifters, so it's safe to power from either 3.3 V or 5 V. We use 3.3 V because the ESP32 is a 3.3 V device.\n- LEDs: long leg (anode) to the GPIO through a 330R resistor; short leg (cathode) to GND. Green on GPIO 25, red on GPIO 26.\n- Button between GPIO 27 and GND. We turn on the ESP32's internal pull-up so we don't need an external one. Pressed = LOW.\n- Aim the sensor straight up. Sit at your normal typing position, press the button to set the target distance, then try slouching forward - the red LED should light when your chest gets ~8 cm closer than the target.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- \"Time of Flight\" really does mean what it says: this chip fires a tiny invisible 940 nm laser pulse and measures how long the photons take to bounce back. Light moves about 30 cm in 1 nanosecond - the chip times that.\n- Unlike the HC-SR04 ultrasonic sensor (which uses sound and gives ~1 cm resolution), the VL53L0X reports in millimeters and works fine at close range. That precision is what makes \"you slouched 8 cm\" detectable instead of \"you moved, maybe.\"\n- Two different chips, one pair of wires. I2C lets multiple devices share SDA and SCL by giving each a unique address. The OLED answers at 0x3C; the ToF answers at 0x29.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Add a buzzer (a piezo again","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":2},{"item_name":"VL53L0X ToF Distance Sensor (GY-VL53L0XV2 compatible)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x4.3mm (white pin)","qty":1}]},{"id":48,"name":"Magnet Swipe Counter on OLED","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from a laptop or 5V USB charger into the ESP32's USB port; the ESP32's 3.3V rail powers the KY-003 and the OLED.","code_language":"cpp","code":"<![CDATA[// Magnet Swipe Counter\n// Wave a magnet past the KY-003 Hall switch. Each swipe increments a counter\n// shown on the OLED, and the green LED flashes once per swipe.\n//\n// Board: ESP32 NodeMCU-32S\n// Libraries (install via Library Manager):\n//   - Adafruit GFX Library\n//   - Adafruit SSD1306\n\n#include <Wire.h>\n#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>\n#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>\n\n// ---------- Pin assignments (match the wiring diagram) ----------\nconst int PIN_HALL = 14;  // KY-003 signal output (active LOW when magnet present)\nconst int PIN_LED  = 27;  // Green LED via 330R to GND\n\n// ---------- OLED setup ----------\n#define SCREEN_WIDTH 128\n#define SCREEN_HEIGHT 64\n#define OLED_RESET   -1\n#define OLED_ADDR    0x3C\nAdafruit_SSD1306 display(SCREEN_WIDTH, SCREEN_HEIGHT, &Wire, OLED_RESET);\n\n// ---------- State ----------\nunsigned long swipeCount = 0;\nint lastHallState = HIGH;          // KY-003 idles HIGH (no magnet)\nunsigned long lastEdgeMs = 0;\nconst unsigned long DEBOUNCE_MS = 40;\nunsigned long ledOffAtMs = 0;\nconst unsigned long LED_FLASH_MS = 120;\n\nvoid drawScreen(bool magnetNow) {\n    display.clearDisplay();\n\n    // Header\n    display.setTextSize(1);\n    display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);\n    display.setCursor(0, 0);\n    display.print(\"Magnet Swipe Counter\");\n    display.drawLine(0, 10, 127, 10, SSD1306_WHITE);\n\n    // Big count\n    display.setTextSize(3);\n    display.setCursor(0, 18);\n    display.print(swipeCount);\n\n    // Status icon\n    display.setTextSize(1);\n    display.setCursor(0, 54);\n    if (magnetNow) {\n        display.print(\"[ MAGNET DETECTED ]\");\n    } else {\n        display.print(\"Wave a magnet by...\");\n    }\n\n    display.display();\n}\n\nvoid setup() {\n    Serial.begin(115200);\n\n    pinMode(PIN_HALL, INPUT_PULLUP); // KY-003 has its own pull-up, but belt-and-braces\n    pinMode(PIN_LED, OUTPUT);\n    digitalWrite(PIN_LED, LOW);\n\n    Wire.begin();  // SDA=21, SCL=22 on ESP32 by default\n\n    if (!display.begin(SSD1306_SWITCHCAPVCC, OLED_ADDR)) {\n        Serial.println(\"OLED not found. Check wiring (SDA=21, SCL=22).\");\n        while (true) {\n            digitalWrite(PIN_LED, HIGH);\n            delay(200);\n            digitalWrite(PIN_LED, LOW);\n            delay(200);\n        }\n    }\n\n    drawScreen(false);\n    Serial.println(\"Ready. Wave a magnet near the KY-003.\");\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n    int hallState = digitalRead(PIN_HALL);\n    unsigned long now = millis();\n\n    // Detect a falling edge (HIGH -> LOW = magnet just arrived) with debounce\n    if (ha","description":"**What it is**: Wave a magnet past the little sensor and the number on the tiny screen jumps up by one. A green LED blinks at the exact same moment, so every swipe gives you a flash and a fresh count.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It feels like a magic trick because nothing touches anything - the magnet just floats by and the screen reacts. Race your dad to 100 swipes, or stick the sensor on a door and turn it into a secret \"who opened my room\" detector.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x KY-003 Hall Switch Sensor (the protagonist - a tiny chip that snaps LOW when the south pole of a magnet gets close)\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (reads the sensor and drives the screen)\n- 1x 0.96\" OLED Display (I2C) (shows the live count)\n- 1x Green 5mm LED + 1x 330R resistor (flashes once per swipe for instant tactile feedback)\n- 1x breadboard\n- A handful of jumper wires\n- Any small magnet from around the house (fridge magnet, motor magnet, the round neodymium ones from old hard drives)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe KY-003 module has 3 pins - usually labelled S (signal), middle (VCC) and minus (GND). The middle pin is power, even though it looks like the obvious \"data\" pin. Double-check the silkscreen before plugging in.\n\n- KY-003 `-` to ESP32 `GND`\n- KY-003 middle (`+` or `VCC`) to ESP32 `3V3`\n- KY-003 `S` to ESP32 `GPIO 14`\n\nThe OLED uses the ESP32's default I2C pins:\n\n- OLED `VCC` to ESP32 `3V3`\n- OLED `GND` to ESP32 `GND`\n- OLED `SDA` to ESP32 `GPIO 21`\n- OLED `SCL` to ESP32 `GPIO 22`\n\nThe LED sits on the breadboard:\n\n- ESP32 `GPIO 27` to the long leg (anode) of the green LED\n- Short leg (cathode) through the 330R resistor to `GND`\n\nThe KY-003 has its own pull-up resistor on board, so you don't need an external one. The sketch also enables the ESP32's internal pull-up as a safety net.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- Hall sensors are how your laptop knows the lid is closed and how your phone knows when the magnetic case is shut. They are everywhere and never wear out because nothing physically touches anything.\n- The KY-003 only reacts to one pole of the magnet (the south pole). If your magnet doesn't seem to trigger it, flip the magnet over - that is almost always the fix.\n- \"Active LOW\" means the signal pin is normally HIGH (3.3V) and drops to LOW (0V) when the sensor sees a magnet. This is backwards from how most people think about switches, so it's a great example of \"the world doesn't always agree with you\".\n- The 40 ms debounce window in the code is there because a magnet swept past quickly can make the digital line jitter for a millisecond or two. Without debouncing, one swipe could count as three.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Try different magnets - a small ceramic fridge magnet vs. a tiny neodymium - and see how close you have to get for each. That distance is the sensor's effective range.\n- Change `PIN_HALL` to a different pin (e.g. `4` or `15`) and update the wiring to match. Get into the habit of changing both at once.\n- Add a \"high score\" reset by holding the magnet near the sensor fo","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"KY-003 Hall Switch Sensor","qty":1}]},{"id":49,"name":"TV Remote LED Color Picker","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from Andy's PC to the Arduino Uno - the Uno's onboard 5V rail powers the KY-022 and all four LEDs.","code_language":"cpp","code":"/*\n * TV Remote LED Color Picker - KY-022 IR Receiver demo\n *\n * Point any household IR remote at the KY-022 and press buttons.\n * On the FIRST run, the sketch is in LEARN MODE:\n *   - Watch the Serial Monitor (115200 baud).\n *   - When prompted, point the remote and press the button you want\n *     to use for RED. Then GREEN, then BLUE, then YELLOW.\n *   - Each press is captured and stored in RAM for this session.\n *\n * After learning, every recognised press lights its matching LED for\n * one second. Unknown buttons print \"?\" to the Serial Monitor but do\n * not light anything - useful for discovering which codes your remote\n * actually sends.\n *\n * Reset the Arduino to re-learn with a different remote.\n *\n * Library: \"IRremote\" by shirriff / Armin Joachimsmeyer (v3.x or v4.x),\n * install from Arduino IDE Library Manager.\n */\n\n#include <IRremote.hpp>   // v3+ header. Older v2.x used <IRremote.h>.\n\n// ---------- Pin map (must match wiring diagram) ----------\nconst uint8_t PIN_IR     = 2;   // KY-022 signal \"S\" pin\nconst uint8_t PIN_LED_R  = 6;   // Red LED   via 330R to GND\nconst uint8_t PIN_LED_G  = 7;   // Green LED via 330R to GND\nconst uint8_t PIN_LED_B  = 8;   // Blue LED  via 330R to GND\nconst uint8_t PIN_LED_Y  = 9;   // Yellow LED via 330R to GND\n\n// Order matches the learning sequence below.\nconst uint8_t LED_PINS[4] = { PIN_LED_R, PIN_LED_G, PIN_LED_B, PIN_LED_Y };\nconst char*   LED_NAMES[4] = { \"RED\", \"GREEN\", \"BLUE\", \"YELLOW\" };\n\n// Storage for the four learned remote codes.\n// 0 means \"not learned yet\". Most real remote codes are non-zero so\n// this is safe for our purposes.\nuint32_t learnedCode[4] = { 0, 0, 0, 0 };\nbool     allLearned     = false;\n\n// ---------- Helpers ----------\n\n// Flash every LED briefly so the user can confirm wiring before\n// the first learn prompt.\nvoid wiringSelfTest() {\n  for (uint8_t i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n    digitalWrite(LED_PINS[i], HIGH);\n    delay(200);\n    digitalWrite(LED_PINS[i], LOW);\n  }\n}\n\nvoid allLedsOff() {\n  for (uint8_t i = 0; i < 4; i++) digitalWrite(LED_PINS[i], LOW);\n}\n\n// Block until the user presses *something* and we manage to decode it.\n// Returns the 32-bit decoded value. Ignores \"repeat\" frames so the\n// user holding a button still only learns one code.\nuint32_t waitForButton() {\n  while (true) {\n    if (IrReceiver.decode()) {\n      uint32_t code = IrReceiver.decodedIRData.decodedRawData;\n      bool isRepeat = IrReceiver.decodedIRData.flags & IRDATA_FLAGS_IS_REPEAT;\n      IrReceiver.resume();\n  ","description":"**What it is**: Point your TV remote at a little dark bubble on the breadboard and press a button. A red, green, blue, or yellow LED lights up, one color per button, like you just hacked the remote to control your own tiny stoplight.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You are teaching the Arduino to listen to a TV remote that already lives in your house, which feels a bit like wizardry. Once it works, hide the remote behind your back and make the lights change from across the room while your sister tries to figure out how you are doing it.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x KY-022 IR Receiver Module (the star - a tiny 38 kHz IR demodulator on a breakout board)\n- 1x Arduino Uno R3 (5V GPIO; the `IRremote` library is rock-solid on AVR)\n- 1x Breadboard (~400 tie-points)\n- 1x F-M jumper pack (for the 3-pin KY-022 header)\n- 1x M-M jumper pack (for the LEDs and resistors on the breadboard)\n- 1x Red, 1x Green, 1x Blue, 1x Yellow 5mm LED\n- 4x 330R resistors (one per LED)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe KY-022 has three pins. Different clone boards label them slightly differently, but the order is always the same: **S** (signal), middle pin is **VCC** on most boards, and the one marked with a `-` is **GND**. If yours has the `-` on the outside instead of the signal, just look at the silkscreen - it's always labelled.\n\n- KY-022 **S** -> Arduino **D2** (the `IRremote` library can use almost any pin; D2 is conventional)\n- KY-022 **VCC** -> Arduino **5V**\n- KY-022 **GND** -> Arduino **GND**\n\nEach LED gets its own resistor between the Arduino pin and the LED's long leg (anode). The short leg (cathode) goes to the breadboard's GND rail.\n\n- D6 -> 330R -> Red LED anode; Red cathode -> GND rail\n- D7 -> 330R -> Green LED anode; Green cathode -> GND rail\n- D8 -> 330R -> Blue LED anode; Blue cathode -> GND rail\n- D9 -> 330R -> Yellow LED anode; Yellow cathode -> GND rail\n- Arduino GND -> breadboard GND rail\n\nWhen the sketch boots it briefly flashes all four LEDs in sequence as a wiring self-test. If one stays dark, that's the row to recheck before worrying about the IR side.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- **Why the dome is dark plastic.** That bubble on the KY-022 is a filter that lets infrared through and blocks visible light. Without it, sunlight or a desk lamp would drown out the remote.\n- **Why 38 kHz matters.** Remotes don't send a steady \"on\" - they send the signal as a 38,000-times-a-second flicker. The receiver is tuned to that flicker, so steady light sources don't fool it.\n- **Pressing the same button gives the same code.** That's the whole point of a remote protocol. The sketch learns four of those codes and reacts when it sees them again.\n- **Pin 13 blinks for free.** The `ENABLE_LED_FEEDBACK` flag makes the Uno's built-in LED blink every time *any* IR frame arrives. Even an unknown button proves the receiver is alive.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Add a fifth \"off\" button that turns every LED off, by extending the learn sequence to five buttons.\n- Swap the LEDs for the 2-channel Songle relay (l","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Blue","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Yellow","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":4},{"item_name":"KY-022 IR Receiver Module","qty":1}]},{"id":50,"name":"Invisible Tripwire Alarm with OLED Face","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from a laptop or 5V phone charger into the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S; the ESP32's onboard regulator feeds the IR module, OLED, and LED from its 3.3V rail.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// Invisible Tripwire Alarm\n// ESP32 NodeMCU-32S + IR Obstacle Avoidance Module + 0.96\" SSD1306 OLED + red LED.\n// When something crosses in front of the IR module the OLED switches from a\n// calm \"ARMED\" face to an \"INTRUDER!\" face, the red LED turns on, and a\n// trip counter increments. State updates the moment the beam is broken or\n// restored, so you get instant visible feedback by waving a hand past it.\n//\n// Libraries (install via Arduino IDE Library Manager):\n//   - Adafruit GFX Library\n//   - Adafruit SSD1306\n//\n// Board: ESP32 Arduino > ESP32 Dev Module (or NodeMCU-32S).\n\n#include <Wire.h>\n#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>\n#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>\n\n// ---------- Pins (must match the wiring diagram) ----------\nconst int PIN_IR_OUT = 32;   // Digital OUT from the IR Obstacle Avoidance Module\nconst int PIN_LED    = 25;   // Red LED via 330R to GND\n\n// ---------- OLED ----------\nconst int SCREEN_WIDTH  = 128;\nconst int SCREEN_HEIGHT = 64;\nconst int OLED_I2C_ADDR = 0x3C;  // Most 0.96\" SSD1306 boards\nAdafruit_SSD1306 display(SCREEN_WIDTH, SCREEN_HEIGHT, &Wire, -1);\n\n// ---------- Sensor logic ----------\n// The IR Obstacle Avoidance Module is active-LOW:\n//   - No obstacle  -> OUT = HIGH\n//   - Obstacle     -> OUT = LOW\nconst int OBSTACLE_LEVEL = LOW;\n\n// Simple debounce so a flickery edge does not double-count\nconst unsigned long DEBOUNCE_MS = 40;\nint           lastRawReading   = HIGH;\nint           stableReading    = HIGH;\nunsigned long lastChangeMs     = 0;\nunsigned long tripCount        = 0;\n\nvoid drawArmedFace() {\n  display.clearDisplay();\n\n  display.setTextSize(2);\n  display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);\n  display.setCursor(28, 0);\n  display.print(\"ARMED\");\n\n  // Calm smiley: two eyes and a small smile\n  display.drawCircle(48, 38, 3, SSD1306_WHITE);   // left eye\n  display.drawCircle(80, 38, 3, SSD1306_WHITE);   // right eye\n  display.drawLine(54, 50, 74, 50, SSD1306_WHITE);\n\n  display.setTextSize(1);\n  display.setCursor(0, 56);\n  display.print(\"Trips: \");\n  display.print(tripCount);\n\n  display.display();\n}\n\nvoid drawIntruderFace() {\n  display.clearDisplay();\n\n  display.setTextSize(2);\n  display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);\n  display.setCursor(4, 0);\n  display.print(\"INTRUDER!\");\n\n  // Wide-eyed alarmed face: big eyes and an \"O\" mouth\n  display.fillCircle(48, 38, 4, SSD1306_WHITE);   // left eye\n  display.fillCircle(80, 38, 4, SSD1306_WHITE);   // right eye\n  display.drawCircle(64, 52, 5, SSD1306_WHITE);   // open mouth\n\n  display.setTextSize(1);\n  di","description":"**What it is**: Set up an invisible beam across a doorway. When someone walks through it, a little face on the screen jumps from calm to shocked, a red light snaps on, and a counter ticks up by one.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Sneak it onto your bedroom door and catch your sister mid-snoop - the screen literally goes \"!\" when she crosses the line. It's the same trick museums use to know when you're getting too close to the dinosaur bones.\n\nA doorway \"invisible tripwire\" that watches a small patch of air in front of the IR Obstacle Avoidance Module. The moment your hand (or your son's hand) crosses the beam, the OLED face flips from calm \"ARMED\" to shocked \"INTRUDER!\", a red LED lights up, and a trip counter on the screen ticks up. Pull your hand away and everything snaps back. It's the same satisfying \"did I really do that?\" feedback as a museum motion sensor, built in an afternoon.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x IR Obstacle Avoidance Module (the protagonist - a digital \"is something in front of me?\" sensor)\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (reads the sensor, drives the OLED and LED)\n- 1x 0.96\" OLED Display (I2C) (for the ARMED / INTRUDER faces and trip counter)\n- 1x 5mm red LED (lights while the beam is broken)\n- 1x 330R resistor (current-limit for the red LED)\n- 1x breadboard, plus F-M and M-M jumper wires\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n- The IR module has a 3-pin header: `VCC`, `GND`, `OUT`. Power it from the ESP32's `3V3` pin and `GND`. (The module accepts 3.3V-5V; 3.3V keeps logic levels safe for the ESP32.)\n- `OUT` goes to `GPIO 32`. The module has its own pull-up, so use `INPUT` (no internal pull-up needed).\n- The OLED is I2C: `SDA` -> `GPIO 21`, `SCL` -> `GPIO 22`, plus `3V3` and `GND`. These are the ESP32's default I2C pins, so `Wire.begin()` without arguments is enough.\n- Red LED: `GPIO 25` -> 330R -> LED anode (long leg) -> LED cathode (short leg) -> `GND`. Resistor first protects the LED if the wire slips.\n- The IR module has a small blue trimpot on the back. Turn it with a tiny screwdriver to adjust trigger distance from a couple of centimetres up to about 30 cm. There are also two indicator LEDs on the module itself: a power LED and an \"obstacle detected\" LED. The obstacle LED lighting up is a great sanity check before you even look at the OLED.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- *Active-LOW logic, again.* Just like the 2-ch relay board: the sensor's `OUT` line sits HIGH normally and pulls LOW when something is detected. Show your son `digitalRead(PIN_IR_OUT) == LOW` and ask him \"so... does LOW mean nothing's there, or something IS there?\" It's the classic beginner trap that's easier to learn once than to memorise forever.\n- *Why debounce?* The IR detector edge isn't perfectly clean - as a hand enters or leaves the beam you might get a few rapid HIGH/LOW flickers across a millisecond or two. Without the 40ms debounce window, one wave of the hand could log 5 trips. With it, you log exactly 1.\n- *Why a small built-in face on the OLED instead of just text?* Because feedback that feels like","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"IR Obstacle Avoidance Module","qty":1}]},{"id":51,"name":"Piggy Bank Coin Counter (TCRT5000 + OLED)","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB-C cable from a phone charger or laptop into the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S; the ESP32's 3.3V rail powers the TCRT5000 module, the OLED, and the indicator LED.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// Piggy Bank Coin Counter\n// Drop a coin past the TCRT5000 sensor - the OLED tallies it.\n// Board: ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (3.3V logic)\n//\n// Libraries (install via Arduino Library Manager):\n//   - Adafruit GFX Library\n//   - Adafruit SSD1306\n\n#include <Wire.h>\n#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>\n#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>\n#include <Preferences.h>\n\n// ---- Pin assignments (must match the wiring diagram) ----\nconst int PIN_TCRT_DO = 16;   // TCRT5000 digital output (active LOW when coin reflects)\nconst int PIN_LED     = 17;   // Green confirmation LED (with 330R)\nconst int PIN_SDA     = 21;   // OLED SDA (ESP32 default)\nconst int PIN_SCL     = 22;   // OLED SCL (ESP32 default)\n\n// ---- OLED ----\n#define SCREEN_WIDTH 128\n#define SCREEN_HEIGHT 64\n#define OLED_RESET   -1\n#define OLED_ADDR    0x3C\nAdafruit_SSD1306 display(SCREEN_WIDTH, SCREEN_HEIGHT, &Wire, OLED_RESET);\n\n// ---- Counting state ----\nPreferences prefs;            // Stores the total across reboots in NVS flash\nunsigned long coinCount = 0;\nint lastSensorState = HIGH;   // Idle state of the DO line is HIGH\nunsigned long lastCoinMs = 0;\nconst unsigned long DEBOUNCE_MS = 250;  // Ignore re-triggers within 250 ms\n\n// Cheerful messages that cycle as the bank fills\nconst char* cheers[] = {\n  \"Saving!\",\n  \"Ka-ching!\",\n  \"Nice!\",\n  \"Keep going!\",\n  \"Future you says THANKS\"\n};\nconst int NUM_CHEERS = sizeof(cheers) / sizeof(cheers[0]);\n\nvoid drawScreen(const char* message) {\n  display.clearDisplay();\n\n  // Header\n  display.setTextSize(1);\n  display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);\n  display.setCursor(0, 0);\n  display.println(F(\"PIGGY BANK\"));\n  display.drawLine(0, 10, 127, 10, SSD1306_WHITE);\n\n  // Big number\n  display.setTextSize(3);\n  display.setCursor(0, 18);\n  display.print(coinCount);\n\n  // Footer\n  display.setTextSize(1);\n  display.setCursor(0, 54);\n  display.print(message);\n\n  display.display();\n}\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n  delay(100);\n\n  pinMode(PIN_TCRT_DO, INPUT);  // Module has its own pull-up\n  pinMode(PIN_LED, OUTPUT);\n  digitalWrite(PIN_LED, LOW);\n\n  // Restore the saved count\n  prefs.begin(\"piggy\", false);\n  coinCount = prefs.getULong(\"count\", 0);\n\n  // Start the OLED on the default I2C pins\n  Wire.begin(PIN_SDA, PIN_SCL);\n  if (!display.begin(SSD1306_SWITCHCAPVCC, OLED_ADDR)) {\n    Serial.println(F(\"OLED init failed - check wiring\"));\n    while (true) { delay(1000); }\n  }\n\n  drawScreen(\"Ready - drop a coin!\");\n  Serial.print(F(\"Loaded saved count: \"));\n  Serial.println(coinCount);\n}\n\nvoid loo","description":"**What it is**: Drop a coin in the slot. The little screen bumps the count up by one, flashes a green LED, and shouts a cheer just for you. Unplug it, plug it back in tomorrow - your total is still there waiting.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It's a piggy bank that actually KNOWS how rich you are, and it celebrates every single coin like you just won a prize. Race your dad to see who can fill theirs first, or sneak up on it and try to drop a coin so fast the sensor misses it (spoiler: it won't).\n\nA real-world piggy bank that knows how much you've fed it. The TCRT5000 sits in the throat of the slot pointing across the gap; whenever a coin passes between the IR LED and the phototransistor, the module's digital output drops, the ESP32 counts it, the green LED blinks, and the OLED bumps the total along with a cheerful message. The count survives reboots because it is written to the ESP32's flash memory.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the brain - reads the sensor, drives the screen, stores the count)\n- TCRT5000 IR Reflective Tracking Module (the \"eye\" that catches each coin)\n- 0.96\" OLED Display, I2C (the running tally and cheer board)\n- 5mm green LED + 330R resistor (an instant \"I saw that\" flash)\n- Breadboard + DuPont jumpers (no soldering)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n- TCRT5000 has four pins: VCC, GND, DO (digital out), AO (analog out). We only use DO.\n- Power the TCRT5000 from 3.3V on the ESP32, not 5V. The module's onboard LM393 comparator is happy at 3.3V and that keeps the DO line at 3.3V too - safe for the ESP32 GPIO.\n- DO is \"active LOW\": idle = HIGH, coin overhead = LOW. The onboard pot lets you tune the trigger distance - turn it until the on-board indicator LED just lights when a coin is in position.\n- OLED is I2C: SDA = GPIO 21, SCL = GPIO 22 (the ESP32's default I2C pins).\n- The green LED's long leg (anode) goes to GPIO 17 through the 330R resistor; the short leg (cathode) goes to GND.\n- Common ground everywhere - the sensor module, OLED, and LED all share GND with the ESP32.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- The TCRT5000 is basically an IR flashlight and an IR camera sitting next to each other. The flashlight always shines forward; the camera only \"sees\" light if something in front bounces some of it back. A coin held a centimetre above the sensor is a great mirror for invisible IR.\n- Why \"active LOW\"? The comparator chip on the module is wired so that \"lots of reflected light\" pulls the output down to ground. It is the standard convention for these modules and is worth getting used to - it will come back on the IR obstacle modules and the PIR sensors.\n- The debounce timer (250 ms) is there because one coin passing through can briefly flicker the sensor twice as it tumbles. We treat anything inside 250 ms as the same coin.\n- `Preferences` is the ESP32's tiny built-in key/value store. We save the count every time it changes, so unplugging the bank does not reset the total.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Build an actual slot: a cardboard tube or a 3D-printed","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"TCRT5000 IR Reflective Tracking Module","qty":1}]},{"id":52,"name":"Porch Night-Light (KY-018 dusk-to-dawn fader)","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"Arduino Uno powered from a laptop USB cable; the KY-018 and the LED both run from the Uno's 5V rail.","code_language":"cpp","code":"/*\n  Porch Night-Light - KY-018 Photoresistor + Arduino Uno\n\n  Idea: when the room gets dark, a \"porch bulb\" (yellow LED) fades\n  ON. When it gets light again, it fades OFF. Smooth - no flickery\n  on/off at the threshold.\n\n  Wiring:\n    KY-018 module   ->   Arduino Uno\n      S (signal)         A0\n      + (middle)         5V    <-- KY-018 wants 5V, not 3.3V\n      - (GND)            GND\n\n    LED (yellow):\n      Uno pin 9  -> 220R resistor -> LED anode (long leg)\n      LED cathode (short leg)     -> GND\n\n  Notes for Andy:\n   - The KY-018 is just an LDR (light-dependent resistor) in a\n     voltage divider on a little PCB. The \"S\" pin gives an analog\n     voltage. Bright light = one extreme, darkness = the other.\n   - We deliberately do NOT use a hard threshold (if dark -> on).\n     A real porch light that hard-switched at 1 lux would flicker\n     every time a cloud passed. We smooth the reading and fade\n     between two darkness thresholds instead.\n   - If your KY-018 clone reads the OPPOSITE way (porch light on\n     in daylight, off in the dark), flip DARK_IS_HIGH below from\n     true to false. That's the only knob you need to touch.\n*/\n\nconst uint8_t PIN_LDR = A0;   // KY-018 S pin\nconst uint8_t PIN_LED = 9;    // PWM-capable pin (yellow LED)\n\n// --- Polarity knob -------------------------------------------------\n// Most KY-018 clones: higher analog reading = darker. A few clones\n// are wired the other way round. If the LED is ON in daylight and\n// OFF in the dark, change this to false and re-upload.\nconst bool DARK_IS_HIGH = true;\n\n// --- Smoothing -----------------------------------------------------\n// Keep a running (exponential moving) average so a passing shadow\n// doesn't snap the porch light on for half a second.\n//   0 = frozen, 1 = no smoothing. 0.05 = gentle.\nconst float SMOOTHING = 0.05f;\nfloat smoothedReading = 512.0f;  // start mid-scale\n\n// --- Calibration ---------------------------------------------------\n// At startup we watch the sensor for ~3 seconds while you COVER\n// then UNCOVER it, so the sketch learns what \"dark\" and \"bright\"\n// look like in your room. Press the Uno reset button to re-do it.\nconst unsigned long CAL_MS = 3000;\nint rawMin = 1023;\nint rawMax = 0;\n\n// --- Sensitivity ---------------------------------------------------\n// \"darkPercent\" is how dark (0-100) it has to get before the porch\n// light starts coming on, and where it reaches full brightness.\nconst int DARK_START_PERCENT = 35;   // start glowing at 35% dark\nconst i","description":"**What it is**: Turn off the room lights and watch a little yellow LED slowly glow brighter, like a porch light waking up at sunset. Turn the lights back on and it fades away again, all by itself.\n\n**Why it's fun**: No button, no switch, no app - the light just KNOWS when it's dark. Cup your hand over the tiny sensor and you become the sunset.\n\nA pretend porch-light that turns on by itself when the room gets dark. The KY-018 photoresistor watches the ambient light; a yellow LED on the breadboard fades up as the room darkens and fades back down at sunrise - exactly how a real dusk-to-dawn porch bulb behaves, but built on the kitchen table.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- KY-018 Photoresistor Module (the \"eye\" - an LDR on a little PCB)\n- Arduino Uno R3 (5V, 10-bit ADC - perfect for an analog sensor)\n- 1x yellow 5mm LED (stands in for a porch bulb; warm yellow looks the part)\n- 1x 220R resistor (current limit for the LED)\n- Breadboard + a handful of jumpers (3x F-M for the KY-018 module pins, the rest M-M for the LED side)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe KY-018 has a 3-pin header that reads, in most clones, `S`, `+`, `-` (signal, power, ground). Use F-M jumpers straight onto those:\n\n- `S` -> Arduino `A0`\n- `+` -> Arduino `5V` (the KY-018 wants 5V, not 3.3V - good habit to lock in now, because when you move to the ESP32 you'll need to remember the ESP32's GPIO and Vcc are 3.3V, not 5V)\n- `-` -> Arduino `GND`\n\nThe LED lives on the breadboard:\n\n- Arduino `D9` (a PWM pin - the `~` symbol is on it) -> 220R resistor -> LED anode (long leg)\n- LED cathode (short leg) -> Arduino `GND`\n\nPin `D9` matters - the sketch fades the LED, and fading needs PWM. If you put it on `D7` instead it can only go full on or full off.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- Why an analog pin (`A0`), not a digital one. The KY-018 doesn't say \"dark / not dark\"; it says \"this much light\", as a voltage from 0 to 5V. The Uno's ADC converts that voltage into a number from 0 to 1023, and that's the number we map into \"how bright should the LED be\".\n- Why smoothing. If the porch bulb just looked at the raw reading and turned on whenever it crossed a threshold, every passing shadow would flicker it. The sketch keeps a running average (an exponential moving average, or EMA) so it reacts to \"the sun went down\" but ignores \"a hand briefly waved past\". This is the single most advanced trick in the sketch - everything else is map/constrain/analogWrite.\n- Why two thresholds (`DARK_START_PERCENT` and `DARK_FULL_PERCENT`) instead of one. Two thresholds give a gentle fade-in region instead of a snap. This is what a real dusk-to-dawn sensor does inside, and it's also what makes the project feel polished instead of toy-like.\n- Calibration at startup. KY-018 clones vary - some give a high reading in the dark, some give a low one, and the absolute values depend on the room. Sampling for ~3 seconds at boot while you cover-then-uncover the sensor lets the sketch figure out what \"normal\" looks like in your room, so you don't have to hardcode ","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Yellow","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"KY-018 Photoresistor Module","qty":1}]},{"id":53,"name":"Laser Tripwire Alarm (LDR + KY-008 pair)","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from a laptop or 5V phone charger into the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S; the board's onboard regulator feeds 3.3V to both the laser module and the photoresistor receiver.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// Laser Tripwire Alarm\n// ----------------------------------------------------------\n// KY-008 laser shines across a doorway into an LM393-based\n// photoresistor module. While the beam hits the LDR, the\n// module's DO pin reads LOW. Break the beam (walk through it)\n// and DO flips HIGH -> we latch a red alarm LED on.\n// Press the re-arm button to clear the alarm.\n//\n// Board: ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (3.3V logic)\n// Serial: 115200\n// ----------------------------------------------------------\n\n// ---- Pin map (must match the Mermaid wiring diagram) ----\nconst int PIN_LASER       = 23;  // KY-008 signal (drive HIGH = laser on)\nconst int PIN_LDR_DO      = 34;  // LM393 module DO (input-only pin, fine for digital)\nconst int PIN_ALARM_LED   = 22;  // Red LED through 220R to GND\nconst int PIN_REARM_BTN   =  4;  // Button to GND, uses internal pull-up\n\n// ---- State ----\nbool alarmLatched = false;\nunsigned long lastEdgePrint = 0;\n\n// Simple debounce for the re-arm button\nconst unsigned long DEBOUNCE_MS = 40;\nint lastBtnReading = HIGH;\nint btnStable = HIGH;\nunsigned long btnChangedAt = 0;\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n  delay(200);\n  Serial.println();\n  Serial.println(F(\"Laser Tripwire Alarm starting...\"));\n\n  pinMode(PIN_LASER, OUTPUT);\n  pinMode(PIN_ALARM_LED, OUTPUT);\n  pinMode(PIN_LDR_DO, INPUT);          // module has its own pull-up\n  pinMode(PIN_REARM_BTN, INPUT_PULLUP);\n\n  // Turn the laser ON and start with the alarm clear\n  digitalWrite(PIN_LASER, HIGH);\n  digitalWrite(PIN_ALARM_LED, LOW);\n\n  Serial.println(F(\"Laser is ON. Aim it at the white dot on the LDR module\"));\n  Serial.println(F(\"and turn the blue trim-pot until the module's signal LED\"));\n  Serial.println(F(\"just barely turns OFF. Then block the beam to test.\"));\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  // ---- 1. Read the photoresistor module ----\n  // DO is LOW when the beam is hitting it (bright enough),\n  // HIGH when the beam is broken (dark).\n  int beamState = digitalRead(PIN_LDR_DO);\n\n  if (beamState == HIGH && !alarmLatched) {\n    alarmLatched = true;\n    Serial.println(F(\">>> TRIPWIRE BROKEN <<<\"));\n  }\n\n  // ---- 2. Drive the alarm LED from the latch ----\n  digitalWrite(PIN_ALARM_LED, alarmLatched ? HIGH : LOW);\n\n  // ---- 3. Re-arm button (debounced) ----\n  int reading = digitalRead(PIN_REARM_BTN);\n  if (reading != lastBtnReading) {\n    btnChangedAt = millis();\n    lastBtnReading = reading;\n  }\n  if ((millis() - btnChangedAt) > DEBOUNCE_MS) {\n    if (reading != btnStable) {\n      btnStable = reading;\n ","description":"**What it is**: A red laser shoots a thin beam across a doorway and lands on a tiny sensor on the other side. Walk through the beam and a red alarm light snaps ON and stays on until you press the button to reset it.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Set it up across your bedroom door and you instantly know when a sibling sneaks in, even hours later, because the light stays lit until you clear it. It is the closest you can get to building a real spy-movie laser tripwire with stuff that fits on a breadboard.\n\n## Eye safety - read first\n\nThe KY-008 is a real 650nm laser diode module (laser-pointer class, typically rated under 5mW but still hazardous). Even brief direct exposure or a glint off a shiny surface can damage the retina, and the damage is painless and permanent. Before powering the board:\n\n- Never look into the beam or the emitter aperture, not even \"just to check it's on\". If you need to confirm it works, look at the red dot on a matte surface (a piece of paper, a wall, the white face of the LDR module).\n- Never aim the laser at a person, a pet, a window, a mirror, polished metal, or any glossy surface that could bounce a specular reflection back at someone.\n- Aim it once with the receiver taped down, then leave it. Treat it like a loaded staple gun: it's pointed somewhere specific, and you don't pick it up to \"see where it goes\" while live.\n- The sketch turns the laser ON as soon as the board boots. Plug power in only after both modules are pointed at each other and taped in place.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x Photoresistor Module (LM393 comparator) - the receiver. It has a built-in pot to set the dark/light threshold and a clean digital DO output.\n- 1x KY-008 Red Laser Module (650nm) - the emitter. The narrow beam crosses the doorway and lands on the receiver.\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S - reads the receiver, controls the laser, latches the alarm.\n- 1x red 5mm LED + 220R resistor - \"the wire's been tripped\" indicator that stays on.\n- 1x tactile push-button - re-arm button to clear the latched alarm.\n- Breadboard + DuPont jumpers.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nReminder: the laser turns on the moment the ESP32 boots. Finish all wiring, point the laser at the receiver, and tape both modules down BEFORE you plug the USB cable in.\n\n- Both modules run on 3.3V from the ESP32 (the laser module is happy at 3.3V; it's a touch dimmer than at 5V but still very visible at room range).\n- The LM393 receiver module has 4 pins: VCC, GND, DO (digital out), AO (analog, ignored here). Only DO is connected to the ESP32.\n- The DO pin is **active LOW when bright**. With the laser hitting the LDR, DO reads LOW. When something blocks the beam, DO goes HIGH. The code reflects this exactly.\n- The KY-008 has 3 pins in a row. On this clone they are:\n  - **S** (signal) -> GPIO 23 on the ESP32. Drive HIGH to turn the beam on.\n  - **middle pin** -> **NC, leave disconnected**. Do not feed 3V3 here; it is not a power input on this module.\n  - **-** (GND) -> GND rail.\n- The LED goes from GPIO 22 through a 220R","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"KY-008 Red Laser Module (650nm)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Photoresistor Module (LM393 comparator)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x4.3mm (white pin)","qty":1}]},{"id":54,"name":"Coin Drop Counter (optical slot + OLED)","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"USB cable from a laptop or 5V phone charger into the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S USB-C port; the ESP32's onboard regulator feeds 3.3V to the slot sensor and OLED.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// Coin Drop Counter\n// ------------------\n// Optical slot sensor on GPIO 4 (digital out). Every time the beam is broken\n// (a coin, a finger, a piece of card), an interrupt fires and we bump the\n// count. OLED shows total drops, time since the last drop (updates live),\n// the last gap between drops, and the fastest gap we've ever seen.\n//\n// Hardware:\n//   Optical slot sensor -> ESP32 NodeMCU-32S -> 0.96\" SSD1306 OLED (I2C)\n//\n// Wiring (must match wiring_diagram):\n//   Sensor VCC  -> 3V3  (try VIN/5V if drops are being missed - see notes)\n//   Sensor GND  -> GND\n//   Sensor D0   -> GPIO 4\n//   OLED  VCC   -> 3V3\n//   OLED  GND   -> GND\n//   OLED  SDA   -> GPIO 21\n//   OLED  SCL   -> GPIO 22\n//\n// Libraries (install via Arduino Library Manager):\n//   - Adafruit GFX Library\n//   - Adafruit SSD1306\n\n#include <Wire.h>\n#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>\n#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>\n\n#define SCREEN_WIDTH 128\n#define SCREEN_HEIGHT 64\n#define OLED_RESET    -1\n#define OLED_ADDR     0x3C\n\nAdafruit_SSD1306 display(SCREEN_WIDTH, SCREEN_HEIGHT, &Wire, OLED_RESET);\n\nconst int SENSOR_PIN = 4;\n\n// Variables touched by the ISR must be volatile.\nvolatile unsigned long g_count       = 0;\nvolatile unsigned long g_lastDropMs  = 0;\nvolatile unsigned long g_lastGapMs   = 0;\nvolatile bool          g_newDrop     = false;\n\n// Debounce - a single coin can bounce the beam a few times on the way through.\n// 40 ms is short enough to catch fast drops but long enough to ignore bounce.\nconst unsigned long DEBOUNCE_MS = 40;\n\nunsigned long g_fastestGapMs = 0;   // 0 means \"not set yet\"\nunsigned long g_lastRedrawMs = 0;\n\nvoid IRAM_ATTR onBeamBreak() {\n  unsigned long now = millis();\n  if (now - g_lastDropMs < DEBOUNCE_MS) {\n    return;  // bounce - ignore\n  }\n  if (g_lastDropMs != 0) {\n    g_lastGapMs = now - g_lastDropMs;\n  }\n  g_lastDropMs = now;\n  g_count++;\n  g_newDrop = true;\n}\n\n// Helper: read a snapshot of the ISR-shared variables atomically.\n// Hides the noInterrupts/interrupts critical section from loop() so the\n// main-loop code stays easy to read.\nstruct DropSnapshot {\n  unsigned long count;\n  unsigned long lastDropMs;\n  unsigned long lastGapMs;\n  bool          newDrop;\n};\n\nDropSnapshot readSnapshot() {\n  DropSnapshot s;\n  noInterrupts();\n  s.count      = g_count;\n  s.lastDropMs = g_lastDropMs;\n  s.lastGapMs  = g_lastGapMs;\n  s.newDrop    = g_newDrop;\n  g_newDrop = false;\n  interrupts();\n  return s;\n}\n\nvoid drawScreen(unsigned long count,\n                unsigned long sinceLastMs,\n     ","description":"**What it is**: Drop a coin through a little slot. The number on the tiny screen jumps up by one, instantly, every single time. Drop ten coins fast and watch it climb to ten without missing a single one.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It feels like the screen is reading your mind because it counts faster than your eye can follow. Challenge your dad to drop five coins faster than you can, then point at the screen when it caught every single one.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x Optical Slot / Speed Sensor (the protagonist - an IR LED facing a phototransistor across a small gap)\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S\n- 1x 0.96\" OLED Display (I2C, SSD1306)\n- 1x Breadboard\n- A handful of male-male and female-male jumpers\n- Coins, popsicle-stick wedges, or strips of paper to drop through the slot\n\nNo resistors needed - the slot sensor module already has its own pull-up and current-limit resistors on board, and the OLED is I2C with internal pull-ups on the breakout. No LEDs in this build, so no current-limiting resistor either.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe slot sensor has 4 pins: `VCC`, `GND`, `D0` (digital out), and sometimes `A0` (analog). We only care about `D0`. When nothing is in the slot, the beam reaches the phototransistor and `D0` sits HIGH. The instant something blocks the beam, `D0` snaps LOW. That falling edge is what we count.\n\n- Sensor `VCC` -> ESP32 `3V3`\n- Sensor `GND` -> ESP32 `GND`\n- Sensor `D0` -> ESP32 `GPIO 4`\n- OLED `VCC` -> ESP32 `3V3`\n- OLED `GND` -> ESP32 `GND`\n- OLED `SDA` -> ESP32 `GPIO 21`\n- OLED `SCL` -> ESP32 `GPIO 22`\n\nOrient the slot **vertically** so coins fall down through the gap under gravity - tape the sensor module to the edge of a book or shoebox with the U-shape opening pointing up. A horizontal slot won't work; coins need somewhere to fall.\n\nIf drops sometimes don't register even though they clearly passed through the slot, try moving the sensor `VCC` wire from `3V3` to `VIN` (the 5V USB rail on the NodeMCU). The LM393 comparator on these slot modules is spec'd 3.3-5V, but some clones produce a cleaner, sharper falling edge with a full 5V supply. The `D0` output is still safe to feed into a 3.3V ESP32 GPIO - it's just a slow logic-level signal pulled to whatever rail the module runs on, and ESP32 inputs tolerate that fine in practice for this use.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- \"What is an interrupt?\" Most code in a loop is busy checking things. An interrupt is the chip dropping everything the moment a pin changes, running a tiny piece of code, then jumping back. For something as fast as a coin falling through a 5 mm gap, polling in `loop()` could miss it. An interrupt cannot.\n- \"Why a `volatile` keyword?\" The compiler usually assumes nothing else changes a variable behind its back. `volatile` tells it \"no, this can change at any moment because an interrupt might touch it\" so it always re-reads from memory.\n- \"What's debounce?\" A coin doesn't break the beam cleanly - it can flicker the signal a few times in a few milliseconds. We ignore any second trigger that comes ","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Optical Slot / Speed Sensor","qty":1}]},{"id":55,"name":"Stealth Timer - hold still or the LED dies","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S powered over its USB-C cable from a laptop or a 5 V USB phone charger; the PIR sensor, OLED and LEDs all run from the ESP32's onboard 5 V and 3.3 V rails.","code_language":"cpp","code":"#include <Wire.h>\n#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>\n#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>\n\n// --- Pin map (must match the wiring diagram) ---\nconst int PIN_PIR       = 13;  // HC-SR505 OUT\nconst int PIN_LED_GREEN = 18;  // \"holding still\" LED\nconst int PIN_LED_RED   = 19;  // \"you moved!\" LED\n\n// OLED\n#define SCREEN_WIDTH  128\n#define SCREEN_HEIGHT 64\n#define OLED_ADDR     0x3C\nAdafruit_SSD1306 display(SCREEN_WIDTH, SCREEN_HEIGHT, &Wire, -1);\n\n// --- Game state ---\nunsigned long stillStartMs   = 0;     // when the current \"still streak\" began\nunsigned long bestStreakMs   = 0;     // best ever streak this session\nunsigned long lastMotionMs   = 0;     // last time the PIR fired\nunsigned long redFlashUntil  = 0;     // red LED stays on until this millis()\nbool          warmingUp      = true;  // HC-SR505 needs ~30s settle time\n\nconst unsigned long WARMUP_MS    = 30000;  // give the sensor time to stabilise\nconst unsigned long RED_FLASH_MS = 800;    // how long the red LED stays lit on a break\n\nvoid drawScreen(const char *bigLabel, unsigned long currentMs, unsigned long bestMs) {\n  display.clearDisplay();\n\n  display.setTextSize(1);\n  display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);\n  display.setCursor(0, 0);\n  display.println(\"Stealth Timer\");\n  display.drawFastHLine(0, 10, SCREEN_WIDTH, SSD1306_WHITE);\n\n  display.setCursor(0, 16);\n  display.print(\"State: \");\n  display.println(bigLabel);\n\n  display.setCursor(0, 30);\n  display.print(\"Now : \");\n  display.print(currentMs / 1000.0, 1);\n  display.println(\" s\");\n\n  display.setCursor(0, 44);\n  display.print(\"Best: \");\n  display.print(bestMs / 1000.0, 1);\n  display.println(\" s\");\n\n  display.display();\n}\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n\n  pinMode(PIN_PIR, INPUT);\n  pinMode(PIN_LED_GREEN, OUTPUT);\n  pinMode(PIN_LED_RED, OUTPUT);\n  digitalWrite(PIN_LED_GREEN, LOW);\n  digitalWrite(PIN_LED_RED, LOW);\n\n  Wire.begin();  // ESP32 NodeMCU-32S default: SDA=21, SCL=22\n  if (!display.begin(SSD1306_SWITCHCAPVCC, OLED_ADDR)) {\n    Serial.println(\"OLED not found - check wiring / address.\");\n    while (true) { delay(1000); }\n  }\n\n  display.clearDisplay();\n  display.setTextSize(1);\n  display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);\n  display.setCursor(0, 0);\n  display.println(\"Warming up PIR...\");\n  display.println(\"Stay out of view!\");\n  display.display();\n\n  stillStartMs = millis();\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  unsigned long now = millis();\n\n  // 1. Warm-up window. PIR output is noisy for the first ~30s.\n  if (warmingUp) {\n    if (now >= WARMUP_MS) {\n      warmingUp     = f","description":"**What it is**: Stand in front of the little sensor and freeze. A green LED glows and the screen counts how many seconds you hold still. Twitch even a tiny bit and the red LED flashes - your streak resets to zero.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It's a real-life \"don't blink\" game you built yourself, and the sensor can spot a finger wiggle from across the room. Challenge your dad, your sister, or the dog - whoever holds the longest streak wins.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x HC-SR505 Mini PIR Motion Sensor (the star: a tiny PIR with a clear plastic dome that fires HIGH when it sees a warm body move)\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S\n- 1x 0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)\n- 1x 5mm green LED (glows while you are holding still)\n- 1x 5mm red LED (flashes when you twitch)\n- 2x 330R resistors (one in series with each LED)\n- 1x breadboard, plus a handful of M-M and F-M jumpers\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe HC-SR505 has only three pins: `VCC`, `OUT`, `GND`. It accepts 5 V on VCC and its OUT line idles at 0 V and pulses HIGH (about 3.0 V on this module) when triggered, which the ESP32's 3.3 V GPIO can read safely without a level shifter.\n\nPower the PIR and the OLED from the ESP32's `5V` pin (which is the USB rail when you have the board plugged into your laptop). Tie every ground - PIR, OLED, both LEDs - back to the ESP32's `GND`.\n\nThe OLED is I2C and goes on the ESP32's default I2C pins: `SDA = GPIO 21`, `SCL = GPIO 22`. The PIR's OUT goes to `GPIO 13`. The green LED's anode goes through a 330R resistor to `GPIO 18`, the red LED's anode goes through its own 330R resistor to `GPIO 19`, and both cathodes go to ground.\n\nImportant first-time gotcha: the HC-SR505 needs about 30 seconds after power-up to settle - it will output garbage during that window. The sketch handles this for you by showing a \"Warming up PIR\" screen and slow-pulsing the red LED. Stay out of view while that's running.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- The HC-SR505 doesn't see *light*. It sees *infrared heat moving across its field of view*. That's why holding still in front of it - even with your eyes open - makes it go quiet.\n- Inside that little plastic dome is a Fresnel lens. It splits the view into zones; the sensor only triggers when warmth crosses from one zone into another.\n- The OUT pin is digital, not analog. There's no \"how much motion\" reading - just yes or no.\n- The ESP32 is reading the pin millions of times per second, but we only act once per loop with a tiny delay - that's enough.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Add a buzzer that beeps softly the longer your streak gets, so you have to hold still through rising tension.\n- Show a tiny \"high score\" bar graph on the OLED that grows with your best time.\n- Save the best streak to ESP32 flash (Preferences library) so it survives a reboot.\n- Add a second HC-SR505 aimed sideways and only count \"frontal\" motion - turns the game into a stealth competition for two people.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nPIR sensors are the everyday motion detector you've seen a thousand times - in burglar ala","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":2},{"item_name":"HC-SR505 Mini PIR Motion Sensor","qty":1}]},{"id":56,"name":"Storm Glass - 3-hour pressure trend forecaster","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S powered from a laptop / phone-charger USB cable; the ESP32's 3.3 V pin supplies both the BMP280 and the OLED through the breadboard rails.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// Storm Glass - barometric trend forecaster on an OLED\n//\n// Hardware: ESP32 NodeMCU-32S + GY-BMP280 + 0.96\" SSD1306 OLED, all on one I2C bus.\n//\n// What it does:\n//   - Reads pressure (hPa) from the BMP280 once per second.\n//   - Keeps a rolling 3-hour history (one sample per minute) in RAM.\n//   - Every second draws: current pressure, temperature, a big trend arrow\n//     (up / flat / down), and a one-word forecast based on the 3-hour delta.\n//\n// Forecast rules of thumb that meteorologists actually use:\n//   - Pressure rising > +1.0 hPa / 3 h .... clearing / fair\n//   - Pressure steady within +-1.0 hPa .... no change\n//   - Pressure falling < -1.0 hPa / 3 h ... rain / storm likely\n//   - Falling fast (< -3.0 hPa / 3 h) ..... storm warning\n//\n// Wiring (matches the Mermaid diagram):\n//   ESP32 3V3 -> BMP280 VCC, OLED VCC\n//   ESP32 GND -> BMP280 GND, OLED GND\n//   ESP32 GPIO21 (SDA) -> BMP280 SDA, OLED SDA\n//   ESP32 GPIO22 (SCL) -> BMP280 SCL, OLED SCL\n//\n// Library install (Arduino IDE -> Library Manager):\n//   - \"Adafruit BMP280 Library\" (also pulls Adafruit BusIO + Adafruit Unified Sensor)\n//   - \"Adafruit SSD1306\"        (also pulls Adafruit GFX)\n\n#include <Wire.h>\n#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>\n#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>\n#include <Adafruit_BMP280.h>\n\n// ---------- pins ----------\n// ESP32 default I2C pins. Both modules sit on this same bus.\nstatic const int PIN_SDA = 21;\nstatic const int PIN_SCL = 22;\n\n// ---------- I2C addresses ----------\n// GY-BMP280 clones are usually at 0x76 (SDO tied low). A few are at 0x77.\n// If begin() fails at 0x76 we try 0x77 automatically.\nstatic const uint8_t BMP280_ADDR_PRIMARY   = 0x76;\nstatic const uint8_t BMP280_ADDR_SECONDARY = 0x77;\n\n// 128x64 SSD1306 OLED, typically at 0x3C.\nstatic const uint8_t OLED_ADDR    = 0x3C;\nstatic const int     OLED_WIDTH   = 128;\nstatic const int     OLED_HEIGHT  = 64;\nstatic const int     OLED_RESET   = -1;   // share ESP32 reset\n\nAdafruit_BMP280 bmp;                       // I2C\nAdafruit_SSD1306 oled(OLED_WIDTH, OLED_HEIGHT, &Wire, OLED_RESET);\n\n// ---------- history buffer ----------\n// One pressure sample per minute, 180 minutes = 3 hours.\nstatic const int HISTORY_MINUTES = 180;\nfloat history[HISTORY_MINUTES];\nint   historyCount = 0;       // how many slots are valid (grows to HISTORY_MINUTES)\nint   historyHead  = 0;       // next index to write\n\nunsigned long lastSampleMs   = 0;   // last time we appended to history\nunsigned long lastDisplayMs  = 0;   // last time we redrew\n\n// ---","description":"**What it is**: A little screen sits on your desk showing today's air pressure, a giant up or down arrow, and one word like \"Fair\" or \"Storm!\" The arrow flips direction hours before the weather actually changes outside.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You can predict the weather before the weather app does, then run around the house yelling \"I told you it would rain!\" Pick it up and walk upstairs and you can literally watch the pressure drop, because you just climbed away from a tiny bit of the sky.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- **GY-BMP280 Barometric Pressure Sensor** - the protagonist. A tiny Bosch chip that measures absolute air pressure to about +-1 Pa. Pressure is what weather forecasters actually watch: rising pressure usually means fair weather coming, falling pressure means a storm coming.\n- **ESP32 NodeMCU-32S** - reads the sensor and drives the screen.\n- **0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)** - shows the current pressure, a big up/down arrow, and a one-word forecast.\n- **Breadboard** plus **M-M** and **F-M jumper wires** - both modules share the same two I2C wires (SDA + SCL) plus 3.3 V and GND, so the breadboard just acts as a 4-rail splitter.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nBoth modules are I2C. That means four wires total from the ESP32, no matter how many I2C devices you add:\n\n- ESP32 `3V3` -> breadboard `+` rail -> BMP280 `VCC` and OLED `VCC`\n- ESP32 `GND` -> breadboard `-` rail -> BMP280 `GND` and OLED `GND`\n- ESP32 `GPIO21` (SDA) -> BMP280 `SDA` and OLED `SDA`\n- ESP32 `GPIO22` (SCL) -> BMP280 `SCL` and OLED `SCL`\n\nBoth boards already have I2C pull-up resistors on them, so no extra resistors are needed.\n\nThe BMP280 sits at I2C address `0x76` on most GY-BMP280 clones. A few clones are at `0x77`. The code tries both so you do not have to care.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- **What is air pressure?** The weight of all the air above you, pressing down. At sea level on an average day it is about 1013 hPa. When a storm front moves in, that number drops; when it leaves, it rises.\n- **Why a 3-hour delta?** Weather services use the 3-hour pressure tendency as the basic forecast signal. One single reading tells you nothing - it is the *change over time* that predicts the weather.\n- **Why a ring buffer?** We keep 180 readings (one per minute for 3 hours). Instead of growing a list forever, we overwrite the oldest slot every minute. This is the same trick used in audio buffers and game replay systems.\n- **I2C is a shared bus.** The OLED and the BMP280 use the *same* two wires. They get along because they have different addresses (`0x3C` vs `0x76`).\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Add a 24-hour delta too (just bump `HISTORY_MINUTES` to 1440 and add a second arrow).\n- Plot the history as a tiny line graph across the bottom of the OLED.\n- Publish the pressure to MQTT once the Orange Pi hub is online, so the Hallway Weather Window project can show it alongside temperature.\n- Calibrate to altitude: feed in the local altitude and the sketch can spit out sea-level-equivalent pressure that matches wha","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"GY-BMP280 Barometric Pressure Sensor","qty":1}]},{"id":57,"name":"Pocket Battery Health Monitor (OLED + LEDs)","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"ESP32 powered by laptop USB. OLED runs from the ESP32's 3.3V rail; LEDs are driven by GPIO. The voltage module is passive (just resistors) and draws no power. The battery under test connects only to the module's input screws.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// Battery Health OLED Monitor\n// Reads a low-voltage DC source through the Voltage Sensor Module (5:1 divider)\n// and shows live voltage, a bar graph, and a status word on the OLED.\n// A green LED lights when voltage looks \"healthy\"; a red LED warns when low.\n//\n// Board: ESP32 NodeMCU-32S\n// Libraries (install in Arduino IDE Library Manager):\n//   - Adafruit GFX Library\n//   - Adafruit SSD1306\n//\n// SAFETY: never feed more than 25V DC into the voltage module.\n//         The module is DC-only. No AC. No mains. Common-ground with the ESP32.\n//\n// IMPORTANT ADC RANGE NOTE:\n//   The ESP32 ADC with 11dB attenuation only stays linear from ~150mV up to\n//   about ~2.45V at the pin. After the 5:1 divider that means inputs up to\n//   roughly ~12V read accurately; above that the ADC starts to clip/saturate\n//   and the number on screen will plateau. The module itself can take 25V\n//   without damage, but the displayed voltage above ~12V is not trustworthy.\n//   For everyday batteries (single 9V, 18650, 12V SLA at rest ~12.6V) this\n//   is fine. Calibrate CAL against a multimeter inside the band you care about\n//   (e.g. 6-10V for 9V alkalines).\n\n#include <Wire.h>\n#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>\n#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>\n\n// ---- pins ----\nconst int PIN_VSENSE = 34;   // ADC1_CH6, input-only, safe for analog reads\nconst int PIN_LED_GREEN = 25;\nconst int PIN_LED_RED   = 26;\n\n// ---- OLED ----\n#define OLED_WIDTH   128\n#define OLED_HEIGHT  64\n#define OLED_ADDR    0x3C\nAdafruit_SSD1306 oled(OLED_WIDTH, OLED_HEIGHT, &Wire, -1);\n\n// ---- voltage divider math ----\n// The module has a built-in 30k + 7.5k divider, ratio 5.0 (Vin = Vout * 5).\n// We feed Vout into ADC pin 34. With ADC_11db attenuation the ADC nominally\n// spans 0-3.3V, but in practice only ~0.15V to ~2.45V is linear. The CAL\n// fudge factor lets Andy nudge the displayed number to match a multimeter\n// in the voltage band he actually cares about.\nconst float ADC_REF_VOLTS  = 3.30f;\nconst float ADC_MAX_COUNTS = 4095.0f;     // 12-bit\nconst float DIVIDER_RATIO  = 5.00f;       // module's printed ratio\nconst float CAL            = 1.00f;       // tweak after measuring with a meter\n\n// Highest input voltage the ADC can still read linearly (with the 5:1 divider).\nconst float LINEAR_MAX_VIN = 12.0f;\n\n// \"Healthy\" threshold - example tuned for a 9V alkaline battery.\n// 9V fresh ~ 9.5V, \"tired\" by ~7.0V, dead by ~5.5V.\nconst float HEALTHY_VOLTS = 7.00f;\n\n// Smoothing: average N samples to calm the ADC jitter.\nconst int ","description":"**What it is**: Touch the little board's two screws to any battery's plus and minus ends. The tiny screen instantly shows the volts, a bar graph, and one big word: HEALTHY, LOW, or DEAD. A green LED or a red LED lights up too, so you know the answer from across the room.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You become the battery doctor of the house. Go hunting through every drawer, remote, and toy, zap each battery with the probes, and sort them into a \"still good\" pile and a \"throw away\" pile - your parents will actually thank you.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- Voltage Sensor Module (0-25V divider) - the star: a tiny board with two resistors (30k + 7.5k) that shrinks a DC voltage down to something the ESP32's 3.3V ADC can survive. The module is rated up to 25V at the input, but as you'll see in Talking points, the ESP32's ADC only stays linear up to about 12V of input. Above that the screen number goes wonky.\n- ESP32 NodeMCU-32S - reads the divider's output on GPIO 34 and drives the screen\n- 0.96\" OLED Display (I2C) - shows the live number, a bar graph, and the word HEALTHY / LOW / DEAD\n- 1x green LED with a 220R limiter and 1x red LED with a 330R limiter - instant \"go / no-go\" feedback even before you look at the screen. Green gets the smaller resistor on purpose - green LEDs need a higher forward voltage than red, so at 3.3V drive a 330R would leave it visibly dim.\n- Breadboard + jumpers - no soldering\n\nA 9V battery (or any low-voltage DC source up to ~12V you want to measure) is the **device under test**. You touch its terminals to the voltage module's input. The whole project itself is powered through the ESP32's USB cable. The voltage module is a passive resistor pair, so it draws no power of its own and does not need a VCC connection.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe voltage module has two sides:\n\n- **Screw-terminal side (input):** the thing you measure. The screws are labelled `VCC` (positive) and `GND` (negative). Andy connects the battery being tested here.\n- **Pin-header side (output):** three pins labelled `S` (signal, the divided-down voltage), `+` (unused, leave alone), and `-` (ground). The `-` pin **must share ground with the ESP32** or the reading is meaningless.\n\nSo the wiring story is: battery `+` and `-` go into the screw terminals. `S` goes to ESP32 GPIO 34. The module's `-` pin goes to ESP32 GND. Same-ground is the rule that makes the whole thing work.\n\nOLED is normal I2C: VCC to 3.3V (most 0.96\" I2C OLEDs have an onboard regulator and accept 3.3-5V; the ESP32's 3.3V rail is fine), GND to GND, SDA to GPIO 21, SCL to GPIO 22.\n\nThe green LED gets a 220R resistor in series with its anode (long leg). The red LED gets a 330R. Cathodes both to GND.\n\n**Do not** feed more than 25V into the module, and **do not** ever connect AC, mains, or the wall. This project is for batteries and bench DC supplies only.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- The voltage module is just a **voltage divider**: two resistors in series. The \"S\" pin sits at the junction and carries a fraction of the input vol","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Voltage Sensor Module (0-25V divider)","qty":1}]},{"id":58,"name":"Raindrop Reporter - live wetness on OLED","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"Powered from a USB-C cable plugged into the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S. The board's 3V3 pin supplies the raindrop sensor module, the OLED, and the LED.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// Raindrop Reporter\n// Reads the Raindrop Sensor's analog output, classifies it as\n// Dry / Misty / Wet / Soaked, shows live numbers on the OLED,\n// and lights a blue LED while rain is detected.\n//\n// Wiring (matches the diagram):\n//   Raindrop module  VCC -> ESP32 3V3\n//   Raindrop module  GND -> ESP32 GND\n//   Raindrop module  AO  -> ESP32 GPIO34 (ADC1, input-only - safe)\n//   OLED  VCC -> 3V3, GND -> GND, SDA -> GPIO21, SCL -> GPIO22\n//   LED anode -> 330R -> GPIO5,  LED cathode -> GND\n//\n// Quirk: the raindrop module is \"wet = low value, dry = high value\".\n// Dry air typically reads near 4095; water shorts the comb traces and\n// pulls the reading down toward 0.\n\n#include <Wire.h>\n#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>\n#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>\n\n#define SCREEN_WIDTH 128\n#define SCREEN_HEIGHT 64\n#define OLED_RESET   -1\n#define OLED_ADDR    0x3C\n\nAdafruit_SSD1306 display(SCREEN_WIDTH, SCREEN_HEIGHT, &Wire, OLED_RESET);\n\nconst int RAIN_PIN = 34;   // analog input from sensor AO\nconst int LED_PIN  = 5;    // blue LED via 330R\n\n// Thresholds tuned for a 3.3V supply and a 12-bit ADC (0..4095).\n// Higher number = drier. Tweak these once you watch the live values.\nconst int SOAKED_BELOW = 1200;\nconst int WET_BELOW    = 2400;\nconst int MISTY_BELOW  = 3600;\n// Anything >= MISTY_BELOW counts as Dry.\n\nconst char* classify(int v) {\n  if (v < SOAKED_BELOW) return \"SOAKED\";\n  if (v < WET_BELOW)    return \"WET\";\n  if (v < MISTY_BELOW)  return \"MISTY\";\n  return \"DRY\";\n}\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n  pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);\n  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);\n\n  Wire.begin(21, 22);  // SDA, SCL\n  if (!display.begin(SSD1306_SWITCHCAPVCC, OLED_ADDR)) {\n    Serial.println(\"OLED not found - check wiring / address\");\n    while (true) { delay(1000); }\n  }\n\n  display.clearDisplay();\n  display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);\n  display.setTextSize(1);\n  display.setCursor(0, 0);\n  display.println(\"Raindrop Reporter\");\n  display.println(\"warming up...\");\n  display.display();\n  delay(800);\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  // Average a few samples so a single noisy reading doesn't make\n  // the display flicker between states.\n  long sum = 0;\n  const int N = 8;\n  for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {\n    sum += analogRead(RAIN_PIN);\n    delay(5);\n  }\n  int raw = sum / N;\n\n  const char* state = classify(raw);\n  bool isRaining = (raw < MISTY_BELOW);\n  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, isRaining ? HIGH : LOW);\n\n  // Percent \"wetness\" for the bar - invert so wet = full bar.\n  int wetness = map(raw, 0, 4095, 100, 0);\n  if (we","description":"**What it is**: Flick water onto a little comb-shaped board and the tiny screen instantly changes from DRY to MISTY to WET to SOAKED, with a blue LED that glows brighter the wetter it gets. There's a wiggling bar on the screen too, so you can watch it climb in real time as the water sits there.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It's a weather detector you build yourself - leave it on the windowsill and you'll know the second rain hits, before anyone else in the house. Try licking your finger and touching the board, or breathing on it really close - you'll see how sensitive it is and feel like a mad scientist running tests.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x Raindrop Sensor (the comb board + its little LM393 amplifier board)\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S\n- 1x 0.96\" OLED display (I2C, address 0x3C)\n- 1x Blue 5mm LED\n- 1x 330R resistor (current limit for the LED)\n- 1x Breadboard\n- Jumper wires (a mix of M-M and F-M)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe raindrop sensor comes in two pieces joined by a 2-wire lead: a flat comb-trace board (the bit that gets wet) and a small amplifier board with the 4-pin header. The amplifier board is where everything plugs in.\n\n- Sensor `VCC` -> ESP32 `3V3`. The module is happy at 3.3V; do NOT use 5V here even though the silkscreen allows it - the analog output would swing past what the ESP32 ADC tolerates.\n- Sensor `GND` -> ESP32 `GND`.\n- Sensor `AO` (analog out) -> ESP32 `GPIO34`. GPIO34 is input-only and lives on ADC1, which keeps working while WiFi is on - a small but important detail on the ESP32.\n- Ignore the `DO` (digital out) pin. The on-board pot only sets the threshold for `DO`; we read the analog signal directly so we don't have to twist the trimmer.\n- OLED: `VCC -> 3V3`, `GND -> GND`, `SDA -> GPIO21`, `SCL -> GPIO22`. These are the ESP32's default I2C pins.\n- Blue LED: long leg (anode) goes through the 330R resistor to `GPIO5`, short leg (cathode) to `GND`.\n\nThe raindrop sensor is \"wet = low value, dry = high value\". That's because the comb traces work as a variable resistor: water bridges them, the resistance drops, and the on-board divider pulls AO toward ground.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- Why GPIO34? Show Sandy the ESP32 pinout. ADC2 pins go quiet whenever WiFi turns on - we picked an ADC1 pin so we don't get bitten by that later.\n- The `classify()` thresholds were guessed. The real ones come from watching the Serial Monitor while flicking water on the board: write down the value when dry, when finger-damp, when a drop is sitting on it, and when it's fully submerged. Edit the four constants at the top to match.\n- Averaging 8 samples is a tiny but useful trick - one stray reading would make the display jitter between MISTY and WET every quarter second otherwise.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Add a buzzer that beeps when state crosses into SOAKED - then it's a basement-flood alarm.\n- Log the state changes with `millis()` over WiFi to the (future) Orange Pi MQTT broker, so you've got a record of when it rained.\n- Use the `DO` pin instead of `AO` and ski","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":6},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":7},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Blue","qty":1},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Raindrop Sensor","qty":1}]},{"id":59,"name":"Tub Filler Traffic Light","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"Arduino Uno powered from a laptop USB cable. The Uno's onboard 5V rail then feeds the water sensor, the LCD, and the LED resistors.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// Tub Filler Traffic Light\n// Arduino Uno R3 + Water Level Sensor + LCD 1602 I2C + 3 LEDs\n//\n// Dip the sensor into a glass of water. The LCD shows a live bar graph\n// and a percent. Three LEDs light up in sequence as the water rises:\n//   - Red  (D5): even a drop touches the combs\n//   - Yellow (D6): about half-full\n//   - Green (D7): full\n//\n// Wiring:\n//   Sensor:  + -> 5V,  - -> GND,  S -> A0   (1M pull-down from A0 to GND)\n//   LCD I2C: VCC -> 5V, GND -> GND, SDA -> A4, SCL -> A5\n//   LEDs:    D5/D6/D7 -> 330R -> LED anode; cathode -> GND\n//\n// Library to install once via Arduino IDE Library Manager:\n//   \"LiquidCrystal I2C\" by Frank de Brabander (or any compatible fork).\n\n#include <Wire.h>\n#include <LiquidCrystal_I2C.h>\n\n// Most cheap I2C backpacks live at 0x27. If yours shows a blank screen,\n// try 0x3F.\nLiquidCrystal_I2C lcd(0x27, 16, 2);\n\nconst uint8_t SENSOR_PIN = A0;\nconst uint8_t RED_PIN    = 5;\nconst uint8_t YELLOW_PIN = 6;\nconst uint8_t GREEN_PIN  = 7;\n\n// Calibration. DRY_MIN should be roughly what you see in the Serial\n// Monitor when the sensor is bone-dry (with the 1M pull-down it should\n// be near 0). WET_MAX is what you see when the comb is fully submerged\n// in tap water - typically 500-700. Re-tune both once you've watched\n// real readings come in.\nconst int DRY_MIN = 0;\nconst int WET_MAX = 600;\n\n// Thresholds expressed as percent of full.\nconst int RED_AT    = 5;   // any droplet at all\nconst int YELLOW_AT = 40;  // about half\nconst int GREEN_AT  = 80;  // basically full\n\n// Custom characters that let us draw a smooth 16-cell bar graph using\n// LCD rows. Each char fills 1, 2, 3, 4, or 5 vertical bars of one cell.\nbyte bar1[8] = {0x10,0x10,0x10,0x10,0x10,0x10,0x10,0x10};\nbyte bar2[8] = {0x18,0x18,0x18,0x18,0x18,0x18,0x18,0x18};\nbyte bar3[8] = {0x1C,0x1C,0x1C,0x1C,0x1C,0x1C,0x1C,0x1C};\nbyte bar4[8] = {0x1E,0x1E,0x1E,0x1E,0x1E,0x1E,0x1E,0x1E};\nbyte bar5[8] = {0x1F,0x1F,0x1F,0x1F,0x1F,0x1F,0x1F,0x1F};\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n\n  pinMode(RED_PIN, OUTPUT);\n  pinMode(YELLOW_PIN, OUTPUT);\n  pinMode(GREEN_PIN, OUTPUT);\n  digitalWrite(RED_PIN, LOW);\n  digitalWrite(YELLOW_PIN, LOW);\n  digitalWrite(GREEN_PIN, LOW);\n\n  lcd.init();\n  lcd.backlight();\n  lcd.createChar(1, bar1);\n  lcd.createChar(2, bar2);\n  lcd.createChar(3, bar3);\n  lcd.createChar(4, bar4);\n  lcd.createChar(5, bar5);\n\n  lcd.setCursor(0, 0);\n  lcd.print(\"Tub Filler\");\n  lcd.setCursor(0, 1);\n  lcd.print(\"Dip the sensor..\");\n  delay(1500);\n  lcd.clear();\n}\n\n// Smooth a","description":"**What it is**: Dip a little probe into a glass of water. A screen shows a bar graph filling up, plus a percent. Three LEDs glow like a stoplight - red when it's nearly empty, yellow when it's getting close, green when the \"tub\" is full.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It reacts instantly. Lift the probe out and the bar drops; push it back in and the green light pops on. It feels like a real gadget you'd see stuck to the side of a bathtub or a fish tank, and you built it.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- Arduino Uno R3 (the brain - reads the sensor, drives the LCD and LEDs)\n- Water Level Sensor (resistive comb - the protagonist; more water touching the combs = lower resistance = higher analog reading)\n- LCD 1602 with I2C Backpack (shows a live bar graph and a percent number)\n- 3 LEDs (red, yellow, green) - \"stoplight\" progress indicator\n- 3x 330R resistors (current-limiters, one per LED)\n- 1x 1M resistor (pull-down on the sensor's signal pin so the reading falls cleanly to 0 when dry)\n- Breadboard + a small handful of M-M and F-M jumper wires (one pack of each is plenty)\n- A glass of water (the \"tub\") to dip the sensor into\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n- The water sensor has three pins labelled `+`, `-`, `S`. `+` goes to Arduino 5V, `-` goes to Arduino GND, and `S` is the analog output that goes to Arduino A0.\n- The 1M pull-down resistor goes from A0 to GND. This is the \"why doesn't it read zero when dry?\" fix - without it, the floating input drifts and shows random numbers when there's no water.\n- LCD I2C backpack: only 4 wires. VCC -> 5V, GND -> GND, SDA -> A4, SCL -> A5. (On the Uno, A4 and A5 double as the I2C bus.)\n- LEDs: each LED's long leg (anode) goes through a 330R resistor to its Arduino pin. Short leg (cathode) goes to GND. Red on D5, Yellow on D6, Green on D7.\n- IMPORTANT - do not submerge the top half of the water sensor board. Only the gold combs should ever touch water. The exposed components above will corrode in days if they get wet.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- Why a \"resistive comb\" sensor? It's just two interleaved bare-copper tracks. Water between them conducts a tiny bit of current. More water covering the combs = more conductive paths in parallel = more current = bigger number from `analogRead`. It's the same trick the Soil Moisture Sensor uses, just shaped to read a vertical column instead of a flat patch.\n- Why the 1M pull-down? An Arduino analog input that isn't connected to anything acts like a tiny antenna - it picks up mains hum and reads random numbers. Tying it weakly to ground through 1M forces it to read 0 when the sensor is dry, but lets the sensor easily overpower it when water is present.\n- The LCD updates fast on purpose - you can dip the sensor in and out and watch the bar graph respond immediately. That tight feedback loop is what makes it fun.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Calibrate `WET_MAX` to your actual glass of water. Dip the sensor all the way and read the value over Serial - that's your real max. Beginner-typical values land between 500","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Yellow","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"LCD 1602 with I2C Backpack","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 1M (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":3},{"item_name":"Water Level Sensor","qty":1}]},{"id":60,"name":"NE555 Heartbeat Blinker (no code, all chip)","difficulty":"absolute beginner","power_supply":"5V from a USB phone charger via the USB-C pigtail (or borrowed from an Arduino Uno's 5V pin) feeds the breadboard rails; the NE555 sips a few mA.","code_language":"bash","code":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# NE555 Heartbeat Blinker - multimeter test procedure (no MCU, no code).\n#\n# Power: feed the breadboard rails with 5V from any USB phone charger\n# broken out via the USB-C pigtail, or from an Arduino Uno's 5V pin if\n# you have it nearby. Anywhere from 4.5V to 9V will work; the LED just\n# gets a little brighter at higher voltage.\n#\n# Set your multimeter to DC volts (20V range) and probe these points\n# while the LED is blinking. Black probe stays on the GND rail the\n# whole time.\n#\n# TEST POINT 1 - Pin 8 (Vcc) and Pin 4 (Reset)\n#   Expected: steady ~5V (matches your supply).\n#   If 0V: power not reaching the chip. Check the rail jumpers.\n#\n# TEST POINT 2 - Pin 1 (GND)\n#   Expected: 0V.\n#   If not 0V: GND jumper to the chip is broken.\n#\n# TEST POINT 3 - Pin 3 (Output, drives the LED)\n#   Expected: meter needle bounces between ~0V and ~3.5V at ~1 Hz.\n#   (The chip output doesn't quite swing to the full rail - that's\n#   normal for an NE555.)\n#\n# TEST POINT 4 - Pin 6 (Threshold) / Pin 2 (Trigger), tied together\n#   on top of the 47uF cap.\n#   Expected: a slow ramp between ~1.6V and ~3.3V (1/3 and 2/3 of Vcc).\n#   This IS the capacitor charging through R1+R2 and discharging through\n#   R2. Watching this voltage ramp up and down is the whole point of\n#   the project - it's the \"time constant\" you've heard about, made\n#   visible.\n#\n# TEST POINT 5 - Pin 7 (Discharge)\n#   Expected: pulses to ~0V briefly each cycle, otherwise floats with\n#   pin 6. This is the internal transistor that drains the cap.\n#\n# Period math (so you can predict the blink rate):\n#   T_high = 0.693 * (R1 + R2) * C   = 0.693 * 20kOhm * 47uF ~= 0.65 s\n#   T_low  = 0.693 *  R2      * C   = 0.693 * 10kOhm * 47uF ~= 0.33 s\n#   Total period ~= 0.98 s   ->   frequency ~= 1.02 Hz (heartbeat)\n#\n# Duty cycle is ~66% high. If you want it closer to 50% (even on/off),\n# make R1 much smaller than R2, or use the slightly fancier \"diode\n# trick\" variant - that's a \"things to change\" item below.\necho \"Probe the test points listed above while the LED blinks.\"\n","description":"**What it is**: Plug it in. A red LED blinks once per second, steady and calm, like a tiny heartbeat sitting on your breadboard.\n\n**Why it's fun**: This is your first project that does something real with ZERO code. No laptop, no typing, no uploading - just a little black chip and a few parts, and it blinks forever the second power touches it.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x NE555 Timer IC (8-pin DIP - the famous chip, in production since 1972)\n- 1x LED 5mm Red (the heartbeat indicator)\n- 2x Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%) - R1 and R2, set the blink rate together with the cap\n- 1x Resistor 470R (1/8W 1%) - protects the LED from too much current\n- 1x Capacitor 47uF electrolytic - the \"memory\" element that takes time to fill and empty\n- 1x Breadboard\n- A few M-M jumper wires\n- A 5V source: a USB phone charger broken out with the USB-C pigtail, OR borrow 5V + GND from a powered-on Arduino Uno sitting next to it\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe NE555 has 8 pins. Hold the chip with the little notch (or dot) at the top - pin 1 is the top-left, then pins count down the left side (1,2,3,4), across the bottom, and back up the right side (5,6,7,8).\n\nPlace the chip across the breadboard's center gully so the left pins are on one side and the right pins are on the other - that's what the gully is FOR, so the two sides of the chip don't short to each other.\n\nConnections (every one of these is a short jumper or a component leg pushed into the right hole):\n\n- Pin 1 (GND) -> GND rail\n- Pin 2 (Trigger) -> wired straight to Pin 6\n- Pin 6 (Threshold) -> top of the 47uF cap (positive leg, the longer one)\n- The 47uF cap's other leg (negative, marked with the white stripe) -> GND rail\n- Pin 7 (Discharge) -> through R2 (10K) -> Pin 6 / Pin 2 / cap+\n- Pin 7 (Discharge) -> through R1 (10K) -> +5V rail\n- Pin 8 (Vcc) -> +5V rail\n- Pin 4 (Reset) -> +5V rail (must be high or the chip stays asleep)\n- Pin 5 (Control) -> leave it floating (a 10nF to GND is optional and we don't need it for a calm 1 Hz blink)\n- Pin 3 (Output) -> through 470R -> LED long leg (anode) -> LED short leg (cathode) -> GND rail\n\nThat is the whole circuit. Eight pins, four passive parts, one LED.\n\n## Talking points\n\nThe NE555 in astable mode is one of the most-built circuits in electronics history. Here is what is actually happening inside:\n\n1. **At power-on**, the cap is empty (0V). The chip's internal comparator sees that, sets its output (pin 3) HIGH, and turns off the \"discharge\" transistor on pin 7.\n2. **The cap charges** through R1 + R2 in series. The voltage on it rises slowly along an RC curve.\n3. **When the cap reaches 2/3 of Vcc** (~3.3V on a 5V supply), the upper comparator trips. Output goes LOW. Pin 7 becomes a short to ground.\n4. **The cap discharges** through R2 only (R1 is now disconnected from the cap by the discharge transistor). Voltage falls.\n5. **When the cap drops to 1/3 of Vcc** (~1.6V), the lower comparator trips. Output goes HIGH again. Pin 7 floats. Back to step 2.\n\nThe LED on pin 3 mirrors the output, so it ","allocations":[{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 47uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"NE555 Timer IC","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":2},{"item_name":"Resistor 470R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1}]},{"id":61,"name":"NE555 Touch-and-Hold Lamp (monostable one-shot)","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"5 V DC from any USB source (phone charger, laptop USB-A, or your TP4056 + 18650 pack) - the NE555 happily runs from 4.5-15 V but 5 V is the friendliest.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// NE555 Touch-and-Hold Lamp - REFERENCE-ONLY pulse-width meter\n//\n// IMPORTANT: This sketch is NOT part of the build. The main project is\n// PURE ANALOG - the NE555, button, resistor, cap, and LED do everything\n// by themselves with no microcontroller. Power the breadboard from a 5V\n// USB supply, press the button, and the LED lights for ~11 seconds. Done.\n//\n// This file is here ONLY if you later want to measure the actual pulse\n// width and compare it to the theoretical T = 1.1 * R * C value.\n//\n// To run this OPTIONAL test you would need (NOT included in the allocations\n// for this project, but you already have them in the kit):\n//   - 1x Arduino Uno R3\n//   - 1x extra Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (you have qty=2, so this is fine)\n//   - A few Jumper Wire F-M (for jumping from Arduino headers to a\n//     breadboard rail) - you have a 20pc pack already.\n//\n// Wiring for the optional measurement:\n//   - NE555 pin 3 (OUT)  -> Arduino D2\n//   - NE555 GND rail     -> Arduino GND (shared ground is critical)\n//\n// Then open Serial Monitor at 115200.\n//\n// Theoretical pulse for R=100K, C=100uF:\n//   T = 1.1 * 100,000 ohms * 0.000100 F = 11.0 seconds\n//\n// Real-world electrolytics drift +/- 20%, so 8.8s to 13.2s is normal.\n\nconst int NE555_OUTPUT_PIN = 2;  // Connect to NE555 pin 3 (OUT)\nunsigned long pulseStartMs = 0;\nbool lastState = LOW;\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n  pinMode(NE555_OUTPUT_PIN, INPUT);\n  Serial.println(F(\"NE555 monostable pulse-width meter\"));\n  Serial.println(F(\"Theoretical T = 1.1 * R * C\"));\n  Serial.println(F(\"R=100K, C=100uF -> expected ~11.0 seconds\"));\n  Serial.println(F(\"Press the button on the NE555 breadboard...\"));\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  bool now = digitalRead(NE555_OUTPUT_PIN);\n\n  if (now == HIGH && lastState == LOW) {\n    // Rising edge - timing pulse just started\n    pulseStartMs = millis();\n    Serial.println(F(\">>> Pulse started (LED ON)\"));\n  }\n  if (now == LOW && lastState == HIGH) {\n    // Falling edge - pulse ended, report measurement\n    unsigned long durationMs = millis() - pulseStartMs;\n    Serial.print(F(\"<<< Pulse ended. Measured duration: \"));\n    Serial.print(durationMs / 1000.0, 2);\n    Serial.println(F(\" seconds\"));\n    Serial.println(F(\"---\"));\n  }\n  lastState = now;\n}\n","description":"**What it is**: Tap the button once and the yellow LED snaps on for about 11 seconds, then shuts off by itself. You can let go right away - the light keeps glowing on its own little timer.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It is like a tiny bathroom light that turns itself off, except YOU built it from a chip and a handful of parts. Race a stopwatch against it, or dare your dad to guess exactly when the LED will blink off.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x NE555 Timer IC (the brain - in monostable / one-shot mode this time)\n- 1x Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x5mm (white pin) - the trigger\n- 1x LED 5mm Yellow (lights up for the timed duration)\n- 1x Resistor 470R - in series with the LED, limits current so the LED does not burn out\n- 1x Resistor 100K - the \"R\" in the timing equation T = 1.1 * R * C\n- 1x Capacitor 100uF electrolytic - the \"C\" in the same equation\n- 1x Resistor 10K - pull-up so the trigger pin idles HIGH until the button presses it LOW\n- 1x Capacitor 1uF 50V - decoupling cap across the 555's power pins, keeps the chip from glitching\n- 1x Breadboard\n- Jumper wires (M-M)\n\nPower: 5 V from a USB cable plugged into any USB power source (phone charger, laptop USB-A, or the TP4056 + 18650 setup you already have). The 555 happily runs anywhere from 4.5 V to 15 V, but 5 V is the friendliest for a beginner project.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nNE555 pinout (looking at the chip from the top with the notch / dot at the LEFT):\n\n```\n            +----------+\n   GND  1 --|.       8 |-- Vcc (+5V)\n   TRIG 2 --|        7 |-- DISCHARGE\n   OUT  3 --|        6 |-- THRESHOLD\n   RESET 4 -|        5 |-- CONTROL\n            +----------+\n```\n\nConnections:\n\n1. **Pin 1 (GND)** to the breadboard's blue/GND rail.\n2. **Pin 8 (Vcc)** to the red/+5V rail. Also put the 1uF decoupling cap directly across pins 1 and 8 (negative lead to GND).\n3. **Pins 6 and 7 tied together** at one junction. That same junction connects to the **bottom** of the 100K resistor and to the **positive (+) lead of the 100uF cap**. The **top** of the 100K resistor goes to **+5V**. The negative lead of the cap goes to GND. (So pins 6, 7, the cap-plus, and one end of the 100K are all the same node.)\n4. Watch capacitor polarity - the white stripe / short leg is the negative side and goes to GND.\n5. **Pin 2 (TRIG)** connects to the 10K pull-up resistor, other end of which goes to +5V. So pin 2 sits at +5V by default.\n6. **One side of the push-button** connects to pin 2; **the other side** connects to GND. Pressing the button briefly pulls pin 2 to GND - that is the \"trigger\".\n7. **Pin 4 (RESET)** to +5V (so the chip never gets reset accidentally - leaving this floating causes weird behavior).\n8. **Pin 5 (CONTROL)** can be left unconnected for now (some textbooks add a 10nF cap here but it is not required).\n9. **Pin 3 (OUT)** to the **long leg (anode)** of the yellow LED. **Short leg (cathode)** of LED to the 470R resistor. Other end of 470R to GND.\n\nMath sanity check: T = 1.1 * R * C = 1.1 * 100,000 ohms * 0.000100 F = **11.0 seconds**.","allocations":[{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 1uF 50V (electrolytic)","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Yellow","qty":1},{"item_name":"NE555 Timer IC","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 100K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 470R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x5mm (white pin)","qty":1}]},{"id":62,"name":"Bump Buzzer (NE555 + SW-420)","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"5V from an Arduino Uno's USB rail jumpered to the breadboard +5V and GND rails (the Uno acts as a USB-to-5V converter; no firmware needs to run on it for the alarm to work).","code_language":"cpp","code":"// SW-420 Bump Buzzer - OPTIONAL test helper sketch\n//\n// This project is PURELY ANALOG once built. The NE555 does the timing,\n// the active piezo buzzer self-oscillates from DC, the SW-420 does the\n// sensing. No MCU is needed to RUN it. The Arduino Uno is wired in only\n// to deliver a regulated 5V from its USB rail to the breadboard.\n//\n// This sketch is OPTIONAL. Load it on the Uno BEFORE building the\n// NE555 stage, to confirm the SW-420 is producing clean pulses and to\n// help you set the blue threshold pot on the SW-420. After that, lift\n// D2 off the SW-420 DO line and use the Uno purely as a 5V source.\n//\n// Wiring for this test only:\n//   SW-420 VCC -> Uno 5V\n//   SW-420 GND -> Uno GND\n//   SW-420 DO  -> Uno D2\n\nconst int SW420_DO_PIN = 2;\nunsigned long lastShakeMs = 0;\nint shakeCount = 0;\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n  pinMode(SW420_DO_PIN, INPUT);\n  Serial.println(F(\"SW-420 sanity check - shake the desk.\"));\n  Serial.println(F(\"DO is active-LOW: idle reads HIGH, shake briefly pulls LOW.\"));\n  Serial.println(F(\"Turn the blue pot until typing nearby does NOT trigger but a desk-tap does.\"));\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  int state = digitalRead(SW420_DO_PIN);\n  unsigned long now = millis();\n\n  // 50ms debounce so one shake doesn't get counted ten times\n  if (state == LOW && (now - lastShakeMs) > 50) {\n    shakeCount++;\n    lastShakeMs = now;\n    Serial.print(F(\"SHAKE #\"));\n    Serial.print(shakeCount);\n    Serial.print(F(\" at t=\"));\n    Serial.print(now);\n    Serial.println(F(\" ms\"));\n  }\n\n  // Periodic heartbeat so you know the sketch is alive\n  static unsigned long lastTickMs = 0;\n  if (now - lastTickMs > 2000) {\n    lastTickMs = now;\n    Serial.print(F(\"[idle] DO=\"));\n    Serial.print(state);\n    Serial.print(F(\" total shakes=\"));\n    Serial.println(shakeCount);\n  }\n}\n","description":"**What it is**: Bump the table and a red light snaps on while a buzzer screams for about ten seconds, then it goes quiet and waits for the next bump. No code, no buttons - just touch the desk and it freaks out.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Hide it under your little brother's chair, or tape it to the cookie jar, and you get an instant booby trap that catches anyone who sneaks up. The best part is you can tune how jumpy it is with a tiny screwdriver - too touchy and a footstep sets it off, too chill and you can shake the table without a peep.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x NE555 Timer IC - the brain, configured as a monostable one-shot\n- 1x SW-420 Vibration Switch Module - the trigger\n- 1x 220K resistor - NE555 timing R\n- 1x 47uF electrolytic - NE555 timing C\n- 1x 10K resistor - pull-up on NE555 TRIG (pin 2)\n- 1x 330R resistor - LED current limiter\n- 1x 100uF electrolytic - rail decoupling next to the NE555\n- 1x Red 5mm LED - visual \"ALARM ACTIVE\" indicator\n- 1x Active piezo buzzer (from the spare-parts drawer - the kind with a sticker that says \"5V\" on the back; it self-oscillates when you give it DC). If you do not have one, swap it for any item you can drive with a digital HIGH and the LED-and-330R alone is still a quiet test alarm.\n- Breadboard, jumper wires\n- Arduino Uno - used ONLY as a USB-powered 5V source (no firmware running the alarm)\n\nNote: this design deliberately does NOT use the LM386 audio amplifier. Earlier drafts paired the LM386 with a passive 8 ohm speaker, which is electrically wrong here: the NE555 output is a static DC level while the timer is running, so a coupling cap into the LM386 would only produce a click at the start and a click at the end - not a continuous tone. An active piezo buzzer solves this in one part because it has its own internal oscillator and only needs DC.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe NE555 is wired as a classic monostable (\"one-shot\"):\n\n- Pin 1 (GND) -> ground rail\n- Pin 8 (VCC) -> +5V rail\n- Pin 4 (RESET) -> +5V (tie HIGH so it is always armed)\n- Pin 2 (TRIG) -> pulled up to +5V through the 10K resistor, AND connected DIRECTLY to the SW-420 DO output (no series resistor in this line - a series resistor here would form a divider with the pull-up and TRIG would never reach the 1/3 Vcc threshold needed to fire)\n- Pin 6 (THRES) joined to pin 7 (DISCH), and both connected to the top of the 47uF timing capacitor. The bottom of the 47uF goes to GND.\n- Pin 7 (DISCH) also connects through the 220K resistor up to +5V. This is the R that, with the 47uF C, sets the pulse length: T = 1.1 * R * C = 1.1 * 220000 * 0.000047 = ~11.4 seconds in theory. In practice 47uF electrolytics carry +/-20% tolerance, so expect anything from ~9 to ~14 seconds. Treat the headline \"10 seconds\" as a ballpark.\n- Pin 3 (OUT) drives two things in parallel:\n  1. The red LED through the 330R resistor to GND (visual indicator)\n  2. The active piezo buzzer (+ pin to NE555 pin 3, - pin to GND)\n- A 100uF bulk decoupling cap sits between +5V and GND right next to pin 8. ","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":4},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":10},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 47uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"NE555 Timer IC","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 220K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"SW-420 Vibration Switch Module","qty":1}]},{"id":63,"name":"LM358 Photodiode Preamp Lux Meter","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"USB cable from laptop into the Arduino Uno; the Uno's 5V rail powers the LM358, the KY-018 module, and the OLED.","code_language":"cpp","code":"#include <Wire.h>\n#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>\n#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>\n\n// --- OLED ---\n#define SCREEN_WIDTH 128\n#define SCREEN_HEIGHT 64\n#define OLED_RESET   -1\n#define OLED_ADDR    0x3C\nAdafruit_SSD1306 display(SCREEN_WIDTH, SCREEN_HEIGHT, &Wire, OLED_RESET);\n\n// --- Signal in ---\n// LM358 output (op-amp A, pin 1) feeds A0.\n// Op-amp B output (pin 7) is the 2.5V virtual ground that is the reference\n// for the transimpedance stage; we also sample it on A1 so we can subtract\n// it in software and report a signed photocurrent.\nconst uint8_t PIN_SIGNAL = A0;\nconst uint8_t PIN_VREF   = A1;\n\n// --- Circuit constants ---\n// Feedback resistor Rf in ohms. With Rf = 47,000 the transimpedance gain\n// is ~47 mV per microamp of photocurrent (Vout = -Iphoto * Rf, measured\n// relative to the virtual-ground reference).\n//\n// IMPORTANT: an LDR is a much \"leakier\" sensor than a real photodiode -\n// it can pass tens to hundreds of microamps in normal indoor light. With\n// the original 1M feedback resistor the op-amp would slam straight to\n// the negative rail and you would see a pinned reading. 47K keeps the\n// stage living happily inside the LM358's swing range. If you ever swap\n// the LDR for an actual photodiode (BPW34 etc.), THEN you can crank Rf\n// back up to 1M-10M to recover sensitivity.\nconst float RF_OHMS = 47.0e3f;\n\n// Arduino Uno ADC reference (default = AVcc ~ 5.0V) and resolution.\nconst float ADC_VREF = 5.0f;\nconst int   ADC_MAX  = 1023;\n\n// Very rough conversion from photocurrent (uA) to lux. The KY-018's LDR is\n// nothing like a calibrated photodiode, so this is a learning estimate,\n// not a metrology-grade reading. Tune K_LUX_PER_UA empirically against a\n// known light source (phone lux-meter app works well enough).\nconst float K_LUX_PER_UA = 5.0f;\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n  delay(200);\n\n  if (!display.begin(SSD1306_SWITCHCAPVCC, OLED_ADDR)) {\n    Serial.println(F(\"OLED init failed\"));\n    while (true) { delay(1000); }\n  }\n  display.clearDisplay();\n  display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);\n  display.setTextSize(1);\n  display.setCursor(0, 0);\n  display.println(F(\"LM358 Lux Meter\"));\n  display.display();\n  delay(800);\n\n  // Print expected ranges so the beginner has something to check against.\n  Serial.println(F(\"Boot self-check:\"));\n  Serial.println(F(\"  Expect VREF 2.45 - 2.55 V\"));\n  Serial.println(F(\"  Expect dV ~ 0 mV when LDR is covered\"));\n  Serial.println(F(\"  Expect dV negative (Iph positive) when LDR is lit\"));\n  Serial.println(F(\"  If","description":"**What it is**: Point the little light sensor at a lamp, a window, or the inside of a closet. The tiny screen shows a number that goes UP when there's more light and DOWN when it gets dark.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Walk around the house hunting for the brightest and darkest spots like a light detective - under the couch, behind the curtains, inside the fridge when the door pops open. Cup your hands over the sensor and watch the number crash to almost zero, like you just turned off the sun.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL) - reads the amplifier output and drives the OLED\n- 1x LM358 Op-Amp (dual) - one half is the transimpedance amplifier, the other half generates a virtual ground\n- 1x KY-018 Photoresistor Module - the LDR is repurposed as a light-driven current source\n- 1x 0.96\" OLED Display (I2C) - shows the millivolts, microamps and estimated lux\n- 1x Resistor 47K (1/8W 1%) - feedback resistor Rf, sized so the LDR's mA-scale current does not saturate the op-amp (gain ~47 mV per uA)\n- 2x Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%) - equal pair forming the 2.5V virtual-ground divider\n- 1x Capacitor 1uF 50V (electrolytic) - feedback cap across Rf, rolls off noise and stops the stage oscillating (a 100nF ceramic film cap is a cleaner choice for this spot - the polar electrolytic will work but adds a little slow drift from leakage; swap if you have one in the spares drawer)\n- 1x Capacitor 10uF - decouples the virtual-ground node so it stays stiff\n- 1x Capacitor 100uF - bulk decoupling on the LM358 V+ pin\n- 1x Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points) - solderless\n- Jumper wires (M-M and F-M assortments)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n**Power.** Arduino Uno provides 5V and GND from the USB rail. The LM358 runs single-supply from those rails.\n\n**LM358 power pins (do not skip this).** The LM358 only has 8 pins and two of them are easy for a beginner to forget. Wire them first:\n- **Pin 4 -> GND rail**\n- **Pin 8 -> +5V rail**, with the 100uF cap from pin 8 straight to GND right at the chip body\n\nIf the chip has no power, nothing else in this build will do anything and you will chase a dead circuit for an hour. Confirm with a multimeter across pins 4 and 8 before going further.\n\n**Virtual ground (op-amp B = pins 5, 6, 7).** Two 10K resistors form a divider from 5V to GND, meeting at the non-inverting input (pin 5). Tie pin 6 to pin 7 to make a unity-gain buffer. Op-amp B now outputs a rock-solid 2.5V on pin 7, which we call VREF. Drop a 10uF cap from VREF to GND.\n\n**Transimpedance stage (op-amp A = pins 1, 2, 3).** Connect VREF to the non-inverting input (pin 3). The inverting input (pin 2) is the \"summing junction\". Put Rf = 47K from pin 2 to the output pin 1, with the 1uF cap (or a 100nF ceramic) in parallel with Rf.\n\n**Light input.** The KY-018 has VCC, GND, and AO (analog signal). We are NOT using its AO comparator path. Instead, power the module from 5V/GND as normal, and run a jumper from the module's AO pin (or directly off whichever LDR lead is easiest to reach on your ","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 10uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 1uF 50V (electrolytic)","qty":1},{"item_name":"LM358 Op-Amp (dual)","qty":1},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":2},{"item_name":"Resistor 47K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"KY-018 Photoresistor Module","qty":1}]},{"id":64,"name":"LM324 Quad Window Comparator 9V Battery Gauge","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"9V battery itself - the same battery being measured powers the gauge through a momentary TEST button and a L7805 regulator that creates a stable 5V reference rail.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// LM324 Quad Window Comparator Battery Gauge - Calibration Helper\n//\n// This is a PURE-ANALOG project - no MCU is required for it to work.\n// Use this sketch on an Arduino Uno ONLY if you want to log the battery\n// voltage while you verify the four LM324 thresholds trip at the right\n// points.\n//\n// WIRING for the helper:\n//   The gauge already has a 10K/10K battery divider (VSENSE node).\n//   Tap VSENSE on the gauge DIRECTLY into Uno A0. Do NOT build a second\n//   divider - that would double-divide V_batt to V_batt/4 and skew every\n//   reading. Also tie Uno GND to the gauge GND so the two share a ground.\n//\n// Expected trip points with the wiring as drawn (uniform 10K ladder\n// between +5V and GND -> taps at 4.00 / 3.00 / 2.00 / 1.00 V):\n//   Blue   off below V_batt = 8.0 V  (V_batt/2 < 4.00 V vs 4.00 V ref)\n//   Green  off below V_batt = 6.0 V  (V_batt/2 < 3.00 V vs 3.00 V ref)\n//   Yellow off below V_batt = 4.0 V  (V_batt/2 < 2.00 V vs 2.00 V ref)\n//   Red    off below V_batt = 2.0 V  (effectively always on while powered)\n//\n// Pin map (matches the wiring diagram):\n//   A0  <- VSENSE on the gauge (already = V_batt / 2)\n//\n// Andy's beginner-friendly debugging companion - not part of the gauge itself.\n\nconst int BATT_DIV_PIN = A0;     // reads V_batt / 2 directly from VSENSE\nconst float ADC_REF    = 5.0;    // Uno's analog reference (default 5V)\nconst float ADC_STEPS  = 1023.0; // 10-bit ADC\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n  Serial.println(F(\"LM324 battery gauge calibration helper\"));\n  Serial.println(F(\"Tap VSENSE on the gauge (NOT V_batt) into A0.\"));\n  Serial.println(F(\"Hold the TEST button on the gauge to power it up.\"));\n  Serial.println(F(\"Reading V_batt every 500 ms...\"));\n  Serial.println();\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  int raw = analogRead(BATT_DIV_PIN);\n  float v_half = (raw / ADC_STEPS) * ADC_REF;   // voltage at A0 = V_batt/2\n  float v_batt = v_half * 2.0;                  // un-divide\n\n  Serial.print(F(\"V_batt = \"));\n  Serial.print(v_batt, 2);\n  Serial.print(F(\" V   \"));\n\n  // Predict which LEDs SHOULD be lit, so you can compare to the real board.\n  Serial.print(F(\"[ \"));\n  Serial.print(v_batt > 8.0 ? F(\"BLU \")   : F(\"--- \"));\n  Serial.print(v_batt > 6.0 ? F(\"GRN \")   : F(\"--- \"));\n  Serial.print(v_batt > 4.0 ? F(\"YEL \")   : F(\"--- \"));\n  Serial.print(v_batt > 2.0 ? F(\"RED \")   : F(\"--- \"));\n  Serial.println(F(\"]\"));\n\n  delay(500);\n}\n","description":"**What it is**: Hold the button and a row of four LEDs lights up - blue, green, yellow, red - to show how much juice your 9V battery has left. As the battery gets weaker, the lights drop off one by one like a fuel gauge in a car.\n\n**Why it's fun**: No code, no screen, no computer brain - just a chip and some parts that magically \"decide\" things on their own. Test every old battery in the junk drawer and find out which ones still have life left and which ones are dead.\n\nA pure-analog, zero-firmware twin of the Pocket Battery Health Monitor - same job (tell me if this 9V battery is still good), no microcontroller, no OLED, no code. Just one quad op-amp doing four comparisons at once and four LEDs that drop off as the battery drains.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x LM324 quad op-amp (the brain - four comparators in one package)\n- 1x L7805 +5V linear regulator (creates a stable reference rail that does NOT sag as the battery drains)\n- 4x 5mm LEDs: blue, green, yellow, red (one per channel)\n- 6x 10K 1% resistors (4 for the reference ladder, 2 for the battery divider)\n- 4x 1K resistors (LED current limiters - 5V/1K = ~3 mA, plenty bright on modern LEDs)\n- 1x 100uF cap (input bulk smoothing for the 7805)\n- 1x 10uF cap (output bulk on the 7805 - borderline-OK on its own at this tiny load; in a \"real\" design you'd add a 0.1uF ceramic alongside)\n- 1x 1uF cap (sits right by the LM324's V+ pin - keeps it stable)\n- 1x small tactile push-button (TEST button - holding it powers the circuit so the gauge does not silently drain the very battery it's measuring)\n- 1x breadboard, jumper wires\n- 1x 9V battery (the thing being measured AND the power source - that's the fun part)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe 9V battery is BOTH the load-under-test and the power supply. That sounds like cheating but it's exactly how a fuel gauge in a car works - same circuit, same source.\n\n**Power path:** 9V battery (+) -> TEST button -> raw 9V rail (filtered by 100uF) -> L7805 IN. L7805 OUT = stable +5V rail (filtered by 10uF). The LM324's V+ pin (pin 4) is tied to the +5V rail, NOT the raw 9V. V- (pin 11) goes to battery GND. The LEDs are also anchored to the +5V rail through their 1K resistors, so the op-amp output only ever has to swing between 0 V and ~5 V - no awkward mixed-rail story.\n\n**Reference ladder (the four thresholds):** four 10K resistors stacked between +5V and GND make a uniform 4-step divider with three taps at 4.00 V, 3.00 V, and 2.00 V, plus the bottom-of-the-stack at 1.00 V. We use those four reference voltages (4.00 / 3.00 / 2.00 / 1.00) directly - no further \"tuning,\" because uniform 10K resistors only give uniform 1.00 V steps from a 5.00 V rail. Live with that, it works.\n\n**Battery sense:** a 10K/10K divider from the raw battery rail to GND gives V_batt/2 at its midpoint. So a fresh 9 V battery presents 4.5 V there, a tired 6 V battery presents 3.0 V. That single midpoint goes to ALL FOUR negative inputs of the LM324, tied together.\n\n**The trip points** (sense input = V_batt/2):\n","allocations":[{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF 16V (electrolytic)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 10uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 1uF 50V (electrolytic)","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Blue","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Yellow","qty":1},{"item_name":"LM324 Op-Amp (quad)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":6},{"item_name":"Resistor 1K (1/8W 1%)","qty":4},{"item_name":"Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x4.3mm (white pin)","qty":1},{"item_name":"L7805 Linear Regulator (+5V)","qty":1}]},{"id":65,"name":"No-MCU Dark Detector (LM358 Schmitt Trigger)","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"9 V wall supply into an L7805 linear regulator that makes the +5 V rail. 100 uF input cap (35 V), 100 uF output cap (25 V) - polarity matters, striped lead to GND on both. No microcontroller is involved.","code_language":"bash","code":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# LM358 No-MCU Dark Detector - multimeter bring-up procedure\n# This is a pure-analog circuit. There is no firmware to flash.\n# Power the board from a 9 V wall supply into the L7805 (NOT from a USB port).\n# Walk through these test points in order with a DMM set to DC volts.\n\nset -e\n\ncat <<'TESTS'\n==================================================================\n PRE-FLIGHT (power OFF, board un-powered)\n==================================================================\n 1. Confirm L7805 orientation. With the printed face toward you and\n    the tab pointing up: pin 1 = IN (9 V), pin 2 = GND, pin 3 = OUT\n    (+5 V). The tab is internally connected to GND.\n 2. ELECTROLYTIC CAP POLARITY (read twice, beginners blow these up):\n       - The 100uF 35V input cap: striped side / short lead -> GND.\n         Long lead -> L7805 pin 1 (9 V input).\n       - The 100uF 25V output cap: striped side / short lead -> GND.\n         Long lead -> +5 V rail.\n       Reversed electrolytics will pop, hiss, or vent. Check them\n       with the cap leaning away from your face before powering up.\n 3. Confirm LM358 orientation: notch / pin-1 dot top-left. Pin 4 =\n    GND, pin 8 = +5 V (Vcc). Channel A (pins 1/2/3) is the Schmitt\n    comparator. Channel B (pins 5/6/7) drives the indicator LED.\n 4. Confirm KY-018: '-' to GND rail, 'middle' to +5 V rail,\n    'S' (AO) to LM358 pin 2 (inverting input).\n 5. Confirm relay module: VCC -> +5 V, GND -> GND, IN <- 1K series\n    resistor <- LM358 pin 1. NO BC547 anywhere.\n 6. Confirm indicator LED: LM358 pin 7 -> 330R -> LED anode (long\n    lead) -> LED cathode (short lead, flat side) -> GND. LM358\n    channel B is wired as a buffer: pin 5 (IN+) tied to pin 1\n    (channel-A output), pin 6 (IN-) tied to pin 7 (its own output).\n\n==================================================================\n POWER ON. Apply 9 V to the L7805 input. First sweep: room bright\n (uncover the KY-018). Second sweep: cover the KY-018 fully.\n==================================================================\n\n TP1  +5 V rail (L7805 pin 3)  . . . . . . . . . expect 4.9 - 5.1 V\n TP2  GND rail  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . expect 0.00 V\n TP3  LM358 pin 8 (Vcc)  . . . . . . . . . . . expect same as TP1\n TP4  LM358 pin 4 (GND)  . . . . . . . . . . . expect 0.00 V\n\n TP5  KY-018 AO into pin 2 (the SENSE voltage)\n        BRIGHT room . . . . . . . . . . . . . . expect 2.5 - 4.5 V\n        COVERED (dark)  . . . . . . . . . . . . expect 0.1 - 0.6 V\n      If both rea","description":"**What it is**: Cover the little sensor with your hand. A red LED snaps on and you hear the relay click. Uncover it and a moment later it clicks off. No code, no Arduino - the board just knows it got dark.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Wire the relay to a desk lamp and you've built a thing that turns the light on by itself when the sun goes down. Wave your hand over the sensor at twilight and listen - a cheap circuit would chatter click-click-click, but yours clicks once and stays put, because you taught it to make up its mind.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x LM358 Op-Amp (dual) - channel A is the Schmitt comparator, channel B is a unity-gain buffer that drives the indicator LED\n- 1x KY-018 Photoresistor Module - the eye\n- 1x 1-channel Relay Module - the muscle (low-voltage DC only) - driven directly from the LM358 output through a 1K series resistor\n- 1x L7805 Linear Regulator (+5V) - TO-220, produces the +5 V rail from a 9 V wall supply\n- 1x Resistor 100K (1/8W 1%) - top of the threshold divider\n- 1x Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%) - bottom of the threshold divider\n- 1x Resistor 220K (1/8W 1%) - positive-feedback resistor (the project rests on this one - it sets the dead-band)\n- 1x Resistor 1K (1/8W 1%) - series resistor on the relay-module IN line\n- 1x Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%) - LED current limit\n- 1x LED 5mm Red - visible DARK indicator\n- 1x Capacitor 100uF 35V (electrolytic) - L7805 INPUT cap (9 V side)\n- 1x Capacitor 100uF 25V (electrolytic) - +5 V rail decoupling, absorbs the inrush surge when the relay coil energises\n- 1x Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm\n- Jumper wires (M-M and F-M, one pack each)\n\nThere is NO Arduino, no ESP32 and no microcontroller anywhere on the board. That's the whole pedagogical point - we earn the +5 V rail honestly with an L7805 and a 9 V supply.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nPower comes in from a 9 V wall supply into the L7805 (pin 1 = IN, pin 2 = GND, pin 3 = OUT, tab is GND). A 100 uF 35V electrolytic sits across the input pins and a 100 uF 25V electrolytic sits across the output pins. Polarity matters - the striped / short-lead side of each cap goes to GND. Reversed electrolytics will vent. Check before power-on.\n\nLM358 pinout (top view, dot top-left): 1=OUT_A, 2=IN-_A, 3=IN+_A, 4=GND, 5=IN+_B, 6=IN-_B, 7=OUT_B, 8=Vcc.\n\n- **Sense path:** KY-018 'AO' -> LM358 pin 2 (inverting input). Bright -> high voltage; dark -> low voltage.\n- **Threshold divider:** +5 V -> 100K -> node X -> 10K -> GND. Node X sits at 5 * 10/(100+10) = 0.4545 V nominal.\n- **The hysteresis:** node X also feeds LM358 pin 3. From pin 1 (channel-A output) a 220K resistor runs back to pin 3. THIS is the Schmitt trick - the output tugs the threshold up or down depending on which state it is in.\n- **Relay drive:** LM358 pin 1 -> 1K series -> relay module IN pin. The 1-ch relay module's input is a high-impedance optocoupler-style stage with its own onboard transistor and its IN-pin threshold sits around 2 V. The LM358 output swings ~0.6 V low to ~3.5 V high, which is comfortably outside that threshold in","allocations":[{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF 25V (electrolytic)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF 35V (electrolytic)","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"LM358 Op-Amp (dual)","qty":1},{"item_name":"1-channel Relay Module","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 100K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 1K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 220K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"KY-018 Photoresistor Module","qty":1},{"item_name":"L7805 Linear Regulator (+5V)","qty":1}]},{"id":66,"name":"LM386 Pocket Speaker Amp (matchbox half-watt)","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"5V from a USB-C phone charger or power bank via the USB-C pigtail. LM386 happily runs 4-12V so a 9V battery works too if you want it portable - just keep the pin 7 BYPASS cap in place at the higher voltage.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// LM386 Pocket Speaker Amp - bench test helper\n//\n// This sketch is OPTIONAL. The amp is a pure analog circuit and works\n// without any microcontroller. Use this only if you want to feed it a\n// test tone instead of music from a phone, to confirm the amp itself\n// is alive before plugging your phone in.\n//\n// Wire Arduino Uno pin 9 in place of the phone jack tip - keep the\n// existing 1uF cap and 10K resistor in the signal path. So the chain\n// is: Uno pin 9 -> existing 1uF -> existing 10K -> LM386 pin 3.\n// The 1uF cap is important: it blocks the DC offset from the Uno's\n// 5V logic pin, which would otherwise upset the LM386's input bias\n// and possibly latch the amp into a stuck state.\n//\n// Tie Arduino GND to the breadboard GND rail. Leave the amp powered\n// from its own 5V USB-C supply - do NOT power the LM386 from the\n// Uno's 5V pin (the Uno regulator can't supply the current spikes\n// the speaker draws and you'll get audible noise).\n//\n// Expected result: a clean 1 kHz square-wave beep from the speaker.\n\nconst int TONE_PIN = 9;\nconst int TONE_HZ  = 1000;   // 1 kHz - easy to hear, well inside speaker range\nconst int BEEP_MS  = 400;\nconst int GAP_MS   = 600;\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n  pinMode(TONE_PIN, OUTPUT);\n  Serial.println(\"LM386 bench test - 1 kHz beep every second\");\n  Serial.println(\"If you hear nothing:\");\n  Serial.println(\" 1. Check 5V is on LM386 pin 6 and GND on pin 4\");\n  Serial.println(\" 2. Check 220uF output cap polarity (+ to pin 5)\");\n  Serial.println(\" 3. Check the 10uF bypass cap is in place from pin 7 to GND\");\n  Serial.println(\" 4. Touch a finger to pin 3 - speaker should HUM loudly\");\n  Serial.println(\"    (your body picks up 50/60 Hz mains - proves gain works)\");\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  tone(TONE_PIN, TONE_HZ);\n  delay(BEEP_MS);\n  noTone(TONE_PIN);\n  delay(GAP_MS);\n}\n","description":"**What it is**: Plug your phone into the breadboard, hit play, and a tiny speaker the size of a coin blasts your music loud enough to fill the room. The whole amplifier fits in a matchbox.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You built a real boombox out of one black chip and a handful of parts, no batteries or apps required. Crank it up at the dinner table and watch everyone wonder where the song is coming from.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x LM386 Audio Power Amp (8-pin DIP)\n- 1x 1uF electrolytic - input DC blocker\n- 2x 10uF electrolytic - one is the gain-set cap between pins 1 and 8, one is the pin 7 BYPASS cap to GND\n- 1x 47uF electrolytic - supply bypass\n- 1x 100uF electrolytic - bulk reservoir on the 5V rail\n- 1x 220uF electrolytic - output DC blocker to speaker\n- 1x 10K resistor - series input attenuator (acts as a fixed volume control)\n- 1x breadboard\n- 1x USB-C female pigtail for 5V power in (any phone charger / power bank)\n- Hookup wires\n- *You supply:* a small 8-ohm speaker (anything from a dead toy or old set of earbuds will do), a 3.5mm headphone jack or a chopped aux cable, and the phone to play music from\n\n> The textbook LM386 circuit adds a 10R + 47nF ceramic snubber (a \"Zobel network\") from pin 5 to GND to damp ultrasonic ringing. We skip it because we don't stock 47nF ceramics, and on a short breadboard the LM386 is usually stable without it. If you hear a faint high-pitched whine after everything else works, that's why - the proper fix is to add the Zobel, not to substitute a 47uF electrolytic (a factor-of-1000 different value, which would short the output through the 10R resistor across the entire audio band and cook the chip).\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nLM386 pinout (notch at top):\n\n```\n    +-----+\n1 --|     |-- 8     1 = GAIN\n2 --|     |-- 7     2 = -IN (to GND)\n3 --|     |-- 6     3 = +IN  (audio from phone)\n4 --|     |-- 5     4 = GND\n    +-----+         5 = OUT\n                    6 = Vcc\n                    7 = BYPASS (10uF to GND - required at high gain)\n                    8 = GAIN\n```\n\nOn the breadboard:\n\n1. Drop the LM386 across the centre groove.\n2. **Power.** USB-C pigtail: red wire to the breadboard's `+` rail, black to the `-` rail. Wire LM386 pin 6 to `+`, pin 4 to `-`. Drop the 100uF (long leg to `+`) and the 47uF (long leg to pin 6) across the rails right next to the chip.\n3. **Gain = 200x.** This is the famous trick: a 10uF cap from pin 1 to pin 8 bypasses an internal 1.35K resistor and pushes the gain from the default 20x all the way up to 200x. Long leg of the 10uF goes to pin 1.\n4. **BYPASS cap (non-optional at high gain).** Second 10uF: long leg to pin 7, short leg to the `-` rail. Pin 7 is the LM386's internal reference-voltage pin. Without this cap to ground the chip hums audibly and is far more likely to break into oscillation. Every datasheet \"typical application\" circuit at gain=200 shows this cap - omitting it is the most common reason a beginner LM386 build sounds noisy.\n5. **Input.** Phone jack tip -> 1uF cap (orient the `+` leg towa","allocations":[{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 10uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":2},{"item_name":"Capacitor 1uF 50V (electrolytic)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 220uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 47uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1},{"item_name":"USB-C Female Chassis Connector (2P pigtail)","qty":1},{"item_name":"LM386 Audio Power Amp","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1}]},{"id":67,"name":"LM386 Telegraph-Style Code Practice Oscillator","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"Powered from an Arduino Uno used purely as a regulated 5V brick over USB (USB jack -> Uno's 5V/GND pins -> breadboard rails). No code is uploaded to the Uno and no GPIO is used.","code_language":"bash","code":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Telegraph-style code practice oscillator - bench-test guide.\n# No microcontroller. Use a multimeter (DMM) to confirm the circuit\n# before you start tapping. The whole thing should draw < 15 mA.\n# If you smell anything hot, pull power IMMEDIATELY.\n#\n# CRITICAL POLARITY CHECK (do this BEFORE plugging in 5V):\n#   - 10uF cap:  + (long leg) toward LM386 pin 3,\n#                stripe / short leg toward GND.\n#   - 100uF cap: + (long leg) toward LM386 pin 6 (Vcc),\n#                stripe / short leg toward GND.\n#\n# Reversed electrolytics can heat up, vent, or pop. Always eyeball\n# both stripes before powering up.\n#\n# Test points are labelled in the wiring_diagram. Probe with the\n# black DMM lead on GND and the red lead on each test point.\n\nset -e\n\ncat <<'TESTS'\n==============================================\nLM386 Telegraph-Style CPO - bench-test guide\n==============================================\n\nStep 0: Eyeball check, power OFF\n  - LM386 notch faces LEFT (pin 1 top-left, pin 8 top-right).\n  - 10uF cap: + leg on pin 3, stripe on GND rail.\n  - 100uF cap: + leg on pin 6 (Vcc side of the button), stripe on GND.\n  - Two 10K resistors form a divider from +5V to GND; their midpoint\n    goes to pin 2.\n  - 100K resistor links pin 5 (output) to pin 3.\n  - 470R + LED: pin 5 -> 470R -> LED anode (long leg) -> LED cathode\n    -> GND.\n\nStep 1: Power ON, button NOT pressed\n  Plug the USB cable into the Uno. Do not press the button yet.\n  TP1 (+5V rail)             -> expect  +4.9 to +5.1 V DC\n  TP6 (LM386 pin 6, Vcc)     -> expect  ~0 V DC (rises briefly if the\n                                100uF still holds charge from a\n                                previous run - wait ~30 s for it to\n                                drain through leakage, then re-probe)\n  TP5 (LM386 pin 2, -IN)     -> expect  ~2.5 V DC (half-supply, set by\n                                the 10K + 10K divider, present even\n                                with the chip unpowered)\n  LED                        -> dark\n\nStep 2: Press and HOLD the tactile button\n  TP6 (LM386 pin 6, Vcc)     -> expect  +4.8 to +5.0 V DC\n  TP5 (LM386 pin 2, -IN)     -> still   ~2.5 V DC\n  TP3 (LM386 pin 3, +IN)     -> expect  a slow sawtooth between\n                                roughly 1 V and 3 V, swinging once\n                                per second. A DMM in DC mode will\n                                bounce; AC mode shows ~0.5 V AC.\n  TP2 (LM386 pin 5, output)  -> expect  square wave between ~","description":"**What it is**: Press and hold the button and a red LED starts blinking, about once a second, like a tiny lighthouse on your breadboard. Let go and it goes dark. Tap out short and long presses and you are flashing real Morse code.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Sailors used to flash messages between ships at night with big lamps just like this, and now you have a pocket-sized version on your desk. Learn the dots and dashes for your name, then flash it across the room to your dad and see if he can read it back.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x LM386 audio power amp (the star - rewired as a slow relaxation oscillator that flashes an LED in Morse-code rhythm, NOT as an audio amp this time)\n- 1x Tactile push-button 3x6x5mm (the \"telegraph key\")\n- 1x 10uF electrolytic cap (the timing cap - this, together with the 100K feedback resistor, sets the flash rate)\n- 1x 100uF electrolytic cap (Vcc bypass - keeps the supply quiet)\n- 1x 100K resistor (the feedback resistor - the cap charges through this; with the 10uF it gives a roughly 1-second flash period)\n- 2x 10K resistors (a half-supply reference divider on pin 2)\n- 1x 470R resistor (current limit for the LED on the output)\n- 1x 5mm red LED (the visible \"telegraph lamp\")\n- 1x Breadboard\n- M-M jumper wires\n- 1x Arduino Uno (used ONLY as a regulated 5V/GND brick through its USB jack - no code uploaded, no GPIO touched)\n\nNote on the \"telegraph\" angle: the inventory has no speaker or buzzer, so this build is a *visible* code-practice oscillator - a blinking lamp instead of a beep. That's historically real too: in the 1800s the Royal Navy and the army practised Morse on Aldis lamps and heliographs (mirror-reflected sunlight). Same code, same rhythm, light instead of sound.\n\n## Safety first - read this before plugging in\n\nElectrolytic caps have a polarity stripe marked on the side. **Backwards = they can heat up, vent, and occasionally pop.** Both caps in this build are electrolytic. Before you connect 5V:\n\n- 10uF timing cap: **plus (long leg) toward LM386 pin 3, stripe (short leg) toward GND.**\n- 100uF supply cap: **plus (long leg) toward LM386 pin 6 (Vcc), stripe (short leg) toward GND.**\n\nEyeball both stripes BEFORE you plug the USB cable in. If you smell anything warm once power is on, unplug immediately.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nLM386 pinout (notch up, looking down at the chip):\n\n```\n        +---v---+\n   1 GAIN | 1   8 | GAIN  8\n   2 -IN  | 2   7 | BYPASS 7\n   3 +IN  | 3   6 | VCC    6\n   4 GND  | 4   5 | OUT    5\n        +-------+\n```\n\n1. Plant the LM386 across the breadboard's centre channel, notch to the LEFT. Pin 1 is top-left, pin 4 is bottom-left, pin 5 is bottom-right, pin 8 is top-right.\n2. **Power rails:** Uno's 5V pin to the breadboard's red rail, Uno's GND to the blue rail. The Uno is just a tidy regulated 5V source - we never upload anything.\n3. **The \"telegraph key\":** the tactile push-button breaks the 5V line BEFORE it reaches LM386 pin 6. One leg of the button to the +5V rail, the other leg to pin 6. Press = chip powered","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 10uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"LM386 Audio Power Amp","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 100K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":2},{"item_name":"Resistor 470R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x5mm (white pin)","qty":1}]},{"id":68,"name":"TDA2822D Stereo Earbud Amp","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"Single 9V PP3 battery via a snap clip feeds Vcc (pin 5) and GND (pin 4) of the TDA2822D, with a 100uF 16V bulk decoupling cap across the rails close to the chip.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// TDA2822D Stereo Earbud Amp - test-tone helper (optional)\n//\n// This project is a pure-analog circuit. The Arduino Uno is OPTIONAL and\n// is only used to verify stereo channel separation BEFORE you connect a\n// real audio source. Once you've confirmed left and right are not crossed,\n// disconnect the Uno and feed audio from a phone or laptop 3.5mm jack:\n//   TIP   = LEFT input cap (10uF -> pin 3 IN1+)\n//   RING  = RIGHT input cap (10uF -> pin 6 IN2+)\n//   SLEEVE= GND rail (battery negative)\n//\n// Test wiring (TEMPORARY, remove after verification):\n//   Uno D9  -> 10K -> LEFT-IN  node (junction at the 10uF input cap)\n//   Uno D10 -> 10K -> RIGHT-IN node\n//   Uno GND -> amp GND (battery negative)\n// The 10K series resistors attenuate the 5V square wave down to a safe\n// audio-level signal so you don't blast your ears or saturate the amp.\n\nconst int LEFT_PIN  = 9;   // OC1A\nconst int RIGHT_PIN = 10;  // OC1B\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n  pinMode(LEFT_PIN, OUTPUT);\n  pinMode(RIGHT_PIN, OUTPUT);\n  Serial.println(F(\"TDA2822D test: 440 Hz LEFT, 880 Hz RIGHT.\"));\n  Serial.println(F(\"Listen: low tone in LEFT bud, high tone in RIGHT bud.\"));\n  tone(LEFT_PIN, 440);\n  tone(RIGHT_PIN, 880);\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  // Swap tones every 2 seconds so you can confirm channels aren't crossed.\n  delay(2000);\n  noTone(LEFT_PIN); noTone(RIGHT_PIN);\n  tone(LEFT_PIN, 880);\n  tone(RIGHT_PIN, 440);\n  Serial.println(F(\"Swapped: high LEFT, low RIGHT.\"));\n  delay(2000);\n  noTone(LEFT_PIN); noTone(RIGHT_PIN);\n  tone(LEFT_PIN, 440);\n  tone(RIGHT_PIN, 880);\n  Serial.println(F(\"Normal: low LEFT, high RIGHT.\"));\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------\n// Multimeter checks (no Uno needed) - do these FIRST, before any\n// audio source is connected:\n//\n//   1. Power off, battery in: measure 9V across the bulk decoupling\n//      cap. Confirms battery polarity at the rails.\n//   2. Power on, no signal:\n//        - Pin 5 (Vcc) -> GND: ~9V.\n//        - Pin 4 (GND) -> GND: 0V (sanity check).\n//        - Pin 1 (OUT1) and pin 8 (OUT2) DC reading: ~Vcc/2, around\n//          4.5V. This is the mid-supply bias the chip applies so the\n//          AC signal can swing both up and down.\n//        - Pin 3 (IN1+) and pin 6 (IN2+): ~0V (held there through\n//          your audio source, or floating low with no source).\n//        - Pin 2 (IN1-) and pin 7 (IN2-): a small positive DC voltage\n//          (the chip's internal feedback divider sets this).\n//   3. The 470uF output","description":"**What it is**: Plug your phone into one end and your earbuds into the other. Hit play and the music comes out loud and clear in both ears, left and right, powered by a tiny 9V battery.\n\n**Why it's fun**: This is the first thing you build that turns into a real piece of audio gear you can actually use. Pull out a battery-powered headphone amp you made yourself at the kitchen table, plug in, and listen to your own music through it.\n\n## Parts list\n\nAllocations below are the canonical parts list. A few items are NOT in the kit inventory and you'll need to bring your own:\n\n- **Bring-your-own (not in kit inventory):**\n  - 9V PP3 battery with snap clip (the power source).\n  - 3.5mm stereo input jack or a sacrificial 3.5mm cable (tip = L, ring = R, sleeve = GND).\n  - A pair of earbuds (32 ohm typical). Do NOT plug in big 4 ohm bookshelf speakers - the TDA2822D is a small-signal chip.\n\n- **From the kit (see allocations):**\n  - 1x TDA2822D dual audio power amp.\n  - 1x breadboard.\n  - 1x 100uF 16V cap (bulk supply decoupling).\n  - 2x 10uF caps (input DC blocking).\n  - 2x 100uF 16V caps (AC bypass at IN-).\n  - 2x 470uF 16V caps (output DC blocking).\n  - 2x 4.7R resistors (output series damping).\n  - 1x Arduino Uno + 2x 10K resistors - ONLY for the optional test-tone procedure. Skip them if you go straight to a phone as the audio source.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n### TDA2822D pinout (DIP-8, looking down at the chip with the notch at the top)\n\n```\n        +--U--+\nOUT1  1 |     | 8  OUT2\nIN1-  2 |     | 7  IN2-\nIN1+  3 |     | 6  IN2+\nGND   4 |     | 5  VCC (9V)\n        +-----+\n```\n\nPin reference for the build:\n- Pin 1 OUT1, Pin 8 OUT2 - speaker outputs (left and right).\n- Pin 3 IN1+, Pin 6 IN2+ - non-inverting (signal) inputs.\n- Pin 2 IN1-, Pin 7 IN2- - inverting inputs. We use these to set AC gain.\n- Pin 4 GND, Pin 5 VCC - power.\n\n**Important correction vs older \"bootstrap cap\" tutorials:** the TDA2822 family does NOT have bootstrap pins. Pins 2 and 7 are the inverting inputs. The 100uF caps you'll see in this design go from each IN- pin to GND - they're AC bypass caps that short the IN- pin to ground for audio frequencies, giving the chip its maximum closed-loop gain (~39 dB) while leaving the internal DC bias path intact. Different role, different physics from the bootstrap caps used on a TDA2003 or TDA2030.\n\n### Power\n\n- 9V battery (+) -> Vcc rail. Battery (-) -> GND rail.\n- 100uF 16V bulk cap across the rails, leads as short as possible, sitting between pin 5 and pin 4. Long leg (+) goes to the Vcc rail.\n\n### Per channel (mirror image for left and right)\n\nFor the LEFT channel (the RIGHT channel uses pin 6, pin 7, pin 8 in the same shape):\n\n1. Audio source -> 10uF input cap (+ side toward the chip) -> chip pin 3 (IN1+).\n2. Chip pin 2 (IN1-) -> 100uF 16V cap (+ side toward the chip pin) -> GND rail. This is the AC-bypass cap that sets gain.\n3. Chip pin 1 (OUT1) -> 4.7R resistor -> 470uF 16V output cap (+ side toward the chip) -> LEFT earbud (+).\n4. LEFT earbud (-)","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF 16V (electrolytic)","qty":3},{"item_name":"Capacitor 10uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":2},{"item_name":"Capacitor 470uF 16V (electrolytic)","qty":2},{"item_name":"TDA2822D Audio Power Amp","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":2},{"item_name":"Resistor 4.7R (1/8W 1%)","qty":2}]},{"id":69,"name":"NE5532 Hi-Fi Headphone Preamp","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"9V battery (or 9V DC wall-wart, centre-positive) feeding the breadboard rails directly. The op-amp draws under 10 mA, so a single 9V will last many hours of listening. If the rail sags below ~6.5V the NE5532 starts to clip - swap the battery.","code_language":"text","code":"// NE5532 Hi-Fi Headphone Preamp - Multimeter Test Procedure\n//\n// This is a pure-analog project. There is no microcontroller in the\n// signal path. Use the steps below with a DMM (digital multimeter) to\n// confirm the circuit is alive before you plug in headphones.\n//\n// What you need:\n//   - The completed breadboard, NOT YET connected to an audio source\n//   - A fresh 9V battery (or 9V wall-wart)\n//   - A DMM set to DC volts\n//\n// SAFETY: keep headphones UNPLUGGED for steps 1-4. Op-amps occasionally\n// oscillate on first power-up and a loud click into your ears is no fun.\n//\n// Test-point map (also marked on the wiring diagram):\n//   TP1 = +V rail (NE5532 pin 8)\n//   TP2 = Virtual ground node (junction of the two 22K resistors,\n//         the 100uF 16V cap, and the far end of both 10K input bias\n//         resistors)\n//   TP3 = Left channel output, AFTER the 1uF coupling cap\n//   TP4 = Right channel output, AFTER the 1uF coupling cap\n//   GND = NE5532 pin 4 / battery minus\n//\n// ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Step 1 - Power rail check (no input signal)\n// ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Probe between TP1 (NE5532 pin 8, V+) and GND (pin 4).\n// Expected: 8.5 V to 9.2 V DC on a fresh battery.\n// If you see 0 V: battery dead, polarity reversed, or rail wire missing.\n// If you see less than 6.5 V: the NE5532 needs at least ~6 V across\n// pins 8 and 4 to behave. Below that it clips and distorts. A tired\n// 9V alkaline under load can sag here - swap in a fresh battery before\n// blaming the circuit.\n//\n// ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Step 2 - Virtual ground check\n// ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Probe between TP2 (virtual ground) and GND. TP2 is the breadboard\n// row where BOTH 22K resistors meet the 100uF 16V cap. It is also the\n// \"far end\" of both 10K input bias resistors - so an easy visual cue\n// is to follow either 10K back from NE5532 pin 3 or pin 5 until both\n// 10Ks land in the same row.\n// Expected: approximately HALF the supply voltage, around 4.3 V to 4.6 V.\n// If you see 0 V or 9 V: one of the 22K bias resistors is missing or\n// shorted. If it slowly drifts: the 100uF 16V cap is in backwards.\n//\n// ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Step 3 - Output DC offset (the most important measurement)\n// -----------------------------------------------","description":"**What it is**: Plug your phone or laptop into one end and your headphones into the other. Music goes in, music comes out, but cleaner and louder, with no static or hiss in the quiet parts.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You built the same kind of audio booster that lives inside real recording studio gear, from a handful of parts on a breadboard. Hand the headphones to your dad, play his favourite song, and watch his face when he realises YOU made the thing the sound is coming through.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x NE5532 Op-Amp (audio dual) - the \"U1\" in the schematic; both halves used (one per stereo channel)\n- 4x Capacitor 1uF 50V (electrolytic) - AC coupling caps (2 input, 2 output)\n- 4x Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%) - 2 input bias resistors to virtual ground, 2 feedback path resistors\n- 2x Resistor 100R (1/8W 1%) - output build-out resistors to protect against headphone-cable capacitance\n- 2x Resistor 22K (1/8W 1%) - virtual-ground bias divider\n- 1x Capacitor 100uF 25V (electrolytic) - power-rail bulk decoupling\n- 1x Capacitor 100uF 16V (electrolytic) - virtual-ground filter cap (kills supply hum on the mid-rail)\n- 1x LED 5mm Red + 1x Resistor 470R (1/8W 1%) - power-on indicator (470R picks the dim end of the inventory's 220R-470R LED range)\n- 1x Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm - build surface\n- Jumper wires M-M\n\nYou also supply: a 9V battery (or 9V wall-wart with the centre pin positive), a 3.5mm stereo audio input (e.g. a cut-up old aux cable), a 3.5mm stereo headphone jack for the output, and a pair of headphones (32-ohm or higher impedance is happiest - see talking points for why).\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThis is a pure-analog project - no microcontroller, no code on a chip. You will validate it with a multimeter (see the \"code\" field) before you plug in headphones.\n\nPower: connect the 9V battery + to the top rail and battery - to the bottom rail. The bottom rail IS ground for the whole circuit. Drop the 100uF 25V bulk cap straight across these rails as close to where the battery wires arrive as possible - it stops the rail sagging when the op-amp briefly draws current.\n\nVirtual ground: the NE5532 runs from a single supply, so we fake a second rail by splitting +9V in half with the two 22K resistors. The junction of those resistors is \"virtual ground\" - it sits at about 4.5V and behaves like a quiet 0V reference for the op-amp. The 100uF 16V cap sits between that node and real ground - it shunts any leftover ripple to ground so the virtual rail is glass-smooth. Mark that breadboard row as **TP2** with a Sharpie or a flag - you'll come back to it during testing.\n\nNE5532 pinout (top view, pin 1 dot at top-left):\n- Pin 1 = Output A (Left channel out)\n- Pin 2 = Inverting input A\n- Pin 3 = Non-inverting input A (Left channel in)\n- Pin 4 = V- (ground)\n- Pin 5 = Non-inverting input B (Right channel in)\n- Pin 6 = Inverting input B\n- Pin 7 = Output B (Right channel out)\n- Pin 8 = V+ (+9V)\n\nEach channel is a unity-gain non-inverting buffer:\n1. Input audio -> 1uF coupling cap -> NE5532 pin 3 ","allocations":[{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF 16V (electrolytic)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF 25V (electrolytic)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 1uF 50V (electrolytic)","qty":4},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"NE5532 Op-Amp (audio dual)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 100R (1/8W 1%)","qty":2},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":4},{"item_name":"Resistor 22K (1/8W 1%)","qty":2},{"item_name":"Resistor 470R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1}]},{"id":72,"name":"LM317 USB-C 3V3 Breadboard Brick","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"USB-C charger or laptop port (5V) feeds the 2-pin pigtail. The LM317T regulates it down to ~3.3V via a 220R/390R divider on the ADJ pin, with electrolytic bypass caps on Vin, Vout and ADJ.","code_language":"bash","code":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# LM317 3.3V Brick - Multimeter verification procedure\n# ----------------------------------------------------\n# This is a pure-analog power supply project, so the right test instrument\n# is a multimeter (DMM) in DC volts mode, NOT a microcontroller. Reading a\n# 3.3V rail with an Arduino Uno's ADC (5V reference) wastes half the ADC\n# range and adds error - and worse, hides the regulator's behaviour behind\n# a noisy ADC. So: probe the test points below with a DMM and tick them off.\n#\n# Set the DMM to \"DC V\", 20 V range (or auto-range).\n# Black probe to the breadboard (-) rail (GND) for every reading except where\n# noted otherwise.\n#\n# ---- Bench-up sequence (do this BEFORE plugging in USB-C) ----\n#   1. Inspect cap polarities. The stripe on every electrolytic is the negative\n#      leg. Stripe goes to GND. Get this wrong and the cap will vent.\n#   2. Inspect LM317 leg order (TO-220, text side facing you, legs down):\n#        pin 1 = ADJ (left)\n#        pin 2 = Vout (middle)\n#        pin 3 = Vin  (right)\n#      NOTE: this is OPPOSITE to the L7805 pinout. Swap them by accident and\n#      Vin meets ADJ and the part will let the smoke out.\n#   3. DMM on continuity (beeper). Probe USB-C VBUS to USB-C GND on the\n#      pigtail side. Should NOT beep. If it beeps you have a short - find it\n#      before applying power.\n#\n# ---- Test points with USB-C plugged in ----\n#   TP1: USB-C VBUS to GND   -> expect 4.75 to 5.25 V (depends on charger)\n#   TP2: LM317 pin 3 (Vin) to GND\n#                            -> within ~0.05 V of TP1 (no series resistor now)\n#   TP3: LM317 pin 2 (Vout) to GND\n#                            -> 3.30 to 3.45 V, rock steady\n#                               (theoretical: 1.25 * (1 + 390/220) = 3.47 V;\n#                                resistor tolerance + LM317 Vref tolerance\n#                                spread it across that band)\n#   TP4: LM317 pin 1 (ADJ) to GND\n#                            -> TP3 minus 1.25 V (the LM317's internal Vref)\n#                               i.e. about 2.05 to 2.20 V\n#   TP5: Across the 22uF input cap\n#                            -> same reading as TP2\n#   TP6: Across the 10uF output cap\n#                            -> same reading as TP3\n#   TP7: Green LED anode to GND\n#                            -> same as TP3 (no drop yet)\n#         Green LED cathode to GND\n#                            -> ~2.0 V (the LED's forward voltage above GND)\n#\n# ---- Pass criteria ----\n#   TP3 reads 3.30 - 3.45 V wit","description":"**What it is**: Plug in a USB-C cable from any phone charger. A green LED glows on the breadboard and the red (+) rail becomes a clean 3.3V power source you can run your ESP32 boards from.\n\n**Why it's fun**: This is your own pocket-sized power station. Once you build it, every future project on the breadboard just plugs in next to it and works, no batteries, no soldering, no buying a special supply.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x LM317T Adjustable Regulator - the workhorse, set to ~3.3V via two resistors on the ADJ pin\n- 1x USB-C Female Chassis Connector (2P pigtail) - 5V input from any USB-C source\n- 1x Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%) - R1 in the LM317 divider (Vout to ADJ)\n- 1x Resistor 390R (1/8W 1%) - R2 in the LM317 divider (ADJ to GND)\n- 1x Capacitor 22uF 50V (electrolytic) - input bulk cap, sits across Vin and GND right at the LM317 legs\n- 1x Capacitor 10uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage) - output cap, sits across Vout and GND right at the LM317 legs\n- 1x Capacitor 4.7uF 50V (electrolytic) - ADJ-to-GND bypass cap, boosts ripple rejection\n- 1x LED 5mm Green - power-good indicator on the 3.3V rail\n- 1x Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%) - current-limit resistor for the green LED\n- 1x Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points) - everything lives here, no soldering\n- 1x Pin Header 1x40 Male Breakaway (2.54mm) - break off a 2-pin piece to anchor the USB-C pigtail\n- 1x Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc - short jumpers to route output to the breadboard's (+) rail\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe LM317 in TO-220 has three legs viewed from the front (text side facing you, legs pointing down): **pin 1 = ADJ (left), pin 2 = Vout (middle), pin 3 = Vin (right).** This is the OPPOSITE of the L7805 pinout - the regulator family is not pin-compatible. Get this wrong and the input voltage hits the ADJ pin directly and the part dies.\n\nLayout on the breadboard:\n\n1. Plant the LM317 across three adjacent rows in the middle of the board.\n2. **Input cap (22uF):** plug it across pin 3 (Vin) and a (-) rail row. **Stripe (the marked leg) is the negative leg - it goes to GND.** Reverse-polarity electrolytics heat up, sometimes loudly. The datasheet's minimum input cap is 0.1uF, but a bigger electro holds the rail up during ESP32 radio bursts.\n3. **Output cap (10uF):** across pin 2 (Vout) and a (-) rail row. Stripe to GND. The LM317 datasheet specifically calls for 1uF tantalum *or* 10uF electrolytic on the output for loop stability. The bigger value is the right pick when using electrolytics because their ESR makes the smaller cap less effective.\n4. **Resistor divider (the bit that sets Vout):** R1 (220R) goes from pin 2 (Vout) to pin 1 (ADJ). R2 (390R) goes from pin 1 (ADJ) to the (-) rail. The LM317 always works to keep 1.25V across R1, which forces a known current through R2, which sets Vout. Math: Vout = 1.25 * (1 + R2/R1) = 1.25 * (1 + 390/220) = 3.47V theoretical. Real-world resistor tolerance plus the LM317's own Vref tolerance puts the actual reading somewhere in the 3.30 to 3.45V band - all comfortably inside the ","allocations":[{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 10uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 22uF 50V (electrolytic)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 4.7uF 50V (electrolytic)","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1},{"item_name":"Pin Header 1x40 Male Breakaway (2.54mm)","qty":1},{"item_name":"USB-C Female Chassis Connector (2P pigtail)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":2},{"item_name":"Resistor 390R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"LM317T Adjustable Regulator","qty":1}]},{"id":73,"name":"LM317T Constant-Current LED Driver","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"6.5 V to 12 V DC from a 9 V battery or bench supply, fed to the +V rail of the breadboard (a 9 V battery is the easy choice; the LM317 needs ~3 V of dropout above the LED + Rset stack, so anything under ~6.5 V will fall out of regulation).","code_language":"bash","code":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# ============================================================\n# LM317T Constant-Current LED Driver - bench test procedure\n# ============================================================\n# This is a pure-analog project. There is no MCU and no code.\n# Instead, follow this multimeter procedure to prove the circuit\n# is doing what the datasheet says: holding a fixed current\n# regardless of LED forward voltage drift.\n#\n# Tools needed:\n#   - Digital multimeter (DMM) set to DC volts, then to DC mA\n#   - 9V battery OR bench supply set to 9V (anything 6.5V to 12V works)\n#\n# Test point map (matches the wiring diagram):\n#   TP1 = LM317 Vin pin (pin 3, the input)\n#   TP2 = LM317 Vout pin (pin 2, the output, top of Rset)\n#   TP3 = LM317 ADJ pin (pin 1) = bottom of Rset = LED anode\n#   TP4 = LED cathode = where the cathode wire meets the GND rail\n#\n# ------------------------------------------------------------\n# Step 1: Power-off sanity check\n# ------------------------------------------------------------\n# With power disconnected, measure resistance from TP2 to TP3.\n# Expected: ~56 ohms (your Rset). If you read kilohms or open,\n# the resistor is wrong or not seated. Fix before powering on.\n#\n# ------------------------------------------------------------\n# Step 2: Verify the reference voltage (the LM317 magic number)\n# ------------------------------------------------------------\n# Power the circuit. LED should be glowing steadily.\n# DMM in DC volts, measure between TP2 (Vout) and TP3 (ADJ).\n# Expected: 1.20 V to 1.30 V (datasheet says 1.25V nominal).\n# This is the LM317 holding its internal reference across Rset.\n# If you see this, the regulator is in regulation. Done.\n#\n# ------------------------------------------------------------\n# Step 3: Verify the current (Ohm's law in action)\n# ------------------------------------------------------------\n# Compute I = V(TP2,TP3) / 56 ohms.\n# Example: 1.25 V / 56 R = 0.0223 A = ~22 mA.\n# Now confirm by measurement: open the LED-cathode-to-GND\n# connection (pull the wire that runs from the LED's short leg\n# to the GND rail) and insert the meter inline there. Set DMM\n# to DC mA (start on the 200 mA or higher range to be safe).\n# Expected: within ~5 percent of the computed value above.\n#\n# ------------------------------------------------------------\n# Step 4: Prove it is a CURRENT source, not a voltage source\n# ------------------------------------------------------------\n# This is the cool part. While running:\n#   ","description":"**What it is**: Hook up a 9V battery and the LED glows at the perfect brightness. Now swap in a fresh battery, or a weaker one, or wiggle the wires around - the LED stays the exact same brightness. It refuses to get any brighter or dimmer.\n\n**Why it's fun**: This is your first taste of a real engineering trick: a circuit that fights back to keep itself steady, no code required. Try to make the LED flicker by tapping the battery on and off - you can't, and that's the magic.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x LM317T Adjustable Regulator (TO-220, the star of the show)\n- 1x Resistor 56R (1/8W 1%) - this is \"Rset\", the only resistor that matters\n- 1x LED 5mm Red - the load we are driving (at a safe ~22 mA)\n- 1x Capacitor 10uF (electrolytic, rated >=16V) - input bulk decoupling\n- 1x Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm\n- A handful of M-M jumper wires\n- A 6.5 V to 12 V supply (a 9 V battery is perfect, or a bench supply)\n\nNo microcontroller. No code. Just one IC, one resistor, and a multimeter to prove it works.\n\n**LED derating note (important):** a generic 5mm through-hole LED is rated for ~20 to 30 mA continuous. We deliberately target ~22 mA with a 56R Rset to stay inside that envelope. If you ever see a tutorial that calls for 100 mA or more through a plain 5mm LED, ignore it - the LED will dim within hours and die within days. Higher currents belong on 1W \"power LEDs\" with a proper heatsink, not on indicator LEDs from a bag of 100.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe LM317T has three legs. Looking at the front (text side), they are, left to right: **ADJ (pin 1), Vout (pin 2), Vin (pin 3)**.\n\nThe trick is that the LM317 always tries to hold exactly **1.25 V between its Vout pin and its ADJ pin**. In the normal \"voltage regulator\" hookup you use two resistors to bleed that reference into a divider. In this hookup you put a single resistor between Vout and ADJ, and the LED hangs off the ADJ pin.\n\nResult: the LM317 forces 1.25 V across that one resistor, which by Ohm's law forces a fixed current through it. And since the LED is in series with that resistor, the LED gets that same fixed current. The current is `I = 1.25 V / R`. With `R = 56R`, that is **~22 mA**, a safe, indicator-LED-friendly value.\n\nBuild order on the breadboard:\n\n1. Drop the LM317T into the board so its three legs land on three separate rows.\n2. Wire **Vin (pin 3)** to the +V rail.\n3. Wire the 10uF cap (rated **>=16V**, since we are running up to 12V on the rail) from +V rail to GND rail, observing polarity (long leg / no stripe = +). If you have a 0.1uF ceramic handy, dropping it in parallel with the electrolytic right at the Vin pin is conventional LM317 hygiene - not required, but it cleans up high-frequency noise the electrolytic is too slow to handle.\n4. Wire the **56R resistor between Vout (pin 2) and ADJ (pin 1)**.\n5. From **ADJ (pin 1)**, jumper to the **LED anode** (the long leg).\n6. **LED cathode** (short leg, flat side) goes to the GND rail.\n7. Connect your power: +V rail to +6.5V to +12V, GND rail to supply ground.","allocations":[{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 10uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 56R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"LM317T Adjustable Regulator","qty":1}]},{"id":75,"name":"ULN2803 Knight Rider Light Bar","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"Arduino Uno powered from laptop USB (5V). The 5V rail also feeds the ULN2803 COM pin (10) and the LED anodes through 220R resistors; the chip sinks each LED cathode to GND when its input pin goes HIGH.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// ULN2803 Knight Rider Light Bar\n// 8 LEDs scanned left-to-right and back, driven through a ULN2803 Darlington array.\n//\n// Wiring (must match the diagram):\n//   Arduino D2..D9 -> ULN2803 IN1..IN8\n//   ULN2803 OUT1..OUT8 -> LED cathodes (LED1..LED8)\n//   LED anodes -> 220R resistor -> +5V rail\n//   ULN2803 pin  9 -> GND\n//   ULN2803 pin 10 -> +5V (COM, load supply)\n//\n// Logic note: the ULN2803 is a SINK driver. When an Arduino input pin is HIGH,\n// the matching ULN2803 output is pulled to GND, completing the LED circuit and\n// turning the LED on. HIGH on the Arduino = LED ON.\n\nconst uint8_t LED_PINS[8] = {2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9};\nconst uint8_t NUM_LEDS = 8;\n\n// Milliseconds the \"dot\" stays on each LED.\n// Lower = faster scan. Try 30 (fast) or 200 (lazy bounce).\nconst uint16_t STEP_MS = 80;\n\nint position = 0;   // current lit LED index, 0..NUM_LEDS-1\nint direction = 1;  // +1 moves right, -1 moves left\n\nvoid setup() {\n  for (uint8_t i = 0; i < NUM_LEDS; i++) {\n    pinMode(LED_PINS[i], OUTPUT);\n    digitalWrite(LED_PINS[i], LOW);  // all off at boot\n  }\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  // Turn all LEDs off, then light only the current one.\n  for (uint8_t i = 0; i < NUM_LEDS; i++) {\n    digitalWrite(LED_PINS[i], LOW);\n  }\n  digitalWrite(LED_PINS[position], HIGH);\n\n  delay(STEP_MS);\n\n  // Advance the dot. Bounce at the ends instead of wrapping.\n  position += direction;\n  if (position >= NUM_LEDS - 1) {\n    position = NUM_LEDS - 1;\n    direction = -1;\n  } else if (position <= 0) {\n    position = 0;\n    direction = 1;\n  }\n}\n","description":"**What it is**: Eight LEDs in a row light up one at a time, with a glowing dot that zooms left, then right, then left again, forever. It looks exactly like the front of KITT, the talking car from Knight Rider.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You built the same effect that 80s TV used on a real car prop, and once it works you can crank the speed dial in the code to make the dot bounce slow and lazy or so fast it almost blurs into a solid line. Show it to your friends with the lights off and watch them lean in.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x Arduino Uno R3 (the brain - sends the \"which LED\" pattern)\n- 1x ULN2803APG Darlington Array - the star of the show: eight transistor pairs in one chip\n- 1x Breadboard (~400 tie-points) - everything lives here, no soldering\n- 8x 5mm LEDs - 5 Red + 3 Yellow (we only have 5 red in stock, the yellows finish the bar)\n- 8x 220R resistors - one per LED to keep them from burning out\n- Jumper wires (M-M for the breadboard, F-M from Uno to breadboard)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe ULN2803 is the trick that makes this clean. It has 18 pins:\n\n- Pins 1-8 on the left are INPUTS (IN1-IN8). Wire these to Arduino pins D2 through D9.\n- Pins 11-18 on the right are OUTPUTS (OUT1-OUT8). Each one connects to the CATHODE (short leg, flat side) of one LED.\n- Pin 9 is GND - connect to Arduino GND and the breadboard ground rail.\n- Pin 10 is COM - connect to the breadboard +5V rail (this is the \"load supply\").\n\nFor each LED:\n\n- Anode (long leg) -> one end of a 220R resistor\n- Other end of the 220R -> the breadboard +5V rail\n- Cathode (short leg, flat side) -> ULN2803 output pin (OUT1 for LED1, OUT2 for LED2, etc.)\n\nArrange the eight LEDs in a row on the breadboard so the chase is visible as a moving dot.\n\nPower rails: Arduino 5V -> breadboard red rail. Arduino GND -> breadboard blue rail. ULN2803 pin 9 to blue rail, pin 10 to red rail.\n\nWhen the Arduino sets D2 HIGH, the ULN2803 pulls OUT1 to ground - current flows from +5V through the resistor, through the LED, through the chip to ground. LED on. When D2 goes LOW, the chip opens the circuit. LED off. Same for the other seven.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- The whole point of the ULN2803 is replacement of eight separate transistor circuits. If you wanted to drive eight LEDs with discrete BC547s, you would need 8 transistors plus 8 base resistors - sixteen extra parts and a maze of wires. Here it is one chip.\n- \"Darlington\" means two transistors in a row inside the chip, which gives huge current gain - a tiny 5V signal from the Arduino can switch loads up to 500 mA per channel.\n- The chip is a SINK driver, not a source driver. It pulls outputs DOWN to ground, it does not push UP to +5V. That is why the LED anodes go to +5V and the cathodes go to the chip. This is \"current sinking\" or \"low-side switching\" and it is the most common way driver ICs work.\n- Pin 10 (COM) has internal flyback diodes built in. We do not need them for LEDs, but they exist so the same chip can drive relays and motors without exploding from coil kickback.","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":5},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Yellow","qty":3},{"item_name":"ULN2803APG Darlington Array (8-ch)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":8}]},{"id":76,"name":"PC817 Optoisolated Relay Trigger Board","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S powered from USB; load-side 5V tapped from the ESP32 Vin rail for the first build (shared ground, no real isolation yet - cut later for a separate brick).","code_language":"cpp","code":"// PC817 Optoisolated Relay Trigger Board\n//\n// ESP32 NodeMCU-32S drives a PC817 optocoupler LED through a 1K resistor.\n// On the load side, the PC817 phototransistor emitter feeds the BC337 base\n// directly (with a 10K pull-down keeping the base at GND when the opto is dark).\n// The BC337 saturates and sinks the relay-module IN pin to ground, which fires\n// the active-LOW 1-channel relay. The relay then closes the green demo-LED\n// circuit.\n//\n// Wiring (MUST match the Mermaid diagram):\n//   ESP32 GPIO 23 -> 1K -> PC817 pin 1 (anode)\n//   ESP32 GND     ----- PC817 pin 2 (cathode)\n//   Load-side +5V (from relay module VCC rail) -> PC817 pin 4 (collector)\n//   PC817 pin 3 (emitter) -> BC337 base (direct connection)\n//   BC337 base -> 10K pull-down -> load-side GND\n//   BC337 emitter -> load-side GND\n//   BC337 collector -> Relay module IN\n//   Relay module VCC -> load-side +5V, GND -> load-side GND\n//   Relay COM -> load-side +5V, NO -> 330R -> Green LED anode -> LED cathode -> load-side GND\n//\n// IMPORTANT: in the first build the load-side +5V is jumpered from the ESP32\n// Vin (USB 5V) rail. That means the two grounds ARE connected, so this build\n// is a wiring exercise, not real isolation. The PC817 is in the signal path\n// so the bridge is a single wire you cut later - see \"Things to change\".\n\nconst int OPTO_PIN = 23;          // GPIO driving the PC817 LED through 1K\nconst unsigned long PERIOD_MS = 2000;  // toggle every 2 seconds\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n  delay(200);\n  pinMode(OPTO_PIN, OUTPUT);\n  digitalWrite(OPTO_PIN, LOW);    // start with opto dark, relay open\n  Serial.println(\"PC817 Optoisolated Relay Trigger - boot\");\n  Serial.println(\"Toggling GPIO 23 every 2 seconds.\");\n  Serial.println(\"Listen for the relay click. Watch the green LED.\");\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  digitalWrite(OPTO_PIN, HIGH);   // light PC817 LED -> BC337 on -> relay closes\n  Serial.println(\"OPTO ON  -> relay CLOSED (LED should be lit)\");\n  delay(PERIOD_MS);\n\n  digitalWrite(OPTO_PIN, LOW);    // PC817 LED off -> BC337 off -> relay opens\n  Serial.println(\"OPTO OFF -> relay OPEN   (LED should be dark)\");\n  delay(PERIOD_MS);\n}\n","description":"**What it is**: Tell the ESP32 to flip a tiny switch, and CLICK - a real relay snaps shut and a green LED lights up. You hear the click, you see the light, and the chip in the middle controls it all without ever touching the LED's wires.\n\n**Why it's fun**: That click sound is the same one in dishwashers, garage doors, and old arcade machines - now you can make it happen on command. Even cooler: the two halves of the circuit never touch each other, they only talk through a flash of invisible light inside a little black chip.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the controller side - 3.3V logic)\n- PC817 optocoupler (the isolation barrier - an LED facing a phototransistor in a 4-pin DIP)\n- BC337 NPN BJT in TO-92 (the muscle that switches the relay-module input)\n- 1-channel Relay Module (active-LOW; we treat it as the \"load\" stage)\n- 1K resistor (limits current into the PC817 input LED)\n- 10K resistor (base pull-down so the BC337 stays off when the opto is dark)\n- 330R resistor + green 5mm LED (a visible \"load\" the relay switches so you can see the result)\n- Breadboard, M-M and F-M jumper wires\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe whole point of this board is that there are two electrical \"sides\" that should not share copper. **In the first build they DO share copper** - we cheat and power both sides off the ESP32's USB rail so you only need one cable. That means this initial build is a wiring exercise, not a real isolation demo. The PC817 is sitting in the signal path ready to do its job; \"Things to change once it works\" walks you through cutting the one jumper that makes the isolation real.\n\n- **Controller side (left):** ESP32 GPIO 23 -> 1K resistor -> PC817 pin 1 (LED anode). PC817 pin 2 (LED cathode) -> ESP32 GND. That is all the MCU sees.\n- **Load side (right):** PC817 pin 4 (collector) ties to the load-side +5V rail. PC817 pin 3 (emitter) drives the BC337 base **directly** (no series resistor), with a 10K from base to load-side GND as a pull-down. BC337 emitter sits on load-side GND. BC337 collector ties to the relay module's IN pin. The relay module's VCC and GND go to the load-side +5V and GND. The relay then switches COM/NO into a 330R + green LED loop.\n\n**Why no series base resistor?** The PC817's phototransistor is already current-limited - it can only deliver as much collector current as its CTR (current transfer ratio) lets through for the input LED current we're using. Stacking a 10K series resistor in front of the BC337 base would form a divider with the 10K pull-down and cut available base drive roughly in half. At 2.1 mA forward current through the PC817 LED (3.3V GPIO minus the LED's ~1.2V Vf, divided by 1K), the phototransistor's output is on the low end of its CTR curve, so we want every milliamp of base drive reaching the BC337. Direct emitter-to-base, with only the 10K pull-down for safety, is the cleaner topology.\n\nThe 10K base pull-down is the safety belt: if the phototransistor is dark, the BC337 base must be at a clean GND. Without it, the","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1},{"item_name":"PC817 Optocoupler","qty":1},{"item_name":"1-channel Relay Module","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 1K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"BC337 BJT (TO-92)","qty":1}]},{"id":78,"name":"BC327 High-Side Load Switch for the Laser","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"USB-C into the XIAO ESP32-C3; the board's 5 V rail powers the laser and the 3.3 V GPIO drives the BC547 inverter.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// BC327 High-Side Load Switch demo for the KY-008 laser.\n//\n// Pin mapping note (READ THIS): on the XIAO ESP32-C3 silkscreen,\n// the pin labelled \"D2\" is internally GPIO4. The Arduino core for\n// this board maps the Dx silk labels to the matching D-number\n// constants, so we use the literal 4 here to make it unambiguous\n// regardless of how the core's headers are configured.\n//\n// Wiring summary (must match the Mermaid diagram):\n//   XIAO 5V  -> breadboard +5V rail (USB rail, NOT the 3V3 pin)\n//   XIAO GND -> breadboard GND rail\n//   XIAO D2 (GPIO4) -> 1K -> BC547 base\n//   BC547 emitter -> GND\n//   BC547 collector -> 1K -> BC327 base\n//   BC327 base -> 10K -> +5V (pull-up, keeps laser OFF at boot)\n//   BC327 emitter -> +5V\n//   BC327 collector -> KY-008 \"S\" (signal / current-limited input) pin\n//   KY-008 GND (\"-\") -> GND, third pin left disconnected\n//\n// KY-008 pin identification (clones disagree, so read YOUR silk):\n//   Look at the bottom of the module - it is silk-printed with\n//   \"S\", \"+\" (or middle/VCC), and \"-\" (GND). Route the BC327\n//   collector to \"S\" only. Leave the unlabeled/middle pin\n//   disconnected unless your specific module's silk explicitly\n//   tells you to tie it to +5V. Some clones short S to + internally\n//   through a current-limit resistor; tying both to +5V on those\n//   variants bypasses the limiter and cooks the diode.\n//\n// Logic recap:\n//   GPIO HIGH  -> BC547 ON  -> pulls BC327 base LOW  -> BC327 ON  -> laser ON\n//   GPIO LOW   -> BC547 OFF -> 10K pulls BC327 base HIGH -> BC327 OFF -> laser OFF\n//\n// Safety: do NOT stare into the laser. Aim it at a piece of paper on the bench.\n\nconst int CTRL_PIN = 4;  // XIAO ESP32-C3 silk \"D2\" = GPIO4\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n  // Pre-load the output latch LOW *before* flipping to OUTPUT so the\n  // pin never briefly drives HIGH the instant pinMode() takes effect.\n  // The 10K base pull-up is what actually keeps the laser off during\n  // the high-Z window before this line runs; this trick just makes\n  // the GPIO's first driven state guaranteed-LOW.\n  digitalWrite(CTRL_PIN, LOW);\n  pinMode(CTRL_PIN, OUTPUT);\n\n  delay(500);\n  Serial.println();\n  Serial.println(\"BC327 high-side switch demo\");\n  Serial.println(\"HIGH on D2/GPIO4 = laser ON, LOW = laser OFF\");\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  // 1 second on, 1 second off, then a short PWM burst to show\n  // the switch is fast enough to dim/blink visibly.\n  Serial.println(\"ON  (1 s)\");\n  digitalWrite(CTRL_PIN, HIGH);\n  delay(1000);\n\n  Seri","description":"**What it is**: Type a command on your laptop and a real red laser beam clicks on. Type another and it clicks off. Wave your hand through it in a dusty room and you can see the line cutting through the air.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It is your first actual laser, and YOU are the switch. Stick it in a cardboard box with a mirror and you have the start of a spy-movie tripwire for your bedroom door.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3 (3.3 V GPIO control signal)\n- BC327 PNP BJT in TO-92 (high-side pass transistor for the 5 V rail)\n- BC547 NPN BJT in TO-92 (small inverter that pulls the BC327 base down)\n- KY-008 red laser module (the load being switched)\n- 1x 10K resistor (base pull-up keeping BC327 off by default)\n- 2x 1K resistor (one for the XIAO -> BC547 base, one for BC547 collector -> BC327 base)\n- Breadboard, jumper wires (USB cable powers everything off the XIAO's 5 V rail)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n**Pin label sanity check first.** On the XIAO ESP32-C3 silkscreen, the pin printed \"D2\" is GPIO4 internally (the silk numbering starts at D0=GPIO2, D1=GPIO3, D2=GPIO4, and so on). This project uses the pin physically labelled **D2**, which the sketch refers to as `CTRL_PIN = 4`. Wire to the silk-labelled D2 pin and the code is correct.\n\n**KY-008 pin identification.** Flip your KY-008 over and read the silk. You should see three letters: `S`, `+` (or no label / middle), and `-`. KY-008 clones genuinely disagree about whether the middle pin is VCC, NC, or the cathode. Treat your own module's silk as the ground truth and only connect what is explicitly labelled. The safe routing for this project is: BC327 collector to the **S** pin, GND rail to the **-** pin, and the third pin left **disconnected**. Do not tie the middle pin to +5 V unless your specific module's silk clearly says so - on the clones where S already has a built-in current-limit resistor, paralleling another +5 V into a mislabelled middle pin will bypass the limiter and burn the laser diode.\n\nThe whole point of the circuit is to switch power on the *high side* of the load (between +5 V and the laser) rather than the low side (between the laser and GND). Low-side switching with an NPN works, but it lifts the laser's ground above true GND while it's off, which makes shared-ground setups misbehave. High-side switching keeps the laser's GND tied solidly to system GND at all times.\n\nA PNP transistor (BC327) turns ON when its base is pulled *below* its emitter. The emitter sits at +5 V. To turn it on, you have to pull the base down to roughly +4.3 V or lower. A 3.3 V ESP32 GPIO going HIGH cannot do that directly: HIGH is already 1.7 V below +5 V, but it can't sink current from a 5 V rail through the base on its own without back-feeding the GPIO protection diodes. So we use a small NPN (BC547) as an inverter:\n\n1. XIAO 5V pin -> breadboard +5V rail. XIAO GND -> GND rail.\n2. XIAO D2 (GPIO4) -> 1K -> BC547 base. BC547 emitter -> GND. (Classic NPN low-side switch driving the base of the PNP.)\n3. BC547 collecto","allocations":[{"item_name":"Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"KY-008 Red Laser Module (650nm)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 1K (1/8W 1%)","qty":2},{"item_name":"BC327 BJT (TO-92)","qty":1},{"item_name":"BC547 BJT (TO-92)","qty":1}]},{"id":82,"name":"ULN2003 Servo Sweep Sentinel","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"Two separate USB 5V supplies sharing only GND: USB-to-Uno feeds the logic, LEDs, ULN2003 and PIR; a second USB-C into a TP4056 board feeds the SG90 servo so its current spikes cannot brown out the Uno.","code_language":"cpp","code":"// ULN2003 Servo Sweep Sentinel\n// One Uno GPIO drives 5 LEDs in parallel through a ULN2003,\n// while a PIR triggers an alarm hold window and the servo sweeps a flag.\n//\n// Power architecture (important!):\n//  - Uno + LEDs + ULN2003 + PIR run from the Uno's USB-fed 5V rail.\n//  - The SG90 servo runs from a SEPARATE 5V supply (a TP4056-style\n//    USB-C breakout works). The two grounds MUST be tied together,\n//    but the servo's noisy 5V never touches the Uno's 5V rail.\n\n#include <Servo.h>\n\n// ---- Pin map (matches wiring diagram) ----\nconst uint8_t PIR_PIN     = 2;   // HC-SR501 DOUT\nconst uint8_t LED_BANK    = 7;   // Drives ULN2003 inputs IN1..IN5 (tied together)\nconst uint8_t SERVO_PIN   = 9;   // SG90 signal wire (PWM-capable)\n\n// ---- Behaviour tuning ----\nconst unsigned long ALARM_HOLD_MS = 5000;  // Keep flag sweeping after last motion\nconst uint8_t  SWEEP_LEFT     = 20;\nconst uint8_t  SWEEP_RIGHT    = 160;\nconst uint16_t SWEEP_STEP_MS  = 15;\n\nServo flag;\n\n// Rollover-safe timing: we store the moment motion was last seen\n// and compare elapsed = (millis() - lastMotionAt). Unsigned subtraction\n// wraps cleanly across the ~49.7-day millis() rollover, so this stays\n// correct forever; `millis() + HOLD` does NOT.\nbool          alarmActive   = false;\nunsigned long lastMotionAt  = 0;\n\nuint8_t       pos     = 90;\nint8_t        dir     = 1;\nunsigned long lastStep = 0;\n\nvoid setLedBank(bool on) {\n  // The ULN2003 is an open-collector SINK driver. When LED_BANK goes HIGH,\n  // each tied input turns on a Darlington pair inside the chip that pulls\n  // its output pin down to near GND. Current then flows: +5V -> 220R -> LED\n  // -> ULN output pin -> GND. LEDs light. LED_BANK LOW = outputs float =\n  // LEDs dark.\n  digitalWrite(LED_BANK, on ? HIGH : LOW);\n}\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n\n  pinMode(PIR_PIN, INPUT);\n  pinMode(LED_BANK, OUTPUT);\n  setLedBank(false);\n\n  flag.attach(SERVO_PIN);\n  flag.write(90);          // centre / \"rest\" position\n\n  Serial.println(F(\"ULN2003 Servo Sweep Sentinel ready.\"));\n  Serial.println(F(\"Warming up PIR for 30 seconds...\"));\n  // Blocking delay() is normally a code smell, but it is fine here:\n  // we are at boot, nothing else needs to run, and the HC-SR501's\n  // pyroelectric element genuinely needs ~30 s to stabilise or it\n  // will fire constantly. Anywhere inside loop() we would use millis().\n  delay(30000);\n  Serial.println(F(\"Armed.\"));\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  unsigned long now = millis();\n\n  // PIR jumper note: this ","description":"**What it is**: Walk past it and a little flag on a stick swings back and forth while a row of five red lights pulses like a warning sign. Stand still and everything goes quiet again, waiting for the next person to wander by.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Hide it around a corner and prank your sister when she walks past. It is your very own motion-triggered alarm, like the ones in spy movies, except you built it.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL) - the 5V brain\n- 1x ULN2003AN Darlington Array (7-ch) - the muscle that sinks current for the LED row\n- 1x HC-SR501 PIR Motion Sensor - the trigger\n- 1x SG90 Servo (9g micro) - waves the warning flag, on its OWN 5V supply\n- 5x LED 5mm Red - the \"warning bank\"\n- 5x Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%) - one current limiter per LED (see math in Talking points)\n- 1x 470uF and 1x 100uF electrolytic - bulk reservoir on the servo rail, local decoupling on the ULN2003\n- 1x TP4056 USB-C charger/protection board - second, independent 5V source for the servo\n- 1x Breadboard, M-M and F-M jumpers\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n**Two power rails, one ground.** This is the single most important idea in the build.\n\n- *Rail A (Uno rail):* USB feeds the Uno, the Uno's `5V` pin feeds the breadboard's left `+5V` rail. That rail powers the LEDs, the ULN2003 (`COM` pin 9), and the HC-SR501.\n- *Rail B (Servo rail):* A second USB-C cable goes into the TP4056 board; its `OUT+` becomes the breadboard's right `+5V` rail. The SG90's red wire is the ONLY thing on that rail.\n- *Grounds:* The Uno's `GND`, the TP4056's `OUT-`, the PIR's GND, the LED cathodes' return path through the ULN2003 `GND` pin (8), and the SG90's brown wire ALL meet at the breadboard's shared `GND` rail. Without this common ground the servo signal makes no sense to the Uno.\n\nWhy bother? The SG90 yanks 600-700 mA when it stalls or changes direction, and the Uno's onboard 5V LDO can only deliver ~500-800 mA total. Sharing the rail will brown out the Uno - the 470 uF cap from the original draft just delays the moment of failure. Splitting the rails fixes it for real, and the optocouplers... wait, no, the ULN2003 has no opto stage; the *split rails* are what isolates the noise here.\n\n**ULN2003 pinout (16-pin DIP):**\n- Pins 1..7 = inputs IN1..IN7\n- Pin 8 = GND\n- Pin 9 = COM (tie to the Uno's +5V rail - this is the return path for the internal flyback diodes)\n- Pins 10..16 = outputs OUT7..OUT1 (mirror image of the inputs)\n\n**Connections:**\n- Uno `D7` -> ULN2003 IN1, IN2, IN3, IN4, IN5 all tied together (one GPIO drives five channels in parallel - the whole point of the project)\n- LED anodes -> Uno's `+5V` rail through a 220R each\n- LED cathodes -> ULN2003 OUT1..OUT5 (pins 16, 15, 14, 13, 12)\n- ULN2003 pin 8 (GND) -> shared GND rail\n- ULN2003 pin 9 (COM) -> Uno's `+5V` rail\n- 100 uF cap across the Uno's `+5V`/GND right next to the ULN2003\n- HC-SR501: VCC -> Uno's `+5V`, GND -> shared GND, OUT -> Uno `D2`\n- SG90: red -> TP4056's `+5V`, brown -> shared GND, orange -> Uno `D9` (PWM-capab","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF 16V (electrolytic)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 470uF 16V (electrolytic)","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":5},{"item_name":"ULN2003AN Darlington Array (7-ch)","qty":1},{"item_name":"18650 Charger / Protection Board (TP4056, USB-C)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"SG90 Servo (9g micro)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":5},{"item_name":"HC-SR501 PIR Motion Sensor","qty":1}]},{"id":83,"name":"NE555 Two-Tone Police Siren","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"9V battery (or 9V bench supply) into the breadboard + rail; one shared rail powers both NE555s and the LM386, with a 100uF bulk cap right next to the chips to keep the amplifier from upsetting the timers.","code_language":"bash","code":"# NE555 Two-Tone Police Siren - Multimeter Test Procedure\n#\n# This is a pure analog circuit (no microcontroller). Power it from a\n# regulated 9V bench supply (or a fresh 9V battery via a snap clip wired\n# to the breadboard + rail). Use a multimeter on DC volts for the slow\n# stage, then switch to AC volts (or just listen) for the audio stage.\n#\n# IMPORTANT - PREREQUISITES:\n#   - You must supply an 8 ohm speaker (0.5 W - 1 W). Not in the kit.\n#   - You must supply a 9V battery + clip, or a 9V bench PSU.\n#\n# TEST POINTS (label these on the breadboard with tape):\n#\n#   TP1 = +9V rail\n#   TP2 = GND rail\n#   TP3 = U1 pin 3 (slow 555 output, ~2.2 Hz square wave)\n#   TP4 = U2 pin 5 (control voltage input on fast 555)\n#   TP5 = U2 pin 3 (fast 555 audio output, ~1 kHz centre, swept)\n#   TP6 = LM386 pin 5 (amplified audio out, BEFORE the 100uF speaker cap;\n#         has a ~4.5 V DC offset on single-supply 9V - use TP7 for clean AC)\n#   TP7 = speaker + terminal (after C5, no DC offset)\n#\n# STEP 1 - Power-on sanity:\n#   - Set meter to DC volts, 20V range.\n#   - Black probe on TP2, red probe on TP1. Expect 8.8 - 9.2 V.\n#   - If you see less than 7V the LM386 is probably oscillating into a\n#     brownout. Add another 100uF across the rails right at the chip.\n#\n# STEP 2 - Slow stage alive:\n#   - Watch the red LED on TP3. It should blink visibly, about\n#     2 flashes per second. That IS the sweep.\n#   - Meter on DC volts at TP3 will bounce between ~0V and ~6-7V as the\n#     output toggles. The average will sit around 3-4V.\n#   - No blink? Check C1 polarity (long leg = +, into pin 6/2 of U1).\n#\n# STEP 3 - Modulation reaching the fast 555:\n#   - Meter on DC volts at TP4 (U2 pin 5, \"CV\").\n#   - You should see the voltage SWING up and down in time with the LED.\n#     Roughly 5V to 7V. That swinging CV pin is what bends the audio\n#     pitch up and down - that's the \"woop-woop\".\n#   - If TP4 sits dead still at ~6V, C2 is either missing, wrong\n#     polarity, or shorted. Reminder: C2 negative leg toward U1 pin 3,\n#     positive leg toward R5.\n#\n# STEP 4 - Audio coming out of the fast 555:\n#   - Switch meter to AC volts, 20V range. Probe TP5.\n#   - Expect roughly 2 - 3 V AC. (The meter is averaging a square wave,\n#     don't worry about the exact number - any reading above ~1V AC\n#     means the oscillator is running.)\n#   - You should also already faintly HEAR a buzz from the speaker.\n#\n# STEP 5 - Amplifier doing its job:\n#   - For the cleanest AC reading, probe TP7 (after C5)","description":"**What it is**: Flip the switch and a tiny speaker on your breadboard wails \"wee-ooo, wee-ooo\" exactly like a police car driving by. A red LED blinks in time with the sound, so you can see the siren as well as hear it.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You built a real siren out of a handful of parts and zero code. Hide it in a shoebox, sneak up behind your sister, flip the switch, and watch her jump.\n\n## Required external parts (you supply)\n\n- **8 ohm speaker, 0.5-1 W**. Any salvaged small speaker works. Not in the kit - this is a hard prerequisite. Without it the project does not finish.\n- **9V power source.** Either a fresh 9V battery + barrel/snap clip wired to the breadboard rails, or a 9V bench supply.\n\n## Parts list (from inventory)\n\n- 2x NE555 Timer IC (U1 = slow sweep oscillator, U2 = fast audio oscillator)\n- 1x LM386 Audio Power Amp (U3 = drives the speaker)\n- 4x 10K resistors (R1/R2 timing pair for U1, R5 modulation series, R6 LM386 input series)\n- 3x 470R resistors (R3/R4 timing pair for U2, R_LED current-limit for the sweep-indicator LED)\n- 1x 22uF electrolytic (C1, slow timing cap)\n- 2x 10uF electrolytic (C2 modulation coupling, C3 LM386 input coupling)\n- 3x 100uF electrolytic (C4 rail decoupling, C5 LM386 output coupling, C6 LM386 pin-7 bypass)\n- 1x 1uF 50V electrolytic (C7 fast 555 timing cap)\n- 1x 5mm red LED (visual sweep indicator - so you can SEE the woop-woop)\n- 1x 400-point breadboard\n- M-M jumper wires\n\n## The math (so you can trust the parts choice)\n\nNE555 astable frequency: **f = 1.44 / ((R_A + 2 R_B) C)**.\n\n- **U1 slow stage** with R1=R2=10K, C1=22uF: f = 1.44 / (30K * 22uF) approx **2.2 Hz**. That's the \"wee-ooo\" rate.\n- **U2 fast stage** with R3=R4=470R, C7=1uF: f = 1.44 / (1.41K * 1uF) approx **1020 Hz** centre. When U1's square wave is AC-coupled into U2's pin 5 (CV), the comparator thresholds get pushed up and down, sweeping the audio frequency roughly across **500-1500 Hz** - the classic siren range.\n\nThe original draft used R3=R4=10K with C7=1uF, which gives only 48 Hz (below the audio band, you'd hear a buzz not a tone). The 470R choice is the fix.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe whole circuit lives on one breadboard and runs from a single +9V rail. There is no microcontroller - the two NE555s and the LM386 do everything in pure analog hardware. This is the same trick used in real toy sirens.\n\n**U1 - the slow modulator (~2.2 Hz):**\n- pin 1 to GND, pin 8 to +9V\n- pin 4 (reset) to +9V\n- pin 7 to pin 8 through R1 = 10K\n- pin 7 to pin 6 through R2 = 10K\n- pin 6 tied to pin 2 (standard astable jumper)\n- pin 6/2 to GND through C1 = 22uF (long leg toward pin 6/2, short leg to GND)\n- pin 5 (CV) left floating (the kit has no ceramic cap to bypass it; the slow stage doesn't need one)\n- pin 3 (output) drives two things in parallel: the red LED via R_LED = 470R to GND (so you can SEE the sweep), AND through C2 = 10uF + R5 = 10K into U2's pin 5\n\n**U2 - the fast audio oscillator (~500-1500 Hz, swept):**\n- pin 1 to GND, pin 8 to +9V, pin 4 to +9V\n- pin","allocations":[{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":3},{"item_name":"Capacitor 10uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":2},{"item_name":"Capacitor 1uF 50V (electrolytic)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Capacitor 22uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1},{"item_name":"LM386 Audio Power Amp","qty":1},{"item_name":"NE555 Timer IC","qty":2},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":4},{"item_name":"Resistor 470R (1/8W 1%)","qty":3}]}]

const SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    id:      { type: 'integer' },
    section: { type: 'string', maxLength: 4000 },
  },
  required: ['id', 'section'],
}

const STYLE_GUIDE = [
  'Section title MUST be exactly: ## Flash it and run it',
  '',
  'Audience: a 10-year-old building with his dad. Click-by-click, no jargon.',
  '',
  'STRUCTURE depending on the project:',
  '',
  '(A) MCU projects with cpp/python code (Arduino Uno, ESP32 NodeMCU-32S, XIAO ESP32-C3):',
  '  Lead-in: "You need a laptop running the **Arduino IDE 2.x** (free download from [arduino.cc](https://www.arduino.cc/en/software))."',
  '',
  '  **One-time setup (only the first time you flash any <Uno|ESP32>):**',
  '  1. (ESP32 only) Add board manager URL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/espressif/arduino-esp32/gh-pages/package_esp32_index.json via File -> Preferences.',
  '  2. (ESP32 only) Tools -> Board -> Boards Manager, install "esp32 by Espressif Systems".',
  '  3. Tools -> Board -> <board name as it appears in the menu>.',
  '  4. If any extra libraries are needed (PubSubClient, DHT sensor library, Adafruit_SSD1306, TM1637, Servo, etc.), say which ones, where to install them (Tools -> Manage Libraries), and what to search for.',
  '',
  '  **Every time you flash:**',
  '  1. Plug board in via USB (note cable type for the specific board).',
  '  2. Tools -> Port -> COM* / cu.usbmodem* / ttyACM0.',
  '  3. Replace any PASSWORD_HERE / placeholder constants in the sketch.',
  '  4. Click Upload (right-arrow icon). For ESP32: mention BOOT button trick on first upload if needed.',
  '  5. Tools -> Serial Monitor at the right baud (check the Serial.begin() call in the code).',
  '  6. What the kid should see in the Serial Monitor + how to know it worked (e.g. an IP address, a sensor reading, "ready").',
  '',
  '(B) Pure analog projects (NE555 / op-amp / etc., code_language=bash, no MCU):',
  '  Lead-in: "Nothing to flash on this one - it is all chip + passives, runs the second power touches it."',
  '  Then a brief power-up procedure (where to feed +5V, what visible thing should happen) + a pointer to the multimeter probe steps already in the Code section if applicable.',
  '',
  '(C) Always finish with a "**Common gotchas:**" mini-list of 2-3 likely failure modes specific to THIS project',
  '  - Wrong WiFi band (ESP32 cannot see 5 GHz)',
  '  - BOOT button trick on the first ESP32 upload',
  '  - Cap polarity backwards',
  '  - LED in backwards (no damage, just no light)',
  '  - Serial Monitor baud rate wrong',
  '  - Library version mismatch',
].join('\n')

phase('Section')

const results = await parallel(PROJECTS.map(p => () => agent(
  'You are writing a "Flash it and run it" section for an existing electronics project. The section will be inserted into the project description right after "## Wiring notes" and before "## Talking points".\n\n' +
  'PROJECT id:' + p.id + '\n' +
  'NAME: ' + p.name + '\n' +
  'DIFFICULTY: ' + (p.difficulty || 'beginner') + '\n' +
  'POWER SUPPLY: ' + (p.power_supply || '') + '\n' +
  'CODE LANGUAGE: ' + (p.code_language || 'cpp') + '\n\n' +
  'ALLOCATED PARTS (to detect the MCU board):\n' +
  (p.allocations || []).map(a => `  - ${a.qty}x ${a.item_name}`).join('\n') + '\n\n' +
  'CODE (first 2500 chars - read for library #includes, Serial.begin() baud, WIFI_SSID, placeholders):\n' +
  (p.code || '').slice(0, 2500) + '\n\n' +
  'EXISTING DESCRIPTION (for context - do NOT repeat its content):\n' +
  (p.description || '').slice(0, 3000) + '\n\n' +
  STYLE_GUIDE + '\n\n' +
  'OUTPUT: Return JSON with id=' + p.id + ' and `section` containing the markdown starting with "## Flash it and run it" and ending with the Common gotchas list. The text will be inserted verbatim into the existing description.',
  { label: 'flash:' + p.name.slice(0, 30), phase: 'Section', schema: SCHEMA }
)))

return { results: results.filter(Boolean), count: results.filter(Boolean).length }
